      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>ALCOHOL AND LIBERTY.</head>
        <p>ALCOHOL AND LIBERTY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln reply to the letter of Mr. G. E. Ho'me I would remind him, that I do not imagine, neither have I said or written, that the pro- hibition of the liquor traffic will cleanse human, nature of all its vices. But does not the evidence of our judges, i.e., the late Lord Chief Justice of England- (London, 1910), the Lord Chief Justice ' of Ireland (Ulster Assizes-, 1911), the Lord Advo- cate of Scotland ,(1910), Justice Bailhacho (Lon- don, December 11th, 1913), and other evidence which could be produced tell us that the drink</p>
        <p>traffic is responsible for a large proportion of the crime of our land and the greatest hindrance to human progress. To speak of personal liberty in connection with such a gigantic evil is cer- tainly to make a "rut" or stumbling-place of personal liberty. I have evidently not under- stood the history of our people in the same way as our friend, and I certainly cannot submit to his idea that we have reached our present stage by refusing even to interfere with the individual as he says. Education for the children was forced upon many unwilling parents. Factories and workshops are closed at certain hours by compulsion under the Factory Acts. The Shops Act prohibits the sale of certain articles one / half-day a week (with exceptions under certain conditions). Mistresses shrieked that they would' not pay their servants' insurance, but they do, because it is now the law of the land. Even here, as in the State of Maine, laws are broken and convictions are recorded, but if we get the trade of alcohol under local veto or control, then I think some of our friends , will be surprised at the reduction which will take place not only in drunkenness, but .also other crimes, as the returns show us very plainly which reach us from the places which have tlic, privilege of saying "Better our land both free and sober."</p>
        <p>BENJAMIN T. PARKES.</p>
        <p>8, Willis-street, January 28th, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>INGREASIN G RENTS.</head>
        <p>INGREASIN G RENTS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Smith's reiteration becomes some- what tiresome. I suppose it is intended to serve ' a purpose, but I must confess I fail to see what it can be.</p>
        <p>But I would point out that whilst a larga share of his last letter is made up of mere repeti- tion, he utterly fails to meet the issue raised .in the questions I last addressed to him. Will you therefore permit me to re-state the position? He has argued thata landlord is now entitled to charge. 6s. 4d. a we.olc._ rent on a six-roomed cot- tage previously rented at 55., becauseo lie has to pay .13 10s. 10d, in rates to the Corporation. Knowing this to be a gross perversion of the facts, I asked him to state the corroot and suggested that 6s. 4d. Aould be an oyerch4r6 _</p>
        <p>of Bd. 'a -week. He attempts to evade the lint by writing about ss. 3d. and ss. 9d. rents, and qualifies his statements with an unctuous " so far as I know," the remarkable thing being that hitherto, he has apparently known everything. It should be known, at any rate, that the ratef, on a house such as I have referred to only amount to 062 9s. 3c1., and if Mr. Smith likes to dispute it, let him issue another challenge to a consultation with an accountant. As to his pathetic story of the mortgagees and builders, I can only say that it does not fit the ease. I will endeavour to make my meaning clear by putting the matter in another way. The houses referred to by me have, I think, been previonsly rented at ss. 3d. By increasing the rents to ss. 4d. it is obvious a further penny a week is obtained, but it is not so obvious to the average individual that an in- crease of 65. a week under a re- assessment, would only mean an extra 11d, a week to the landlord, and yet such is the con- sideration the latter has for his tenants that he prefers to charge the latter rent to exact the extra halfpenny. It might, of course, occur to some people that the extra halfpenny coact be met in other ways, to the greater satisfaction of those concerned, excepting, perhaps, the Cor- poration, but apparently it has not occurred to the landlord builders I have in mind. Then I have another item of information which. may interest Mr. Smith. I understand a sort of rent "auction" is being tried in the town. I do not say it has been generally adopted, but applicants for a certain house have been told that the one offering .the most rent would be given the tenancy. Now, I believe this is an- innovation, and looking at the matter dispas- sionately, it seems to have great possibilities, especially whilst cottages are so scarce, and ,is a scheme for rent raising which might be .relied MI to extract the uttermost farthing. It would be interesting, as an abstract question, of course, to have Mr. Smith's views in regard to the matter. „ • . As to the People's Budget, it has had to stand a great deal of political obloquy, and will not doubt have to stand a lot more, but its provisions have been, and still can be, amply justified. Other causes of a more potent character have been responsible for the stagnation in the build- ing trade, one of which has been the eagerness of the moneyed public to take advantage of the great trade boom. Now that it is passing, the builders are coming into their own again, judg- ing from reports from various parts of the country, and with the scarcity of houses obtain- ing at present, and the enhanced values due to increased rents, to say nothing of the creation of ground rents, one can predict a high time for • some of them, at any rate, in the near future.' Conversely the manufacturers may fall upon lean times, and a consequent reduction even on a normal output, and I am wondering if then we shall have Mr. Smith advocating reduced rates on their behalf._ h would. certainly be a somewhat unique experience, but he could plead ' a desire to be consistent as his reason. In regard to these manufacturers, he has not produced a shred of evidence to prove his state- ments, and in view of this, and 'of the fact that he has failed to support his plea for a 68. 4d. rent. I feel there has been some justification for my , letters. Before concluding my correspondence on this subject, I would refer Mr. Smith to Monday's daily papers. He will find that a Tenants' Defence League has been formed in Leeds, and that drastic action has been proposed on the tenants' behalf. I merely mention the matter without comment for his consideration. A. 11ORNBY RATLIBONE. Latchford Without, January 29th, 1911</p>
        <p>- For Cakes, Pastry, Puddings 4 Pies. BOR - I I t S 7' 44; f I " 4 BAKING POWD R. . t i.. , . 4 i 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>FOOTBALL STRIKE.</head>
        <p>THE FOOTBALL STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Sir.—Will you kindly allow me a space in your valuable paper to make a few observations re- garding the above strike?aottf the eool Irlgeeeta commencement emot ishent eeydn. be t strikers hiorri ey amusing to a good many of your readers to tis how ow agp of oithati going ,3they7 the t, oseason aidsiplayoe 3n,Committee forto m demandinglme iread love o e aed. same player has received as much as et 9,00 out of the club, and I contend, Sir, he is not worth any more than the others. All this claptrap about Welshmen having " made " Warrington is a fallacy. Warrington - was a. premier club long before Welshmen were imported, and it is here where I blame the Com- mittee in allowing players to dictate to them. I. maintain that if a committee cannot command discipline in a team they should not exist. The Committee has made a big mistake, in my opinion, in reinstating these players, who must have been suffering from swelled heads. I wonder they did not demand all the gate money for beating Huddersfield. Players of this type only cause strife in a club. Frawley, Stunts and others would be in Warrington to-day only for the feeling among the players. Let me in conclusion say that all true lovers of sport will deeply resent the cOwardly and un- manly remarks towards the hard-working and energetic secretary, Mr. Bradshaw, who, in my opinion, has the club at heart. Warrington has always treated the players generously at all times; and they ought to realise that they must obey, not command.</p>
        <p>PLAY THE GAME.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>DANGER IN THE WEATHER Peps the Real. Safeguard Against Coughs,</head>
        <p>DANGER IN THE WEATHER Peps the Real. Safeguard Against Coughs,</p>
        <p>Colds and Bronchitis.</p>
        <p>Sudden changes in the weather soon find out the weak-chested and spell real danger for the bronchial sufferer. But there is one effective way of guarding against throat and chest troubles and preventing a revival of the old cough and bronchitis, even in the most treacher- ous weather. Take breathe-able Peps tablets. As a Peps tablet dissolves on the tongue it gives off valuable medicinal fumes and these fumes are breathed down the windpipe into every nook and corner of the lungs. In this way a unique strengthening and healing agent is actually passed over the tissues beyond the reach of ordinary physic. Every part of the breathing system—the parts affected by coughs, colds and bronchitis, &amp;c.—become strengthened and revitalised when bathed by these wonderful Peps fumes. Soreness in the 'chest and throat is soothed away, troublesome mucus and phlegm easily come up, and it is astonishing how free and easy the breathing becomes. Peps bring not merely, ease and comfort to the chest, but give real protection in " catch-cold " weather.</p>
        <p>is,rnore grateful than the relief which 'br g Adtchronic bronchial sufferqs, esp. agdd ; nothing is more effective in guarding -the children from chest weakness. ' Peps are entirely free from harmful drugs and are now established as the most reliable family remedy for coughs, colds, sore throat, bronchitis, asthma, influenza cough, catarrh, wheeziness, whooping cough and croup.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>COMMANDER EVANS,</head>
        <p>COMMANDER EVANS,</p>
        <p>South Polar Hero's Coming Visit to Warrington. A GREAT PERSONALITY. _</p>
        <p>Commander Evans. C. 8., R.N., who visits Parr Hall, Warrington, on Saturday evening, February 14th, to give his account of the Scott Expedition, has a most interesting personality. Although just over thirty years of age, he does not look more than about twenty-one or twenty- two. He has a vivacity of demeanour and feature which is of the greatest service to him in telling the famous story that has now gone round the world. Then the modesty of the nar- rative has a distinct charm. Little will be learned from the lecture about Commander Evans himself. He is too intent upon paying handsome tribute to the heroic efforts of his comrades to think of his own doings. The lecture is full of just the kind of informa- tion that enables every listener to understand the tremendous difficulties with which an ex- pedition has to contend and the sufferings and hardships that explorers must be prepared to face. Certainly no one who admires heroic deeds—done in the presence of apparently immi- nent death—should miss an .opportunity of 'hearing Commander Evans and seeing the mag- nificent slides and films. Mr. R. H. Pearson, 75, Bridge-street, War- rington, is now selling the tickets, aid a great ,udience is assured., „ Mr. Pearson and Mr. Johnson, of Kendal, are responsible for this important visit. Dr. Joseph, deputy-Mayor of Warrington, will preside.</p>
        <p>audie</p>
        <p>AN IDEAL LECTURER.</p>
        <p>"Old Journalist" writes :</p>
        <p>less, as is liver here on the 14th February;beu feoormomnean formallyd the excep- tion va to commendis sto de. the hleercetuiis'enowlniiecT hardly conceivable, Warrington be crowded by an audience eagep Polar Expedi- tion to the rule, Parr llill will on rthtaut occasion be first hand the story of the Scott o d Privilege, and can assure those whoi store for Expedi- tion. I happen already to have enjos3t-iell that it in anticipation that a treat is n. thena, officer, handsome-faced, keen-eyed, A broad-shouldered, w"and fluent ringing voice, and a gift of breezy. . Evans is Picturesque description, Commander upstanding,.tniavaal indeed an ideal lecturer. Of course, the lecture does not lack pathos, for the speaker is bound to refer to the death of Captain Scott and his four comrades; but his narration abounds in amusing touches—as I could easily show by us , some of his capital jbutnkesi. T t would d leebetnuren,famirnrteo. anticipate to the extent Of taking the edge off over, is illustrated by superb photographs and films by Ponting (who, by-the-bye, will himself Presently Start on tour with some wonderful slides). The ice pictures consisf gorgeous- . fantasies of Nature, proud, tower bergs, like Ithine castles, fluffy ploughed acres, shimmering flat expanses of frozen sea- under the cold moon, green glassy caverns and white cruel wastes blinding the eye even on the screen with their pitiless glare -r- all are things to wonder at. But against this eternal background of pure white stand out sharply the black figures of bearded men, muffled and padded and clumsily booted, feasting jbyously and plodding over the wastes as the miles dragged along, and them- selves and their sledges dwindled to vanishing point on the horizon. The films are more alive Still—real motion, real men tramping wearily and doggedly, and with smiles and tobacco making the most of the scant comfoit of the tiny tent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
