      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>EXAM</head>
        <p>EXAM</p>
        <p>ADULT SCHOOL WELCOME.</p>
        <p>Visit of an Organiser to Consolidate the</p>
        <p>Work In Warrington.</p>
        <p>A MERRY EVENING SPENT.</p>
        <p>A tea and social evening was held in the Academy-street School on Saturday, for the pur- pose of giving Mr. David P. Boyd, one of the National Union organising secretaries, Kho has come amongst us for a fortnignt's consolidating work, a real hearty welcome to Warrington. The Schoolroom proved none too large for the com- pany-. After tea, with the redoubtable Mr. John Ransome as chairman, we settled do l% n for a jolly evening.- No " kill-joys " in the Adult Schools ! We believe in enjoying life. And vie did on Saturday evening certainly have a jolly Lancashire time of it. 'there IV rl3 no lack of artistes to entertain; some, members of the vari- ous schools, and others not yet members. We had a surprise visit from Mr. Tem Youde, of Stockport, and Mr. George Ingall, of Pendleton, who came to join in the fun. Ambng-it the galaxy of those who entertained were the-Misses Isherwood, Parkes and Wright, and Messrs. Isherwood, Foster and Ashworth. Mrs. Catch] made a most delightful first public appearance as a ventriloquist, and the witty things said by the two dolls were heartily enjoyed by all. No Adult School sing-song would be complete with- out our own inimitable, irrepressible Mr. John , Smith, with his " Little-Jack-Horner-cum-I- diddle-diddle-the-eat-and-the -fiddle- cum - Mary - had-a-little-lamb " version of "Mary." His was the "star" turn of the evening. Then Mr. David Boyd received his welcome and made his speecl oW et liaedvocialerroifntgitioen Schools.ihapjiest socials in the history f h</p>
        <p>LONGFORD. son Dl ro.nlitiickie oomn eSnu deamy boeji).esn of df thee Bs eiibiloeo es- of The speaker dealt with the largei question of allowing women to take part in Church work, taking the lessons and preaching. He pointed out that without woman there would be no success. He gave some good illustrations of how a good woman could make a man a suc- cess in life by her help and sympathy. Alto- gether the lecturette was much. enjoyed and heartily applauded at the finish. It is gratify- ing to find that the menThers of this young school are gradually learning the art of self-expression, and are taking more part in the proceedings and "coming out of their shells,'" more every Sunday. When this school gets into its new premises there is every sign of another strong and vigorous force being added to the commun- ity. Stick it, Longford !,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>SANKEY BRIDGES MEN'S. _</head>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES MEN'S. _</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Lowe, ofP—enie—th, gave a leeturette on Sunday on a phase of the Drink Question, im- plied in the words "No man liveth to himself." The speaker said the wisdom of abstaining from the use of all alcoholic liquors may be seen, not only in the injury which is done to those who do drink them, but to those with whom the drink comes in contact. lie questioned the right of an individual to plea4e himself in the matte' for this very reason: If a man lived a solitary life the case would not he quite the same; in ,that ease his behaviour would not affect anyone but himself. No man has a right to do wrong</p>
        <p>to another in order to. please himself. The speaker also said that most of the vice, poverty, crime and insanity were directly or indirectly connected with the consumption of alcoholic drink. Not only the living, but those to come were affected. Further, the calls upon the purse of the nation in consequence was enormous, and this call came heaviest upon the sober section of the community. This was not right; those who were responsible ought to bear the burden. Until this curse a-as removed any real progress in the direction of a brighter personal and national life would be retarded'</p>
        <p>A Diversity of Opinion., , In the disc'ussion afterwards various opinion&amp; were expressed on the matter. One member said that in certain industries the work made heavy drinking unavoidable. Other drinks, such as, tea, coffee and cocoa had been tried, but none kept a man up as ale did. As might be expected this view did not meet with general acceptance. A,Arther m,e_mber -hid that alc*ol, bail an un, dotriVed posson, ought to eomei under '.he time lae", as other poison's. Another said that because doctors ordered bitter beer and porter as stimu- lants it must be right to drink them. If the doctors ordered castor oil for the same or othes reasons, it is not at all likely as a result that huge vested interests for the unlimited supply of castor oil would become a social feature of the community. There is a lot of humbug talked, another speaker said, about taking bees because the doctor ordered it, the truth was that folk took beer because they liked it, and for no other reason. The President, in summing up, said that The strongest case against alcoholic liquors was the economic one—the absolute waste they created in the communal and national life. They were a destroying, not a constructive force.</p>
        <p>, STOCKTON HEATH MEN'S. Mr. David P. Boyd paid a visit to the above school on Sunday morning last and gave an address on "India and Its People." The ad- dress was listened to very attentively by a large lengthy of et 600 present,detailed outline of class, there being upwards and the speaker gave a the methods of the natives of India, of the ways in which they live and work, and he also dealt with their religious and educational affairs. Mr. Boyd also took the Bible lesson, dealing with the metlMds and lives of INlartha and Mary at some length. Afterwards many of the members took part in the discussion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>YOUNG RECHABITES.</head>
        <p>THE YOUNG RECHABITES.</p>
        <p>The members of "John Dimond" Juvenile Tent of the Independent Order of Rechabites, along with their parents and friends, assembled in Friars' Green Schoolroom on Monday even- ing for the annint meeting and distribution of prizes. Mr. ent) and Bro. Gaskell, of Orrell. A 1) which was rendered in a very ported by Bro. William Lowe (tent superintend- ent) presided, being sup- effimenit•ogmraamnnmeer included the following items: Recitations, " Sign the pledge" (Vera Davies), "His first cigar" (Phyllis Lowe), "Sent home by God" (Elsie Hardman), Lancashire dialect recitation (May Elliott); songs, "Pass it on" (May Wood- land), "Asleep in the deep" (Harry S. Good- win); pianoforte solo (Phyllis Ingham), and Lancashire reading "Curing a woman tamer " (A. Wilcock). Bro. Gaskell presented the prizes for regular attendance to about 50 members. A special prize was awarded to Jessie Davies by Mr. H. W. Naylor for obtaining the most new members during the year. A Past Chief Ruler's sash was presented to Alfred Hewitt for having filled the various offices of the tent. A silver brooch was also presented to May Elliott for services rendered to the tent. A very pleasant meeting was brought to a close by the singing of the closing ode, each 'member receiving an orange on leaving the meeting. The annual distribution of prizes of the " Jonadab " juvenile tent (1830) of the Indepen- dent Order of Rechabites took place on Tues- day evening. Superintendent Bro. Dunkley occupied the chair, and gave an interesting ad- dress. The enterfainment included solos by Alice Bibby and Thomas Bibby; recitations by Alice Bibby and Elsa Hardman, and humorous song by J. H: Bibby. Councillor Hawthorn, who distributed the prizes, gave an impressive address and complimented the 70 prize-winners for their attending so regularly to obtain a prize. ire presented Sisters Phyllis Ingham and Elizabeth Taylor with Past Chief Rulers' sashes, for which they qualified by going through all the offices of the tent. Before clos- ing the meeting initiated a new member, Bro. Bate, and this was a little experience for the parents. On leaving each member and friend received an orange and apple, also a motto card for 1914.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening, in connection with the • 1.0. E., the annual prize distribution to the ' juveniles took place at the Wycliffe Schools, when Mrs. 'Simpson presented the book prizes to the children, who afterwards spent a very eay- able time in reading them. Songs and recita- ,tions were given by the children, and Brother Fellows presented to Mrs, Simpson an honorary member's emblem of the Order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>ER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 31, 1514.</head>
        <p>ER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 31, 1514.</p>
        <p>BOTELERIANS DINE.</p>
        <p>Urgent Need for the Erection of a New Grammar School.</p>
        <p>PRESENT BUILDING UNSATISFACTORY.</p>
        <p>Botelerians boasting of a one-time connection with the Warrimston Grammar School assembled on Saturday night amid the scenes of their youthful haunts to re-kindle memories and to participate in the twelfth annual dinner of the "Old Boys'" Union. Their love for the old school, where they received the first impetus to speed them through life's arduous tasks, was Manifest, and more than one speaker alluded to the need of a new building, erected amid fairer surroundings. Mr. Seg,ar Owen, president, took the chair, and amongst others present were the following: Messrs. E. R. Pickmere (Liverpool Town Clerk), Vere B. Davies, C. S. Burgess and E. Aubrey White, past presidents; Sir Gilbert Greenall, Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, Mr. W. Peter Rylands, and Mr. R. C. Parr, governors; the Rev. H. Gray, head master; Messrs. F. C. Herzog, J. Young, F. S. Rossiter, J. W. Pearson, R. Sower- butts and F. M. Darby, and the Rev. E. Gun- ner, staff. The "old boys" included Messrs. J. Henry Smethurst, chairman of committee; Ed- ward Gordon Burgess, joint hon. secretary; D. J. Dunn, hon. treasurer; and L. S. Henshall, F. A. Milling, S. L. Partridge, W. H. Payton and J. H. Sankey, members of the committee, and the following: Messrs. A. N. Browne, J. Browne, P. 0. Bailey. W. Carson, C. R. Chambers, T. R. Critchley, Councillor Dr. E. J. Fox, Messrs. H. Fairclough, W. B. Forshaw, M. J. Mover, R. Isherwood, C. N. Joseph, H. T. Johnson, T. C. Locker, W. Moores, B. Markley, J. H. Mason, Travers Pickrnere, W. A. Proud, T. Proud, L. Pierpoint, G. H. Howson, W: B. Randall, F. H. Randall, F. 0. Stansfield, J. Sudiow, I. Sankey, G. 0. Venn, S. Wallhead,, C. S. Woods, H. F. Woods and W. Young.</p>
        <p>OLD HEADMASTER'S. WISHES. The HON. SECRETARY, Mr. E. G. Burgess, read a number of letters from members and friends, who were unable to be present, chief amongst which was a letter from the Rev. Offley H. Carey, who, writing from Trusham Rectory, Chudleigh, South Devon, said that it was now nearly 33 years since he saw the last of all of them, and more than fifty since he re-opened the school with twelve boys, after its two years' closure for re-building. He wished to be re- membered to all he had known and not quite forgotted after half a lifetime. He wished they would have a happy gathering; with pleasant memories of bygone days.</p>
        <p>THE FOUNDER'S MEMORY. Mr. .7. H. SMETHURST, who proposed the toast "The pious memory of Sir Thomas Boteler," prefaced his remarks by an allusion to the changes in the committee and the illness of Mr. Stanley Walton, one of the hon. secretaries, to whom he had sent a telegram expressing their deep regret and wishing him a speedy recovery. (Cheers.) The Union had a matter of .26175. sd. in hand, which, although, it appeared a small amount, was quite satisfactory. Then be had to call their attention to the Willcocks Memorial Fund, a fund which was of the greatest possible benefit. Of course, they could not always help old boys in the way they would like. One of their old boys was about to take some part in the governmen of the town. He referred to Dr. Fox, and it was to be hoped be would not be, afraid of the kicks and rebuffs he would un- doubtedly receive. He was glad.that the "fire that occurred at the ancestral home of -the Botelers the previous Sunday was not disastrous. Their founder un- doubtedly had the idea of doing good for the poor children, and one could only hope that the 'old foundation would not change more than was necessary, and they would all be satisfied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>"PROSPERITY TO THE SCHOOL."</head>
        <p>"PROSPERITY TO THE SCHOOL."</p>
        <p>- - - • --• The PRESIDENT proposed - "Prosperity to the School." He referred to the absence of Mr. Walton, saying that he was shocked to hear that his health had broken down, but he was glad to know there was no great fear. He was proud of the honour that had been conferred upon him. However, he admitted that when heajoined the committee he always hoped tliat sane day he w0441-,be honoured as the preliZeirrThr their . and ,address his old schoolfellows in a room' he entered for the first time 30 years ago that month. (Applause.) He recollected the uproar there was in the school when the Associa- tion code took the place of the Rugby on the football field. (Laughter.) The Rev. E. J. Willeneks was a great master, but a still greater friend. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>, It was a blow to him when the whole charac- ter of the governorship was altered. He thought that change was a pity, as to a certain extent it was working against the long life of the Union. He referred to the question of boarders. When he was at school there were 22 or 23 boarders, and it seemed to him that if a school was to be formed with real traditions, it must not only be local, but far-reaching. They must have boarders, especially as there wav'st probability of the school rearing its head in a more salubrious climate. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Boarders' Residence Wanted.</p>
        <p>The authorities should arrange for a house to be attached to the. school, so that boys from a long distance might be in residence, and that would enlarge the general outlook. In the case of dormitory life the school became part of a boy's self, and Mr. Willcoeks greatly felt the loss of the boarders. His wish was that the boys should be'trained for the life they had to lead. There had been a desire that boys should be trained more in business methods, but that was a great mistake. If they gave boys the full, open life of a school away from sordid influences, they would gather the necessary acumen to separate themselves from the Mere sordidness of the business life. It ivas Mr. 'Willcocks' wish to set up a very high ideal in the work of the boys as well as in their play. (Applause.) Ms whole recollection centred round Mr. Will- cocks, and he believed he was the first' president who was really a Willeocks boy. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>FOUNDED FOR POOR _BOYS..</head>
        <p>FOUNDED FOR POOR _BOYS..</p>
        <p>The liF;;;;;;;;,—repTying":ll7l;ehalf of the staff and himself, said that reference had been made to the good feeling in the school. There was no doubt that a good feeling did exist among_ the 120 boys. They were coming near to the 400th year of the inception of the school, and if one went Nick a shade farther, into the 14th cen- tury, they would find the desire of the founder of the oldest of their public schools, Winches- ter, was the education of the poorer boy. Un- doubtedly it was Sir Thomas Boteler's idea to help the poorer boys. It was desirable they should move from their present premises owing to the. industrial pro- gress round the school. If, as it was hoped, in the near future, before the year 2000 at any rate —(laughter)—they found themselves planted elsewhere, they would have adequate 'provision for boarders. They would have to walk first, and then they could run. The first thing to be done was to have the school, and then see what opportunities came their way.</p>
        <p>"THE GOVERNORS" TOASTED. Dr. Fox submitted this toast, and, referring to the rebuffs they said he would get in the Council, he replied that whatever kicks he re- ceived, they could not outweigh the kind and gratifying manner in which his election had been received. He was grateful for the educa- tion he had received at the school, as it had stood him in most valuable stead in every work he had undertaken.</p>
        <p>- The governors had been fortunate in the selec- tion of the Rev. Horace Gray as the head master, and the present condition of the school was due to the whole-hearted interest which Mr. Gray took in the school, which he put before everything else. (Hear, hear,and applause.) They trusted, as "old boys," that the school would not lose its original character, but would re- main the same. (Applause.) The Board of Education had hint6d at the unsatisfactory condition of the present school , sue. It was undesirable for boys to have to go , up School-brow to the school, and it was hoped, therefore, that the governors would soon be able to announce a new site, and that even a prouder and better school' would start on a still more brilliant Career: (Applause.) The toast was received with musical honours.</p>
        <p>UNSUITABLE SURROUNDINGS.</p>
        <p>Sir GILBERT GREENALL, in responding, said he thanked all present for the kind manner in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WORKHOUSE MASTER AND MATRON.</head>
        <p>WORKHOUSE MASTER AND MATRON.</p>
        <p>Former Candidates Selected.</p>
        <p>Two directly diverse courses were taken by the Warrington Board of Guardians at their meet- ing on Wednesday in regard to the appointment of officials. They decided to advertise for an imbecile attendant, and not to advertise for a Master and Matron, because some of the mem- bers knew of a couple whom they thought suit- able, and a motion is to be moved at the next meeting by Mr. G. Mather that Mr. and Mrs. W. Loxham, of Patricroft Workhouse, be ap- pointed Master and Matron, at a combined salary of ...t175 a year and emoluments, in place of Mr. and Mrs. Brown, appointed to the Dews- bury Workhouse. LABOUR EXCHANGE TABOOED. •</p>
        <p>The Clerk (Mr. A. Bottomley) reported. ,that an attendant in the imbecile section had been discharged for misconduct, and suggested that they should folio* the usual procedure of applying to the Labour Exchange for a man. Mr. Furness thought that was not the best course to adopt. There was a risk in getting an attendant through the Labour Exchange. They should advertise the vacancy and have a selec- tion.'</p>
        <p>The Clerk said there was no difference in the pay for such attendants all over the countrY When they advertised and got two or three men to attend they generally found that the applicants made it into a little outing, at the expense of the Guardians.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Simpson: Those chosen through Labour Exchange will have some attachment to the town. the</p>
        <p>Mr. Mather thought it was not wise to give the applicants what amounted to really a day out. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: The Guardians get outings and their expenses are allowed. • Mr. Mather The Guardians work for nothing. Finally, by 11 votes to 6, it was decided to advertise the vacancy. HEAD OFFICIALS RESIGN. -</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Mr. ;;;diUrs. Brown, Master and Matron, tendering their resignation, in view of their appointment to the Dewsbury' Workhouse. - _</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Mather moved acceptance, and this was agreed to. I am prepared to propose, or give notice of motion,. said Mr. Alather, that two of the appli• cants who came before us at the last election be asked to accept the position. A better Master and Matron than them I do not think we can find, and Mr. Lowry, the Government Inspector, recommends them. They are Mr. and Mrs. William Losham, of Patricroft. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: How do they come to know of the vacancy?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>CLERK EXPLAINS.</head>
        <p>THE CLERK EXPLAINS.</p>
        <p>- - -- --- The Clerk said on the last occasion the Board made an appointment Mr. and Mrs. Loxhain ran a close second. The House Committee, learning of the Dewsbury appointment for Mr and Mrs. Brown, had talked the matter over; and expressed the thought that Mr. and Mrs. Loxham would be prepared to accept the ap- pointment.</p>
        <p>_ To make sure upon that point, he wrote to them, and they replied that they were prepared to accept the appointment. Mr. Bent, the chairman: The question is whether this will satisfy the Board or not? Mr. Garnett said there might be other couples worthy of the position. He had never been in the habit of begging a servant to accept a Posi- tion. It•was a bad principle to go upon. If it was necessary to advertise for an imbecile at- tendant, it was equally as necessary to advertise for the head of the Institution. (Hear, hear.) TO TAKE THE FEELING OF THE MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: To take the feeling of the meeting, will someone move that the vacancy, be advertised? Mr. Furness could not see the slightest objec- tion to the proposal. It was the most business- like move they could adopt, not only in the matter of economy, but in securing a good Master and Matron. They were in a demo- cratic age, when the ,time of autocratic masters had gone I;lACqoaard. (Laughter.) Mrs. Simpson : It 'l.oulir be cruel to adver- tise and bring people here when we are going to give the appointment to Mr. and Mrs. Lox- ham.</p>
        <p>The Rev, A; M. Mitchell: I object to indivi- dual members taking' anY steps without consult- ing the Board: The House Committee had no right ----</p>
        <p>-Mr. Jolley: It was only mentioned at tho House Committee.</p>
        <p>no "TIED THEIR HANDS." occasion to tie the Mr. Garnett: You tied our hands. Ido not agree with that principle. The Clerk: There is Guardians' hands in any shape or form. The Rev, A. M. Mitchell: I move that the position be advertised, because I do not see any reason why we should depart from our usual custom.</p>
        <p>The motion did not find a seconder and fell to the ground.</p>
        <p>Mr. U. Ma-titer gave notice that at the next meeting he would propose that Mr. and Mrs Loxham be appointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
