      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>There is a sure cure at hand. Mrs. Clara E. Slater, Of Southporr, the Largest Beltmaker in England, is again visiting your town. She needs no introduction to the older residents, as her periodical visits have been looked forward to and appreciated for the last TWENTY years; but to the younger women Mrs. Slater would say that during her Career she has cured and relieved over 100,000 cases. Mrs. Slater strongly deprecates the use of internal instruments or operations., as she has proved from practical experience that the inevitable results from such methods are CANCERS, TUMOURS, AND OTHER OBNOXIOUS DISEASES. Mrs. Slater's appliances are known the world over, and can be worn with absolute safety. Note carefulfy the data and address of her visit, and consult her whilst there is yet time—delays are dangerous.</p>
        <p>eoNsuur ME ON WEDNESDAY NEXT. Ruptures, Women's Internal Weaknesses, Displacement, etc., Cured and RelieVed Without Operation's or Internal Implements. Our delicate organisations tender us liable to contract internal ailments which, if not attended to in- time, may cause us years of suffering. Most women. especially the younger ones, generally do their utmost tci keep the nature of their ailments to themselves, in many cases not even telling thei, own relatives—this no doubt from i • sense of false modesty. Any though of consulting a specialist is immedi ately stampeded. Why do you not ac- cept the inevitable and consult a lad, specialist—one of your own sex, wh; has had over twenty years' experiencL amongst cases similar to yours, and who has cured and relieved over 90,000 ! Whether it be internal weak- ness, displacement, rupture, or kindred complaints, I can cure you. Read these Local Testimonial,. WARRINGTON LETTER. LYMM TESTIMONIAL. "Dear Madam,—You will be pleased to -learn that already I have derived "Dear Madam, Your crusade much benefit from your Rupture Belt. against the use of internal instru- Although I have only worn it a fort- ments is, I should imagine, one of the night I feel like a_ new woman. If I greatest blessings bestowed on us poor make the same progress (and lam sure • women who have been deluded into again I will) I will be strong and well the use of same. On second thoughts, in a very few weeks."—Mrs. J. it appears obvious that such methods PRESTON BROOK EVIDENCE. - cannot do the patient any good; at "Dear Madam,—lt is With feelings least this was my experience. How- of the utmost gratitude that I pen the ever, after adopting your treatment following to tell you of the relief I for a ,fortnight only, I am already greatly improved, and well on the way have already had from wearing the appliance. It seems wonderful that to my former good health." such a change could have been effected in two weeks only. I feel nearly as strong as ever I was."—Mrs. E. G. A WIDNES LADY writes! "Dear Madam,—l can honestly say that your claim to cure internal weak- ness without operations has been well sustained in my case. I have tried several so-called cures with unsatis- factory results. However, your treat- ment, which causes no inconvenience, has already done me a great amount of good."</p>
        <p>FURTHER WARRINGTON PROOF. A lady to whom I had supplied a belt a fortnight previous came to see me to express her thanks for the way my appliance had cured her. She said that previous to wearing it she had not been able to do any work dur- ing the past five years, and yet, after wearing the belt only a fortnight, she could get about as well as ever. In fact, she walked about four miles to sot me.</p>
        <p>ALL ADVICE FREE. MRS. CLAIRA E. 67.4ArrEIR</p>
        <p>Visits WARRINGTON on WEDNESDAY NEXT (February 4th). ' DRUIDS' 'HALL. Hours 2 to 5.</p>
        <p>Write for New Illustrated Booklet, "Why Internal Instruments should not be worn," to Mrs. CLARA E. SLATER, Belgrano, Finsbury Park, London, N.</p>
        <p>BOTELERIANS DlNE—continued.</p>
        <p>found that an assault had been committed, but on defendant agreeing to apologise and pay the costs, the ease was dismissed.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>TT COPPER. Bonsat.—Frederick Moor, brick- setter,. 12, Ship-yard, was charged with, on January 20th, stealing from a yard off Knuts-4 ford-road, a copper boiler, value 285., the pro' party of Mr. John Lunt, contractor. Williay Bolton, carter, 68, Back Dallam-lane, wa charged with receiving part of the proceeds. Moor was sent to prison for one month, and Dol. ton was fined 205., or 14 days.</p>
        <p>A telephone linesman at Haslingden on Wednesday found suspended from a telephone pole the body of Squire Holt (50), weaver, a married man with a family. A verdict of "Death from food poisoning" was returned at an inquest on Nellie Allerton (2), whose parents reside in Bath-street, Hulme. Melichester.</p>
        <p>------ FRIDAY. SOLD COAL TICKETS.—Mary Ann Libbeter and Thomas Libbeter, husband and wife; were charged with stealing household articles from furnished lodgings, 3, Stanley-street, the pro- perty of Mrs. McCabe, lodging-house keeper.—lt was stated that prisoners pawned several articles of bedding, AT., and had neglected their three children. Mr. Law and Inspector Wain- wright had done their best for the prisoners, and coal tickets which had been given to them had been sold for 2d. each.—Both prisoners were sent to gaol for a month each for stealing, and for the neglect the female prisoner was committed to gaol for two months and the male prisoner for one month.</p>
