      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>To Do All They Can.</head>
        <p>To Do All They Can.</p>
        <p>It was imperative that the governors should not lose sight of its character and individuality. While it was most desirable that the social standing of the school should be maintained, there was nothing to prevent boys in more humble stations in life from attending. The wish of the governors was that the education should be open to anyone who was fit to receive it. They all knew the Duke a Wellington's saying that the battle of Waterloo was won on the playing fields of Eton. It was, however, not merely the games, but also the esprit de corps, which was so marked a characteristic of the public schools, which had helped to make Eng- land what she was. De could' assure the "old boys" that as chairman of the governors he should do all he could for the school. (Ap_ plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>"THE PRESIDENT."</head>
        <p>"THE PRESIDENT."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. HERBERT MASON proposed the toast "The President." Mr. OWEN replied in an ap- propriate manner. The proceedings were 'punctuated by songs, &amp;0., given by the Rev. H. Gray, and Messrs. P. 0. Bailey, W. Moores, T. Critchley' and-W. B. Forsbaw. Mr. W. H. Payton accompanied, and also gave a pianoforte duet in conjunction with Mr. F. M. Darby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. R. T. Fairclough (in the chair), Alderman Tinnion and Mr. T. Pemberton. "A VERY BAD BEST.”—Mary Langley, of 6, Jolley's-yard, Barbauld-street, was charged with stealing coal, valued 9d., from the Arpley New Railway Sidings.—Prisoner, when arrested by a railway detective, said that other women did it, and she thought she could.—Alderman Tinniot spoke strongly upon the attitude of the railwa. company in regard to this case, and in reply 62, the detective's statement that the cornpany did their best to stop the business., the Alderman retorted "It is a very bad bost."—The *Oman was sentenced to one day's imprisonment.</p>
        <p>/11</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor Roberts (in the chair), and Messrs. J. Hepherd and C. Jackson Holmes. SCENE AT A PUBLIC-HOUSE.—Mary Fisher, 10, Dolman's-lane, was charged with being drunk and disorderly, assaulting P.C. Gibbs, and re- fusing to quit the Crown and Sceptre Hotel. On Monday afternoon prisoner went into the hotel in an intoxicated condition, and was ejected by the licensee, at whom she threw a bottle. The police officer was called, and on the way to the station she kicked him several times. —She was sent to prison for three months. WHEN WOMEN FALL OUT.—A widow named Sarah Hough, of 183, Thelwall-lane, summoned FranCeA Groom, a married woman, of 214, Knutsford-road, for assault. Mr. Steel appeared for the complainant, and Mr. Swallow defended. —Complainant's story was 'that as she was pass- ing out of the Royal Court Hotel the defendant made use of an objectionable remark against her. Complainant waited outside the hotel for Mrs. Groom, and informed her that if she con- tinued using such language she would "give her in charge." Defendant thereupon struck her several blows in the chest and swung her round. —This was denied, it being stated that Mrs. Groom only waved her hand in telling complain- ant to mind her own business.—The Bench</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
