      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>AGE</head>
        <p>AGE</p>
        <p>Stone (exact size) disff charged by Doan' i Pills.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. SMITH, 181, Sydenham Road, North, West Croyden, Surrey, says : "But for Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, I should have undergone a dangerous operation.</p>
        <p>"I was almost mad with pain. There was barely any urinary action, and it was agonising to attempt relief.</p>
        <p>"The enormou, wiL ~ was eventually brought away by Doan's Pills. In the interests of others I give my consent to publication of these facts, and an illustration of the stone." (Signed) "C. SMITH."</p>
        <p>Stone is most frequent in the earlier and later periods of life, though the complaint is becoming prevalent at all ages. Uric acid crystals form, as a rule, the basis, and stones vary in size between a small berLy and a walnut.</p>
        <p>Symptoms are :—Local irritation, heat, dyspepsia, frequent painful urination, stoppage, pain wheu jolted, and blood.</p>
        <p>Sickness, shivering and intense pain in the groin mark the creeping of a stone from the kidneys to the bladder.</p>
        <p>Doan's Pills promote activity in the kidneys and bladder, which they soothe and strengthen, so that uric acid deposits are dislodged and driven frcm the system.</p>
