      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>.. .• , • . ' ..... -, . , . .. , , .."-[-. ~ , I I . . .. . . . _ ,</head>
        <p>.. .• , • . ' ..... -, . , . .. , , .."-[-. ~ , I I . . .. . . . _ ,</p>
        <p>Backache Kidney Pills</p>
        <p>CHICKEN FOOD.</p>
        <p>Should Preference be Given to Mash or Dry Meals ? THE POULTRY HOUSE.</p>
        <p>The relative merits of soft and dry food for chickens have often been discussed. Personally I am quite satisfied, says a "Farm and Home" authority, to compromise between the two, but during the winter months it is not advisable to give very much soft food. The best plan is to make a custard with milk and eggs, for this is nourishing and will not cause scouring. If given twice or three times a day the birds will not need to be given cold water to drink until they are some weeks old, and this is the best way to avoid bowel trouble. For the rest, give a good, clean mixture of small grains, and don't be afraid to spread a little among some litter, for even chickens benefit by scratching exercise in cold weather.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>POULTRY HOUSE.</head>
        <p>THE POULTRY HOUSE.</p>
        <p>. - - Many poultry houses fail in that they are made of boarding which is too thin, with the in- evitable result that they are too cold during the winter, and too hot during the summer. In a permanent house the boarding should be an inch in thickness, while in st, portable shed rather thinner timber may be employed, since one of the requisite features of such a house is that it is light and easily movable from'one place to another. If the present poultry houses on the farm are made of thin boards they can be greatly improved by covering the outside with Sheets of thick brown • paper or unbleached calico, which acts as an effective preventive against draughts. The house should be tarred, and, while still wet, some sheets of brown paper or calico should be laid on, after which two more coats of tar, at an interval of about a day, should be applied. The paper or calico becomes practically a part of the wood, lasting as long as does the house itself.</p>
        <p>THE QUESTION OF VENTILATION. A good system of ventilation is a very essen- tial feature of the house, for it stands to reason that if they thelafr, eh iwbr odhuse nnacir oet no freteosuceffiledtre rtiosn bthrieleenalthhhoe uaysneidt i afip trresod t. thing in the morning, there is a stuffy, perhaps a disagreeable smell, then one may be quite cer- tain that the system of ventilation is at fault and the sooner it is put right the better. There are plenty of simple 'ways of providing the neces- sary amount of fresh air. A few holes pierced in each end of the house immediately below the eaves answer the purpose quite well, particu- -I:.rly if a sliding shutter is arranged which can close or partially close some of the holes during very cold or windy weather. Instead of the i several holes one large one may be used; n a house measuring 6ft. by Sft. the opening at each end should be about Bin. by 3in., which provides an abundant supply of fresh air.</p>
        <p>WHITEWASHING. The interior of the poultry-house should be; whitewashed at least three times a year, and the, present is an excellent time for doing it, as a little later in the season it takes so long to be- come thoroughly dry. A good preparation con- sists of lime dissolved in four gallons of water, with the addition of half a pound of soft soap and one pint of paraffin oil. The oil kills all insect life, and the soap causes the mixture to adhere to the walls. The ends of the perches' should be dipped into paraffin oil, and the sockets into which they fit should be washed out with the same liquid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>ONTARIO'S VIGOROUS AGRICULTURAL</head>
        <p>ONTARIO'S VIGOROUS AGRICULTURAL</p>
        <p>POLICY.</p>
        <p>The rapid growth of Ontario's cities and the increase in her industrial population have taxed the counties to their limit for the production of a food supply. The farmer has found that his output was far from sufficient and through lack of skilled help it was grwing less. Action on the part of the Government to relieve this situa- tion continues in aggressiveness: The efforts of the Department of Agriculture to help the farmer are carried on through many channels Through his Provincial Government the farmer has a hand on his shoulder continually. Since 1907 the District Representative has been abroad in the land, and wherever he has appeared the farmers' acres have doubled and trebled in pro- duction.</p>
      </div>
