      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>PROPHECY FULFILLED.</head>
        <p>A PROPHECY FULFILLED.</p>
        <p>- - - - - As far back as last September, experts de- clared the present Currant crop to be the finest received within living memory. Their opinion has turned out literally correct. It is rich in duality, and exceptionally savoury. Added to those desirable attributes there is another ele- Pent which must unfailingly bring joy to the Heart of the housewife. The price of Currants ja decidedly moderate—nay, as compared with the wonderfully high food yalue of the fruit. CUrrants are remarkably cheap. It is inapos- eible to get better value for money in the way of foodstuffs than by buying Currants. If, at all times Currants benefit both health and pOoket, then the advantages of the fruit should be emphasised wholeheartedly at the present time. The existing stock of Currants are not only beautiful, but inherently perfect. They have a richness of flavour that is indescribable. Try them this way to-day.</p>
        <p>CURRANT MARMALADE VIIDDING. 4 oze. Currants, 4 ozs. breadcrumbe, 4 ozs. ,11et, 4 ozs. sugar, 4 ozs. marmalade. 2 ozs. flour, • eggB, teacupful milk. Method: Mix all the Ingredients together; grease a pudding basin or fli°llld with butter, and dust with sugar. Put I,eaml the mixture, cover with greased paper, and steam a hours.</p>
        <p>"RICULTURAL BENEVOLENT INSTITU- TION. 211 e following is a list of the local harvest and ;401" church collections which have been for- / 41.(leci to the Royal Agricultural Benevolent 31P3titUtion between. the Ist January and the b_r3t December, 1913 : str,°. L. J. T. Darwall, Walton, Warrington, to,: o. 3d.; Rev. C. G. Greenham, Halton, Run- -43 ss. Bd.; Rev. U. L. Johnstone, Aston, 'eston Brook, £2 2s.</p>
