      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>EXAMINER, .SATURDAY,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, .SATURDAY,</p>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>MR. RUNCIMAN'S OFFER. Tuberculosis Order to be Amended.</p>
        <p>The Marquis of Crewe, as president of the Cheshire Milk Producers' Association, occupied the chair on Monday at an agricultural con- ference at Crewe. Mr. Walter Runciman, President of the Board of Agriculture, attended and listened with interest to a long discussion trn the subjects of tuberculosis in cattle and the treatment of milk and cheese under the Food and Drugs Act and the increased railway rates for milk.</p>
        <p>Mr. Runciman said there were eight cases pending before the Railway and Canal Commis- sion on the raising of railway rates, and until those cases were decided it would be impossible to say with any certainty ,exactly how far the railway companies had exceeded their legal powers. But on the general equity of the rais- ing of the rates he believed he was speaking the minds, not only of those who sent milk on their lines, but of the general public? when he said that milk producers ought not even within the spirit of the Act to be compelled to pay more on the carriage of their milk than the railway company could prove was due to the increase on the labour charge in respect of the carriage of that milk. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>- Turning to the question of the quality of milk, he said he was afraid they must have some standard. His Board were in constant communication with the local authorities, try- ing to induce them to use the Food and Drugs Act and the regulations as a means of prevent- ing the sale of adulterated milk, and not to attempt to use them as a means of preventing the sale of what was called pure milk. The Government had made a 'very tentative 'start • with the tuberculosis order in respect of cattle. Experibnce of the working of that order had shown that the two valuations provided in respect to compensation for slaughtered cattle did not work well, and amendments would be introduced.</p>
        <p>Mr. Runciman indicated that delay in inspec- tion and valuation would alsos,he dealt with in an amending order, and hinted that provision might be made for &amp;cow to be killed after 72 hours and compensation paid. He also hoped that they would be able to deal with the complicated matter of administration expenses inssuch a way that the burden thrown on the local authorities might be reduced. Two objects would be to reduce the burden on the rates and reduce the loss to the farmer.</p>
        <p>Prior to the conference, the fifteenth annual meeting of the association was held under the chairmanship of Mr. Henry J. Tollemache. The report stated that the membership of the association totalled 2,098. In regard to the Tuberculosis Order, 1913, the Council remarked that they had been carefully watching the operations of the Order, and had come to the conclusion that the fears entertained had been fully justified by experience, as many members of the association had been called upon to make heavy sacrifices in the public interest, the com- pensation being entirely inadequate, while the administrative expenses, which were a charge on the county rates, to which the farmers were large contributors, would prove to be very heavy indeed.</p>
        <p>The report and accounts were adopted. Lord Crewe was re-elected president of the association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>THE BEST•VIOLAS.</p>
        <p>The Royal Horticultural Society each year carry out tests of various plants in Surrey, and the information is valuable for those not well acquainted with the names and colours of the plants. Three hundred and sixty-eight differ- ent varieties were grown, and the following re- ceived recognition : Admiral of the Blues—a vigorous grower of free-flowering habit with large violet-purple ray- less flowers. Agnes Kay, with large flowers, having the lower and side petals white edged with pale violet blue, while the upper petal is almost wholly violet blue. Alexandra—Flowers large sulphury white, rays blue, margins pret- tily crinkled. Bethea—A fine bedder with sul- phury white-rayed blooms of good form. Blue Duchess—A good bedder„ flower large, pale violet and rayed. Bridul Morn—One of the best blues. Fred Williams—With good-shaped flowers, pale violet purple, with prettily crin- kled margins. Purity—With large rayless white flowers, valuable for bedding out. Redbraes White—Pale greenish white, very free flower- ing. Redbraes Yellow—With flower deep golden yellow and rayless. Royal Scot—Flower deep violet purple. Sulphurea—Pale sulphur yellow deepening in the lower petal to primrose yellow, rayless, long and free blooming. Virgin White —Pale sulphur, white, and rayless flowers. The following violas were commended for bay- ing stood well through two seasons :—White-- Dawn, James Pilling, Kate Blyth, Palmer White, Pencaitland, Snowflake, Virgin White. Yellow and Golden—Bullion, Crievi, Kingcup, Molly Pope, Primrose, Walter Welsh. Purple and Violet—Arabella, Archie Grant, Cornuta, Countess of Kintore, Ivanhoe, Kitty Bell, Maggie Mott, Max K--lb. Rotherfield Belle, Wil- liam Robb. Violet Blue and White—Mrs. Chichester.</p>
        <p>PREPARING FOR THE PEA CROP. The autumn -sowing of peas is not now often resorted to, and it is very doubtful whether any advantage resulted from doing so, as, owing to slugs, mice, and other pests, it was impossible to get an even plant, and patchy rows are un- profitable and an eyesore. It is possible to have peas quite as early and with far less trouble by raising them under glass, and planting out as early in the spring as the state of the ground and the weather will allow. The seed may be sown now in small pots, on turves, or in boxes, placed in a frame to germinate. If planted out before they have filled the pots with roots, no check will be given, and they, as a rule, outstrip those that have been sown in the autumn and mature their crop earlier. Nothing is gained by sowing very early either on warm borders or in the open, the first week in February being soon enough. It is far better to wait for a tirae if the ground is not in good working order. Most of the early varieties are very dwarf, and there is no need to trouble about staking, with the exception of a few spray branches, which aot as a shelter in the event of bad weather. This refers to peas grown in the garden, as most of the early peas that come into our markets are grown in the open without any stakes. Of course, in the sheltered garden the haulm grows much taller, but it will be found that if peas are left unstaked they will not grow so tall as those that have been staked.</p>
        <p>HOW I RUBBED AWAY A STONE OF FAT FROM MY HIPS AND ABDOMEN IN TWO WEEKS TIME For years I tried every possible means to hide the excessive fat on my hips and abdomen, it seemed as if it had accumulated on just the two places that it would be most noticeable. Every- thing that I tried failed to make me any thin- ner. But thanks to a lady friend who had studied herboristerie, I learned the secret of a harmless plan which enabled me to rub away with ease a stone of useless fat in only two weeks. The plan is so simple and some of my friends seem so glad to know of it that I think there , may be sister readers of the " Warrington Examiner" who have perhaps been longing for this very advice. First, get from any good chemist 1 dram of quassia chips and 3 ounces of cirola bark extract. Take them home and after putting the quassia chips in a pan pour over it a teacupful of boiling water. When this has stood for about a minute, strain through a cloth and add the cirola bark extract. Then pour the mixture into a bottle and apply it every night and morning with your hands for about ten minutes, using a circular movement. Each time you can almost see some of the fat melt away. At first I thought it must be a dream, but when I measured there was no longer any doubt. I soon grew too slender for my clothes and had to have them taken in, but it , was a pleasure to do it, and I shall feel eternally grateful to the lady who showed me how, easy it was to regain my youthful figure. E.• L. A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>(Quail Ottus.</head>
        <p>(Quail Ottus.</p>
        <p>DR. MELLOR AND HIS RESIGNATION, Regret Expressed by Cairo-street Worshippers.</p>
        <p>There was confirmation on Monday night in the Cairo-street Schools that Unitarianism, in Warrington at any rate, is endowed with vitality, for there was a large and an interested gathering for the annual tea, and several speakers at the after meeting spoke in glowing terms of the valuable services of the Rev. Dr. S. Mellor, and expressed regret at his pending departure. _ _ _</p>
        <p>T. E. Knowles gave a report of the Sunday school, in which he stated that it was in a very flourishing condition. There was a decrease of twelve scholars against last year, hut of this number eleven had gone abroad. Dr. Mellor, who received a flattering recep- tion, said, that he was proud of the excellent assembly, More especially because it was but a normal reflection of the condition of the affairs at the Cairo-street Chapel at the present moment. Such a meeting was not an accidental thing. They had a very good and regular at- tendance at the evening service, and he was pleased to report that the Sunday morning at- tendance was increasing. (Applause.) If any of them thought they were attracted to tY the accidental personality of the pulpit, he asked them to dismiss it. tradition of the church which at- Even if there was no human voice in the church, the walls would cry out. at- tracted alrti aaetdsn chapelttee b them. He thanked the chapel and Sunday school offi- cials and the caretaker for the great assistance they had rendered during the year. Dr. Mellor concluded his remarks by an earnest reference to his resignation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>IMP OF DISCOURAGEMENT. Striking Address at Wseliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>IMP OF DISCOURAGEMENT. Striking Address at Wseliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>A striking address was given at the meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. society on Sunday after- noon by Mr. Robert Wood, who not only showed his speaker, but in a quartet, of wlslicahbillliety as a author and composer, he showed a considerable amount of poetic and musical ability. The Rev. T. T. James presided. The speaker said he came to offer a word of encouragement to those in deadly conflict with the imp of discouragement. When they looked around - they were grieved to find lives that promised well had gone all to pieces, t‘ud they were at a loss to account for such unexpected catastrophes. It was the result of discourage- ment.</p>
        <p>Was he addressing any whose thoughts—Were an uncertain quantity? If the tempter per- suaded them to give up the fight—banish that wrong thought. There was a way out. Put their heart and soul in tune with the Infinite, banish the impure thought of "I cannot do it," and repeat with all their strength of mind "I will." It could be done, and thus having put to flight the enemy, the evil thought, success would belong to them. B they demonstrated their charac- ter. They should think just as if the thoughts were visible, like all about them. High, health- ful and pure thinking could be encouraged and strengthened; its current could be turned by perseverance would gaa rt a fiini lad. si ti ii (11 eseleaeltmsh ruodnui ti figi il ei lii ttiht.,fe o buti mine•m To inaugurate pure and lofty thinking might at length render it easy, then pleasant and finally delightful. They must not allow stand- ing room for mental discord. The man who entertained it was a' person of uncertain quan- tity," and was, as was usually described by the man in the street,. a " wobble'. A delightful quartet We hate heard of a " land," the composition of the speaker, Mr. R. Wood, was admirably rendered by Mrs. War'. burton, Miss Plimpton, Mr. Poole and Mr. Warburton. They also excellently sang "0 Jesu, Saviour, King of Love." Mr. Cotton read the lesson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>BOLD STREET wE:LEYRN CHURCH. The Guild Festival Proves Successful.</head>
        <p>BOLD STREET wE:LEYRN CHURCH. The Guild Festival Proves Successful.</p>
        <p>Manning, of Burnley, who was some eight years ago. n g Christianitysery i services a Sunday was the occasion of the Wesley Guild festival, and special services were conducted by as preached, in which and personaleveni stationedtv he At yt B bpe ovi. at. • he Jti ointcshael velp has sermon morningir. mi n og iti o n in the individual life Mr. Manning showed hoY'aw. and purify national daily forni in mreuflecnatledli,feit.selßfanhoteinviilely and character, hut also throughout the com- dourse must be marked by personal chastity and et escinortmemmteone : individual commercial de jei 11. were sympathy. Domestic it eardependentcn ai. w ho af tHeaven. England a large coming of the kingdom friends gathered to hear Mr. Man- casaoondididoing every one was hastening forward the ning Guilders lecture I owes to On Monday evening company of Methodism." . _</p>
        <p>occupied the chair, and in his address the reverend -------- introducing the tPkheeaspeeaker, referred to the esteem wilnh woheicchoußrosled-sotfreet regarded M. Manning. gentleman contrasted the England of the eigh- teenth century with the country when Wesley 'ended his labours. The social and spiritual life of the people were alike in their exceeding wickedness. withln theirf act , thehist history "good old vice, days" were crowded depravity and laxity. 'lnto this chaos John Wesley was born, and it was against these tremendous odds that he com- menced to wage a glorious and successful war- fare. The spiritual revival which marked the commencement of his great missionary cam- paign served to quicken the religious conscience of the whole nation and to arouse even the Established Church from a condition of indiffer- ence. To religious pessimists thisrevelation of God's power against sin must come with reassuring • • grace. The spiritual outlook to-day is bright and full of the promise of great things. It is for Methodism to arouse itself and, prove worthy of its glorious Past bY striving for a more glorious future. The Rev. 11. Williamson thanked Mr. Man- /ling on behalf of the Guild. A feature of the festival was the musical pro- gramme, which was arranged by the choir- master, Mr. F. nielonaa• Old Methodist hymns and tunes were sung with true Method- ist fervour, and in addition two anthems were faithfully rendered by the choir. The soloists on Monday evening were Miss E. Taylor and Mr. T. McArthur, both of whom deserve special thanks and commendation.</p>
        <p>CHRISTIAN EMPIRE. .devoted his life to an ih*Ficaas, by Leo Frobenius, a is of very remarkable interest. elucidation of the historical . enigmas of the African equatorial, is something far ezmiTAhaNen VoiceAsaFvßanl AFRICAN Af wNAfh more than an account of the exploration expedition he led in horifstAlafnrica, Tl9hloe-rielis altdaring theory or speculation which is new that, for the space of nearly a thousand years, a Christian civilisation or culture hinterland of the West, where, as is well known, there are ethnological .and other evidences of civilisationsehie i in the existed in the heal: Phcenician and Pre' Leo Probenius now comes forward with the con- tention, substantiated to some extent with proof or what looks like proof, hat there was a Chris- tian .age in the equatorial, memories of which linger yet in the minds a some of the races. Instead of there being. no organised body politic, no real religion, no industrial development in the Soudan and the Niger region before the Islamic advance from the north, that invasion was, he claims, held back for a thousand years by "a great wave of ancient culture," whose place of derivation was Byzantium. There is a legend of a Persian warrior—the Kisra legend—who ruled the West under the overlordship of Nubia, and brought Christian- ity, since overlaid by Mohammedanism, into ' the kingdoms there. It was a dynasty which observed the Christian religion, had firmly established itself by the time of the Hedjira in the seventh century, and• maintained its • ground, more or less, until modern times. Such proof of this as can be gleaned from the Old Arabian writers is necessarily very obscure; their statements are governed by an obsession with the Islamic idea; the real evidence is to be found in race, in oral tradition, in religious ideas,- images, and art in general. - '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>JANUARY 31, 1914.</head>
        <p>JANUARY 31, 1914.</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>Masculine Attire Better and Safer than Feminine Attire.</p>
        <p>FOOD VALUES.</p>
        <p>Poultry 'farming on up-to-date lines, and at the high. prices ruling for eggs and produce, must be a money-making business. Under im- proved methods of rearing, the hen can be made a much more profitable bird than she used to be, and a record in egg-laying has been made in the United States by a hen which produced 291 eggs in 365 days—altogether nine times her own weight in eggs. The ordinary farmyard fowl thinks she has done her duty if she lays 52 eggs in the year, but by scientific poultry-rear- ing from the best egg-laying stock, it is ex- pected befOre long to arrive at the egg-day hen. Yet with all this progress, eggs are dearer than ever for the time of year, and prices are now quite out of proportion to their food value. It is said that people are wasting money in buy- ing eggs at 2d. or 21d. each and according to in- vestigations conducted by the State rood Com- missioners of the United States, it is possible to get twice the food value for the same money, if expended on bacon, butter,- cheese, or even butcher's meat. If housewives would take that to heart, it might induce them to go to the trouble of finding substitutes for the breakfast egg. Whilst people consider that article indis- pensable because of its greater convenience, the annual consumption of eggs is likely to go on increasing. It amounts in the United Kingdom to about 4,500,000,000 eggs every year, but only 1,700,000,000 'are produced at home, of which Ire- I land alone supplies about two-thirds.</p>
        <p>The Importance of Vegetables. That fresh-grown vegetables are good for the complexion is well known, and the "Lancet" this Week has a word or two to say, in favour of the medicinal action exercised by the onion, leek, and other 'products of the kitchen garden. Onions eaten at night are one of the old-time housewife's remedies, not only for sleeplessness, but for coughs and colds, and there are good grounds for this belief, as they produce per- cpiration, and assist gastric digestion. Spinach and turnip tops are well-known laxatives, and the former has a good deal of iron it it. The tomato is a stimulant of the liver, and the belief entertained by numbers of people, that it is conducive to cancer, has no support from medical science. Carrots, turnips and parsnips have all valuable medicinal properties. Carrots are especially recommended as an antidote to derangements of the liver and biliary com- plaints, and in this way they have a beneficial effect on the complexion. They are said to be better eaten raw if they can be taken this way, but the best principle is, of course, to avoid fads and freak cures, and attach greater importance to the inclusion of fruit and vegetables in our regular everyday -dietary.</p>
        <p>Dispensing with Skirts. The growing custom of dressing small girls in boy's clothes has several points in its favour. For one thing it is warmer in winter time, and although generation has succeeded generation in dressing little girls in socks and short skirts, it is quite a fallacy to suppose that bare knees and legs are healthy for children in cold weather. " Pantalettes," or tight-fining trousers and gaiters all in one, are not only warmer, but help to place small girls on terms of equality with their brothers in their games, and make them less liable to accidents. This is shown very strikingly in the fire statistics. Up to the age that it is customary to dress boys and girls alike, accidents from burning are about equally divided between them, but as they grow up into their teens, fire accidents are three or four times as numerous among girls. Masculine attire is not only safer and more con- venient for little girls, but it is also being frankly adopted in the day time by women visitors at the Swiss resorts. Feminine moun- tain climbers used to carry a detachable skirt, which they could resume for the sake of appear- ance on their return to the town or hotel, but there is a growing tendency to abandon the conventional garment altogether for winter sports and very cold weather. In Canada, where there is often forty or fifty degrees of frost,</p>
        <p>* £lOO,OOO worth of HOUSE FURNISHING Goods all marked at GENUINE REDUCTIONS. The Time to Furnish-February 2.t0 March 14. Illustrated Sale Catalogues post free. LONDON ROAD, LIVERPOOL. -1-14</p>
        <p>44 44 44 44 ril 14+14444414+4144414444144+++,11'11÷ 4014,1"1"1401"1"14,14 4414</p>
        <p>women have, at such times, to wear thick woollen " pantalettes," which they discard and ,put on when going in or out of doors, and they too are beginning to recognise the advantages of the masculine form of attire, and to advocate a -bold change that will afford them greater com- fort and convenience.</p>
        <p>The Tango Frock: The Tango is responsible—among other things —for a gown of quite a new type, capable of much variety, and combining the best points of the afternoon frock and the tea gown. It may be severely simple, and fashioned in some hand- some silken material, or it lends itself admir- ably to much elaboration of draperies and trim- ming. A favourite, material for the making of the Tango gown i 4 the soft taffetas, and often only one fabric is then employed. The skirt ,should be narrow at the feet, but fall on the hips. Pleated tulle fills the bpening'in the V-shaped bodice, and in the skirt when that is slit in front. The Tango dress, as is fitting, is made of materials in the most brilliant shades or in rich fabrics of subdued colouring. A modified medici collar of tulle makes a becom- ing finish to the neck. Tulle will find a place in all kinds of toilettes—dresses, blouses, vests and waistcoats being made of it. Trimmings for the Tango dress are as various as the styles in which it is built, and include the übiquitous fur ruches and pleatings of taffetas, tulle; or satin, or arti- ficial flowers.</p>
        <p>Preparationi for Guests not to Come.</p>
        <p>RAY .&amp; MILES ANNUAL CLEARANCE SALE.</p>
        <p>COL. SYKES AND THE ULSTER WOMEN.</p>
        <p>Sir Edward Carson has said when the "war" begins in Ulster the women and children are to remain there "to encourage their gallant hus- bands and fathers in the fight." Cheshire Unionists on Saturday decided to make prepara- tions to receive the guests, who, Carson says, are not to come.</p>
        <p>At the yearly meeting of Hie Cheshire Union- ist Association Col. Alan J. Sykes moved: "That in the event of this Government forcing the Home Rule Bill upon Ireland without the authority of the people of the United Kingdom and the loyalists of that country being com- pelled to resist by force of arms this attempt to exclude them from their rights under our Im- perial Parliament and to place them under the authority of a Nationalist Parliament, it is de- sirable that steps be taken to make provision for the women and children of those who are engaged in actively defending their liberties, and that for the furtherance of this object it is desirable to seek the co-operation and assistance of the Primrose League Habitations and the Women's Unionist Associations." The resolution was carried.</p>
