      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>Flounced Skirt.</head>
        <p>The Flounced Skirt.</p>
        <p>Much diverstty is apparent in skirts. Some of the big houses are sending out tailored suits and dresses of a very simple plain design, whilst others are as elaborate as it is possible to make them. The prophets are of. opinion that the two or three tier skirt' will supersede draperies as the season advances. There is no doubt that a determined effort will be made to win favour for the flounced skirt, in comparatively heavy materials, such as fine cloth, cashmere, silk and satin. But whether the efforts will be crowned with success, time alone will show. The flounces are quite plain, and cling to the figure in most eases, the- skirt being sometimes slightly draped round the feet, but not in a way to add to the width, and SO destroy the peg-top effect. Ruches with pinked-out edges, as worn in the sixties, are to be revived, and with them will be seen the chenille, so Much donned at that period. A dress of black and white tulle was trimmed heavily with jet, the tunic being edged with chenille.</p>
        <p>The Season's Colours. • One.can speak a little more confidently ofthe colours than of other distinguishing features of Spring Modes, which are still in the mak- ing. Colours have to be settled some months in advance, and these are to be soft and dull in comparison with the ultra-brilliant and vivid tints of last summer. There is, of course, a class of women who always insist upo- _1 _ flounced colours, whatever the time or season. Cone Town Council agreed upon a town plan- These will not find themselves unprovided for, fling area, and it was decided to obtain the con- but the leading- houses are pinning their faith sent of the Local Government Board to the pre- to subdued and non-assertive colour effects. paratioq of a preliminary scheme. Black and white will be even more popular than The question of the Sunday closing of shops last year, and greys, violets and attractive com- is again coming to the front in Bury and dis-. binations of blue and green will be much in trict as a result of the work of the local Free evidence. Yellow and "old gold" tones are Church Council, who are preparing a memorial on mentioned as likely to predominate, but it seems the subject, possible that the preference will be for browns, At the annual meeting of the Manchester terra-cotta and cedar, in all of which the leading Hospital Work Society it was reported that the houses are showing goods in a dozen Or more committee had been able to distribute over 3,000 shades, garments amongst the hospitals of the city.</p>
