      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EXAMINER</head>
        <p>EXAMINER</p>
        <p>"GENERAL</p>
        <p>ELECTION OR CIVIL</p>
        <p>VAR!"</p>
        <p>If the justification put forward for Ulster- men's threat of civil war is an odd one, the expedient which the Opposition urge as the sole means of • avoiding civil war is still more odd. Mr. BONAr. LAW has expressed the view, in a letter to a Tory candidate, that if the Govern- ment want to avoid civil war they must hold a referendum or a General Election on Home Rule. But does he ,know that, if a referendum were taken and the majority of votes were given in favour of Home Rule, the Ulstermen would submit? True, he has stated in the House of Commons that, if another General Election were to be taken on the Home Rule issue; and the result was favourable to Home Rule, he would advise the Ulster insurgents to submit. But Loan LANSDOWNE has explained that, given a favourable verdict on Home Rule, what would happen would be that the Lords would set to work to convert the Home Bule..Bill into Some- thing more •pitiatable to thernselves‘ aid their friellik:. —What more -natural, then, that when the Government refuse to accept the palatable repast,: the Opposition should declare that the mandate Of the 'General Election meant, not that the Home Rule Bill should be passed into law as it stood, bul that it should be improved by the house of Lords? On the theory which the Tories are working to death, it would be quite seasonable for them to demand yet another General Election, and countenance once more the threat of civil war. Of all the defences of rebellion,:lhe plea that the Government, which admittedly putting the case of Home Rule before the electors—LOßD LANSDOWNE -said Mr. ASQUITH had "made it clear" the Parliament Act would be used to pass a Home Rule Bill— had not put the issue in the shape and manner. in which their opponents think they ought to have put it, is the thinnest and most contempt- ible ever brought forward.</p>
        <p>WHO PAYS THE DUTY? Under the new American Tariff tinplates are adillitted into the United States at 15 per Mitt. As a result orders have Leen already booked for 40,000 tons of tinplate from Wales to be delivered in June. While these figures are rela- tively small cam Pared with the 800,000 tons Produced by the American mills, they have proved sufficient to compel the American'manu- facturers to reduce their prices by the amount of the duty. taken off. As a result, American users of tinplate will be able to purchase what is to them a raw material, at prices consider- ably lower than they have been paying hitherto. Commenting in its editorial columns on January sth, on the above facts, the "Toronto Weekly Sun says :</p>
        <p>Under the Canadian tariff tin-plates are admitted free of duty altogether, but general manufactures of tin can be imported, under the general tariff, only on payment of a duty of 25 per cent. Enamelled ware is charged 35 per cent, under the general tariff. The production of finished tin or enamelled ware in Canada is pretty well in the hands of a combine. We allow that combine to import its raw material free of duty, or under a nominal rate, and then allow it to charge consumers 25 or 35 per cent. more than fair value for the finished product. Thus we enable a few manufacturers of tin and enamelled ware to make undue profits at the expense of millions to whom these articles are matters of every-day necessity.</p>
        <p>It is strange that if the foreigner, as the Tariff Reformers insist so vehemently, pays the duty, the consumer should find that he, too, pays -an increment over and above the natural price, and one which corresponds so exactly with the amount charged to the duty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Preachers for To-morrow.</head>
        <p>Preachers for To-morrow.</p>
        <p>Legh-street Primitive Methodist—Morning and evening, Rev. A. J. Chessum. Thursday, Rev. J. Watkin, Birkenhead.- Bold-street Wesleyan—Morning, Rev. H. Mid- dlewood ; evening, Rev. E. Morgan.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan—Morning, Rev. H. Wil- liamson; evening, Rev. W. A. Beckett. Christadelphians—Evening, Mr. W. Lake, Stockport. Wycliffe Congregational, Pastor's First Anni- versary—Morning and evening, Rev. T. T. James.</p>
        <p>Cairo-street Chapel—Morning and evening, Mr. Herbert Barnes, Manchester. Golborne-street Baptist—Morning and even- ing, Rev. H. Ogden. St. john's Presbyterian—Morning and even- ing—Rev. W. Reid. Latchford Baptist—Morning and evening, Rev. . S. Chedburiu</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SATUR,DAY,</head>
        <p>SATUR,DAY,</p>
        <p>JANUARY 31,1914.</p>
        <p>THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>THE TRIP.</p>
        <p>As was to be expected, ourlittle account last ; week of the doings of the Conservative- " Liberal " deputation in Ireland, undermining as it did the effect the trip was intended to have, has roused Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., to wrath. Last night, at the Parr Hall, he attacked "The Examiner." in characteristic style; but we regret to say there was but a small audience to hear him. Alderman Bennett complimented "The Examiner" upon being fair-minded enough to reproduce the deputation's report—a , Compliment which may be left to describe our position, notwithstanding the Ntemb2r's cut- pourings. In another column we publish several replies to our last week's criticism of the excursion to Ireland to study ,lie Home Rule question on the skit, the most Unportant of the letters coming frolii members of the deputation. The whole of our correspondents miss the essential point in our remark's, which was to deny the right of the "Liberal" section of the deputation to be re- garded as qualified to speak in the name ,of the Liberal party. If their opinion Is accepted as an individual opinion, we are content, and our pro- test will have well served its purpose. Our correspondents seem aggrieved that no attempt was made to dispute the conclusions to which the deputation came. We have disputed those conclusions time without number, and no addi- tional weight can be given to statements because those who make them tranSport Theniselves from England to Ireland for a few hours. However, their conclusions are once again contested this week most vigorously in an article by an .Ulster Protestant, whose unail- swerable arguitieUtS' :we :trope' the • fleputedion may find soothing To .their.wounited feelings.</p>
        <p>HOW TO SELECT "LIBERALS." Mr. Bellian consents to the sending out of another deputation, in which both " parties " should be represented, and even agrees to a mjority of the deputation being Liberals, pro- viding they were not " official " Liberals. Why should he object to official Liberals? Three of the Conservatives in his deputation were official —including himself—in addition to the organis- ing secretary. We note that the prospective Unionist candidate for Hyde has been calling for two "young Liberal"' volunteers to go to Ire- land at his expense, and that on the Vice-Chair- man of the Young Liberals League offering his services he was politely told that the. "reserva- tions which I attached to the -invitation, to :inch you refer, were for the purpose of dis- qualifying gentlemen like yourself." We think that if people are to speak as Liberals, Liberals should have some little, voice in the selection. W. O seems a fair proposition, but—</p>
        <p>COMMANDER EVANS. Commander Evans,'who was second in com- mand of the Scott Antarctic Expedition, and has a first-hand story to tell of one of the rrio7l thrilling and glorious experiences _through which man has ever been called upon to Pass, is paying Warrington an outstanding honour by visiting the ram on Saturday, Ferruary 14. He is coming specially from perlin to Warring- ton, and, as over 100 towns have been refused dates for a lecture, we may consider ourselves to be highly favoured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>SUNDAY CARS AND SUNDAY REST.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY CARS AND SUNDAY REST.</p>
        <p>The Tramways Committee have been further Considering how best to:reconcile the conflicting interests of the tram drivers and conduc- tors, and of the public who use the cars on Sundays. They propose to run up to two o'clock, on Sundays, a half-hourly service on the,Latchford, WilderspoOl and Sankey routes; twenty minutes' Iserviee On the Cemetery route; and a fifteen minutes' Service on the Longford route. After two the ordinary ser- vices will be resumed. By this arrangement drivers and conductors can be free from duty every fourth Sunday. Whether" this arrange- ment will be satisfactory' all round remains to be seen. A car with half-hourly intervals does not appear to be very convenient for church- goers "on the last minute," and there remains the question of the men's pay, upon which no information is to hand.</p>
        <p>La</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS IN THE STREETS. The Chief Constablellaving, as requested by - the Town Council, considered the question raised by the Warrington Citizens' Temperance. , League, has reported to the Watch Committee that, in his opinion, "there has been a marked improvement in the condition and habits of the young people and their conduct in the streets, and that Warrington compares very favourably with any other town in Lancashire." We are very pleased to have such high authority for this opinion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>SHALL WE SPEI‘NIDILLTIOHNAT? QUARTER- OF A</head>
        <p>SHALL WE SPEI‘NIDILLTIOHNAT? QUARTER- OF A</p>
        <p>-•----- The Local Government Board have addressed a letter to the Town Council calling attention to the reference in the : report of the Medical 1 Officer of Health for last year to the desirability of introducing the water-carriage system in place of the existing pail system, and requesting , Abe views of the Council on the matter. A sub- committee has been instructed to consider the I letter. In connection with this matter the Board also suggest that the question of re-sewer- -1 ing the town should be considered. It is clear; if .there•isto.jv a. that dia-nge of system along ' the lines indicated, a pec'esary preliminary would be the construction of sewers to receive the sewage. This, as we pointed out some time ago, would mean an expenditure of any sum up to £250,000.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR AS FORESTER. To-night the Mayor (Councillor Peter Peacock) is to be honoured by his Mother Forester Court —Court Warrington, 430. At the Blue Lion his brethren are to make him a present of an illu- minated address, while the Mayoress,.who will previous! have been initiated a member of Me female court, court " WinMarleigh," will be presented with a handsome satchel. Last Sat- urday his Worship officiated at a presentation to Bro. LI. Davies, an extended report of which appears in to-day's "Examiner."</p>
        <p>A FAMOUS LOCAL ELECTION. By his reference in his article in to-day's " Examiner " on "Warrington Worthies" to the famous 1868 election, Mr. Mounfleld will waken memories in the minds of many of our readers, who are hot yet past the autumn of life. Though the chief actors have passed away, there must be many who have stories to tell. If such will communicate with us we shallbe glad to print their reminiscences.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mountield possesses copies of boine of the to se'e leaflets or broadsides which other readers election pcetry of the period, and would be glad may have. He also lies a copy of a famous cartoon, which we hope to reproduce in our next issue.</p>
        <p>A YEAR'S POLICE WORK. : Very fulFand fuirlytsatisfactory are the figures which the Chief Constable gives in his seventh annual report relative to "crime in the borough" and other matters which come under the notice of the force. Of the more serious class of offence, classed as indictable, 252 were reported, a decrease of 52. A serious crime a week less ,is substantial progress. Unfortunately, nine more juveniles were charged with theft, and adults charged with minor offences, increased by 38. Drunkenness decreased by 18 cases. Moro than five children a week on the average, the year round, were found wandering in the streets and restored to their parents. Honesty is, as we have said previously, a greater virtue locally than the losers of articles belie': Dur- ing the year 402 articles were found and handed over to the police, but only 234 were restored to Ithe owners on application. The force, which numbers 87 of all ranks, costs £9,725 Os. Bd. a year, which is met-as follows :</p>
        <p>£ s. d. . 3,840 0 10</p>
        <p>Government contribution Sundry receipts Local rates Total</p>
        <p>807 16 11 5,077 2 11</p>
        <p>9,725 0 8</p>
        <p>THE SCIENCE OF RELIEVING THE NEEDY. A writer in the "Manchester Guardian" has some very pointed and useful things .to say about alms-giving, with special reference to the part a properly-managed Guild of Help can play in what is really one of the most important of Social concerns. lie draws attention to .the fact -that.clergyrrien. are directly Charged to See to the needs of their Parishioners, and to exhort to the end that those needs may be.met by .Other ' parishioners. But he deprecates the practice of the clergyman personally undertaking the dis- pensation of the money raised, especially when ' relief is made conditional on attendance at church. "Such miscalled charity curses those who give and those who take." The writer says':—</p>
        <p>I think it is Mr. Charles Booth who quotes the case of the decent working woman in a very poor district of London who, on being asked if she attended any place of worship or mothers' meeting, replied "Oh, no. We've been pretty bad off this lasi winter, but ...never as bado•as that." On the other hand, for the churches to do nothing in cases of real want would be fatal. And it would be a grievous waste of valuable material if all the garnered knowledge and experience of many religious workers were not used for any relief work. This is where the Guild of 'Help comes in. The office of this institution is a " clearing house of knowledge and experience," from which without actually handling the relief, the charity of all can be directed into proper and deserving channels upon scientific lines, which rule out most of the chances of fraud and wasteful over- lapping.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>WORK AT THE BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>WORK AT THE BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The Special Committee apPointed at the last meeting of the Council to report as to the rate of progress that is being maintained in the con- struction of Warrington Bridge have had several meetings. They proposed to call in an expert to advise them as to the alleged delay and the probable cost of the completion of the work. The question of the proposed appoint- ment was considered at the ordinary meeting of the General Purposes Committee on Monday, and it was decided to hold a special meeting of the General Purposes Committee on Thursday last to consider the matter. After a prolonged discussion at that meeting the proposal of the Special Committee to appoint an expert Was dc-. leated by a large majority.</p>
        <p>. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>PANTOMIME AT THE ROYAL COURT. " Cinderella " Creates a Record.</p>
        <p>Warrington has been well catered for recently in the way of pantomimic productions, and judg- ing by the large audiences at the Royal Court Theatre this week, to witness the gorgeous spec- tacular and highly diverting "Cinderella," pre- sented by Mr. Joe Redfern, they are by no means tiring of the frolic and fun which sur- rounds a pantomime,,' The story of ." Cinder- ella"- is as connected as possible and the stage effects Iht:tnighout are magnificent ' "Me "Fairy Coach, drawn by the four Shetlands, is a struc- ture of dazzling splendour: The latest senti- mental and humorous songs are rendered. Miss Dorothy Veitch is a captivating Prince, with her dash and vivacity. Miss Winnie Victoria as Cinderella sings remarkably well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>"Human Hearts" Next Week.</head>
        <p>"Human Hearts" Next Week.</p>
        <p>Hearts will be trumps in Warrington next week, for it is then that the great American drama, "Human Hearts," pays us a visit to the Court Theatre. "Human Hearts," described as a play "of heart and soul interest," has been enormously successful throughout the United States for the last twelve years, and on its recent production in London it scored a genuine triumph. It is not otten a play can boast of having conquered two continents; but ,"Human Hearts" has undoubtedly done so. Its great telling moments, and its natural appeal to *lovers of clean and wholesome melodrama, will ensure its success wherever played. The cbm- paily, which is a powerful one, is under the per- sonal direction of Messrs. Kenneth Rivington and George Howard, and includes that sterling actor, Mr. Felix Pitt, who will always be well remembered in the provinces for his fine per- formance of "The Silver King."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>ENTERPRISING "BAILIFF." Karno Fun at the Hippodrome.</head>
        <p>ENTERPRISING "BAILIFF." Karno Fun at the Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>Once again we have to compliment the Hippo- I drome management on the excellence of the vaudeville menu provided this week. A capital .bill is headed by one of Fred Karno's greatest of all laughter producing productions, "The Bailiff," and the funny antics Of Mr. Albert Bruno as the persistent Perkins creates roars of laughter. "The Heid o' the Hoose" is a wel- come.comedy sketch presented by Mrs. Oraeme Goring andrCo. An excellent skating and danc- ing turn is supplied by Sinclair's Thre. Dia- monds. "Babs" give a clever exhibition of toe dancing, and the other turns are good.</p>
        <p>Strong Varieties Next Week.</p>
        <p>-- - - The Musical Johnstons provide a turn which is of the best and their show is well worth seeing. The Spinette Quintet give a sensational dancing act. Den. Daring presents cleverly trained pigeons. Irene May, a star vocalist, is anothei great turn. The other members are clever, and all combine to make a strong and interesting programme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>THEATRE ROYAL.</head>
        <p>THEATRE ROYAL.</p>
        <p>Next week the attraction at this house Will he Fitzgerald's Juggling Girls, in their miginal speciality, "American Swells at Practioe.' On Saturday afternoon there will be a boxiag exhi- bition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>A very acceptable programme will be presented at the Star Kinema next week, when In- humanity" and ''Satan's Castle" will form the chief Attractions. The auxete-gramoplione will Also be heard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>PARK PICTURE PALACE.</head>
        <p>PARK PICTURE PALACE.</p>
        <p>A sensational wild animal picture will be shown at the Park Picture Palace during the early part of next week, and this will he super- seded by "When the Earth Trembles" fm Thursday, Friday and Saturday.</p>
        <p>NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>The programme at the New. Picture House next week will be full of interest, for on Mon- day, Tuesday and Wednesday a Portuguese revolutionary war picture, "Hatred," will be shown. During the latter part of the week "The Black Snake" will receive chief attention.</p>
        <p>PICTUREDROME.</p>
        <p>An excellent programme will be submitted at the Picturedrome next week, when the chief pictures will be "By the Cross" and "The Wastrel."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>LABOUR TOPICS.</head>
        <p>LABOUR TOPICS.</p>
        <p>UNORGANISED WARRINGTON WOMEN.</p>
        <p>BY "A WORKER."</p>
        <p>The Women Workers.</p>
        <p>The women workers of Warrington are very badly organised, and this is a matter which calls for immediate attention. Evidently some efforts are to be made in this direction. " The Labour Gazette," which now appears as a neatly got up trade union organ, makes the following inter- esting statement: "One of the most pressing needs of the moment is the organisation of our women workers. With the exception of the cotton operatives, the wives and daughters of the Warrington trade unionists remain un- organised, uncared for, and exploited. How much longer is it going to take to convince men that whether it be wife or daughter that neces- sity forces into the factory they must also be organised? Trade unionism is not confined to men; it is for women also. Women can be organised for a contribution of 2d. per week. We mention this simply to prove that liundreds of women are losing shillings per week because they are not paying their pefice. If the women neglect to protect themselves against the com- mercial forces of capital, employers are sure to take advantage of their weakness. Equal pay for equal work should be the motto of every women worker. Men have been forced to struggle for their position. Women cannot ex- pect it without an effort." Absurd Strike Breaking. _</p>
        <p>When a Corporation sets out to break a strike amongst its employes it entails serious cost to the ratepayers. This was proved at Leeds, and ,now Blackburn is proving it more clearly than ever. The gas works staffs were refused a paltry increase, so went on strike. They were being paid ss. 7d. per shift. - The Corporation have been•carrying on the -work with the aid of im- ported labour, and are paying these men from 12s. to .21 per shift, with free lodgings, free beer, ' free tobacco and free amusements. In a month the strike, or rather the employment of " knobsticks," has cost the ratepayers .210,000. About £5OO would have met the men's desire, yet 20 times that amount has been Spent because the Tory majority of the Council has resisted the men's request. The free beer has aroused a great protest, and public opinion is so with the strikers that the Corporation will have to give way, but not, perhaps, until about .225,000 of the ratepayers' money has been unnecessarily squandered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Successful Local Artistes In Miscellaneous Programme.</p>
        <p>Wednesday's miscellaneous concert by the Warringtori Musical Society drew only the cus- tomary very moderate attendance. It was, nevertheless, an exceeding interesting concert. Ind, one which,..therefore,ileserved a much more generous measure of public support. The chorus was c:iiefly concerned in presenting Coleridge- Taylor's "Kubla Khan," a work quite new to Warrington concert frequenters. While in an ' all-round sense the society's choral members (at preeent—unvialry small in numbers) "acquitted themselves in a highly creditable manner in this cantata, they by no means made all the points of attack, nor did they achieve all the niceties of expression, which suggest themselves on looking into the score. The composer's luscious and somewhat too lavish orchestration was, on the other hand, handled with quite sat- isfactory restraint by the society's instrumental- ists, win; were reinforced with the usual profes- sional help, and in many respects they were eminently successful, under Mr. Crossley's baton, in disclosing the exquisite richness of the music. Miss Nellie Anderton sang the part scored for the solo voice with an intensity of expression and a declamatory force which, while quite in keeping with the feeling of the work, did much towards uplifting it into a •plane of real seriousness and conviction. Judged music- ally, the work cannot, however, be regarded as an entirely satisfying attempt to bring home to one the delicious ana dream-like vagueness of Coleridge's poem. One listens to it with the feeling that, greatly though we may admire the rich colouring of the music and the piquancy of its rhythms, its melodies are of so common- place a kind that they never-really express the languor and mystical quality of the words.- A dainty and graceful part-song for female voices, "Beauteous morn" (German), a rousing Bacchanalian chorus (Elliott) for male"voices, and the repetition of "The Viking Song, heard at one of last season's concerts, completes the list of Choral works presented, all of them being perfermed in a manner which fully maintained the society's present high standard of singing. Of the performance of the orchestral items, and of the playing of NUBS Clarice Newell, the piano soloist, it must be said, unreservedly, that these were excellent. Miss Newell's rendering of the Schumann Concerto was marked by lin intelligent grasp of its meaning and purpose, and by a power of interpretation which only a most admirable technique and unusual tempera- mental qualities made possible. Nor was the Liszt-Busoni Polonaise less convincing in testi- fying to Miss Newell's splendid equipment as a pianoforte exponent, and one may be pardoned an expression of the hope to hear more of this gifted young lady's playing in the near future. "Finlandia " received an entirely adequate interpretation by th,-. orchestra, and, so far as could be tation by the orchestra, and, so far as could be seen, its peculiar mixture of ponderous harmony and elusive melody came in the nature of a surprise to many in the audience. The Tschaikowsky valse and the intermezzi from "The Jewels of the Madonna," while a little unsteady in places, were, on the whole, well played.</p>
        <p>try t</p>
        <p>Miss Anderton sang a number of songs artis- tically and in a voice of a rich yet flexible quality. The best of them was " Secrecy " (Wolf) and "Turn ye to me." To " Mandolin " (Debussy) and. "A Ballynure Ballad" we were unable to attach any meaning because of our inability to catch the words. And, the music of both these songs being too slight to have any separate interest, if one missed the story one missed everything. For an encore Miss Ander- ton sang " A song of thanksgiving" (Allitsen) in a manner as soulful as it was artistic. Mr, Vincent Needham played as a flute solo the fan- tasia caprice "Neapolitaine." It had little in. terest apart from being a display of Wonder- fully facile execution of florid writing in the form of variations on a well-known melody. But it greatly pleased the audience, who vOciferously encored it. - k</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>ADVERTISE</head>
        <p>ADVERTISE</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER</p>
        <p>IT WILL</p>
        <p>PAY YOU WELL.</p>
        <p>STRIKES AND POLITICS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes' Further Reply to "Labor</p>
        <p>Omnla Vincit."</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am pleased to learn that my future acti- vities are anticipated by such a large number of People, and beg them to wait patiently until I am prepared to divulge my plan of campaign, which is yet only in the process of construction., In fact, I have not yet decided on my destiny.: it may be Parliament, or, if a little more fortu- nate in the ballot, Purgatory. I have to answer "Labor Omnia Vincit" about strikes and politics. Of course, this is not a tall job, because we perfectly understand each other, and we are only writing to create a little interest until _something really important takes place. It's like this : When you have• to ,say a lot and also writ" good deal, you Wee- sionally get people at your audiences that have periodical, „nerve shocks, which arouse them from theirr- tibernations, and during '4.lllat momentary awakening they imagine that noth- ing has happened during their sleep. Old Rip Van Winkle was surprised to find his wife grown - grey and the old homestead overgrown' with ivy when he awoke from his 20 years' slumber on the mountain side. Poor old chap, he could not understand why things went on in his absence. Some travel with the spheres and mould their lives and circumstances according to the fluctu- ations, whilst others are always running after the shadow of the last movement. " The Examiner' is read by thousands of working men, who do not get the chance :of knowing what is going on upon their commit- tees, and I think its columns are a perfectly 'legitimate place for me to make my complaint, and Mr. Dukes to make an _ explana- tion." Sd says "Labor Omnia Vincit." _ _</p>
        <p>I asked last week that questions of a delicate nature should be asked through those bodies who were shouldered with the responsibilities; but "Labor Omnia Vincit" seems to think it my duty openly to explain what those committees consider private _ business. What impudence, from a man who writes under a nein-de-plume ! He is, I think, a member of a trade union. As such, he Can get all he asks for through this medium• if not, he has no right to it. But if, on the other hand, he is seeking to make public the private affairs of the movement for the benefit of its enemies, the sooner his identity is disclosed the better. Does he not think the employers know enough already, or is it that he seeks to divulge what their hired hacks have failed to discover? What are we to think of those who are sup- posed to be fighting in our camp flashing signals of our plans into the camp of our opponents? "Labor Omnia Vincit" says : "If Mr. Dukes gets to Parliament," &amp;c. Does he suppose Mr. Dukes is so anxious to get to Parliament? Does he think that I am so miserably ambitious as to sell my soul for a job? If I were the other people would only be too anxious to buy it. I am pleased "Labor Omnia Vincit" has stated the reason why I advocated political action, viz., to control the instruments of force, army, police, &amp;c. I wonder if South Africa has enlightened him any; at least we hope It has conveyed some light to those youthful minds before which the game Military Service is being dangled. But, of course, this is of no impor- tance to "Labor Omnia Vincit." Economics are not necessary; it --is only employers who 'should bother about these things. He talks somewhere about seven-tenths of their labour time being given to these. I wonder how this happens. Is this a result of economic law, , known only to those who confiscate the seven- tenths? If not, perhaps "Labor Omnia Vincit" will enlighten us. Our friend says: "When the people under stand." So say we, but they don't, and, what is more, never will until economics are as well understood by the workers as the employers; then, ignorant revolt, as "Labor Omnia Vincit" calls it, will become intelligent combat. What is meant by that phrase intelligent adaptation? What are we to conform to? Is it the so-called progress of modern capitalism that "Labor °Ennis Vincit" wishes us to adapt ourselves to? Adaptation to modern industrial requirements means acute ipecialisations, a sordid repetition ' of simple mechanical process. I know this , means greater production, so dear to the heart of employers, but it also means the annihilation ' of inan and the production of the automaton. Yes, progress will be slow until we get men 1 ' more ready to assist than to obstruct by useless 7 , and negative criticism. We may organise men 4 • industrially and politically, and yet remain stagnant if the fellowship of comrades is beguiled by minds incapable of accepting the success of their own movement. r When the struggle was carried to the work- -1 .</p>
        <p>shop not a few -revolutionaries began to seek excuses for retiring from the fight. It is passing strange that these mendicants have not the common decency to remain neutral, whilst the work they at one time claimed to live for is carried out for them gratis. "Free-doers are at a disadvantage as compared _ _ _</p>
        <p>with free-thinkers, because mankind suffers-mor• manifestly from the consequences of action than of ideas." (Neitasche.)</p>
        <p>• CHARLES DIIIKM 26, Dandonald-avenue, Stockton Heath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>COKE BETTER THAN COAL. An Example of How to Check the Smoke</head>
        <p>COKE BETTER THAN COAL. An Example of How to Check the Smoke</p>
        <p>Nuisance.</p>
        <p>The Gas Committee of the Salford Corporation ' has set a commendable example with regard to municipal efforts for the abatement of the smoke nuisance. In addition to circularising the fac- tories and warehouses in the borough, pointing out the advantages of using coke, which is claimed to be a smokeless fuel, they are offering special facilities for its use for domestic pur- poses. In order to bring the coke within the reach of all classes of consurw‘rs, it has been decided to sell it in smaller quantities and at a reduced price. The minutes of the Engineering and Metals Executive, submitted to the meeting of the Man- chester Chamber of Commerce on Wednesday, contained the following resolutio "That this Executive, believing that existing legislation with regard to smoke abatement is not oeing effectively carried out by some local authorit;cs, urges His Majesty's Government to afford facili- ties for the full discussion of a Smoke Abate- ment Bill, with a view to more uniform action by authorities.; P.tiMinistering larger areas." The Bard aclopted this resolution, and ordered that copies be sent to the principal Chambers of Com- merce in the United Kingdom. The other pro- ceedings of the two sections and their executivee were also adopted.</p>
        <p>" Prudential " Stall Dinner. On the kind invitation of Mr. S. Brocklehurst (superintendent) and his assistants, this now recognised annual event took pli4cs on Thurs,lay evening at the Winmarleigh Cafe, SarAey-street. Every member of the staff, numbering 50,was pre- sent, with the exception of two, a 110 c\ ere un- avoidably prevented through illness. One of the medical referees, Dr. Ferguson, as ansongst the company. Letters of apology for non-attend- ance were read from the Mayors of Birkenhead and Warrington, the former being the Inspector for the company for "G" Division. Mr. Brockle- hurst, in a very interesting speech, congratu- lated the staff on the successful results attained during 1913, the district having finished fourth out of 50 in the division. Further, he pointed out the indirect good a company like theirs, through their staff, was doing for the town, for no less a sum than £17,000 had been paid during the past year in claims locally, and, what was even more remarkable, since the commencement of the payment of sick benefits under the nevi' Act a sum of over .P. 3,100 had been paid by their company alone in Warrington. Several other enthusiastic speeches followed, and a very suc- cessful year would appear to be in store for thieli company in Warrington this year. A capitaH musical programme added to the enjoyment o:ti the occasion, and the following took part: Mt° Brough and Mies Brooklehurat, Messrs. S. u arocklekurst, Jack, Walker and Whitehouse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>G.F.S. Members. The Rev. J. Collins conducted the annual Girls' Friendly Society admission service at St. Thomas' Church on Sunday, when 20 girls were 'admitted. Miss Moores presided at the organ Und special hymns were sung.</p>
        <p>Unitarian Anniversary. The anniversary services of the -Cairo-street 'Unitarian Chapel were held on Sunday, when large Congregations assembled. Dr. Mellor Preached both morning and evening, and on each Occasion he referred rather briefly to his impend- ing departhre. He intimated that it was only after carefully considering the matter and coun- selling with men whom he respected, and whose opinions he valued, that he had decided to accept the invitation.</p>
        <p>‘Varrinutonian Honoured. It has given Warringtonians considerable Pleasure to note the appointment of Mr. A. J. Ashton as Recorder of Manchester. He received his early education at the Boteler Gramma', School, and his appointment comes after a very auceessful career. lie has become one of the recognised authorities on patent law and cora- PanY cases. Mr. Ashton is an ardent Liberal, _t_nd fought Mr. Gershom Stewart in the Wirral nivision at the last election, but he was in a lainerity by 2,316 votes.</p>
        <p>P. and K. Enterprise. ti A highly important step in connection with new Partington Steel and Iron Works was icaken on Monday evening, the first cast of steel ieing carried through satisfactorily, in the pre. ,soenee of .Mr. J. J. Bleckly and Mr. W. P. Pyland l s Crectae). To ensure that there will be no in any section the directors have got each ~tage of the process of manufacture' started Sep- 4,JatelY. In the spring of last Year the coke ,i'ena were used, and in autumn the blast fur- (Pee were brought into use. The final process, t°lling, • ithoped,will be commenced very</p>
        <p>Rev. G. A. Coupe Departs. I will always look back with preasure to the ,1111! I have spent in St. Barnabas' Parish." t;e11 were the words of the Rev. G. A. Coupe, ho concluded his ministry on Sunday, in reply- .ng to a presentation of a handsome oak chim• Mg clock to himself by Mr. J. E. Morgan (Vicar' lirarden), on behalf'of the congregation, who had tit together in the Schoolroom at the close of Bervice, under the presidency of the Rev. J. G. Dollingworth. Mr. Coupe, who has been :!Ill'ate at St. Barnabas' for the past two years, _flaS been appointed to the curacy of Bolton-by- tiowland, in the Diocese of Ripon.</p>
        <p>In the Name of Charily. Crosfield's Band are ever ready to assist charity. Following precedent, they are to give concert in aid of the Nursing Association in the Hippodrome to-morrow. The conductor, gr. W. llalliwell, is presenting an ideal pro- Rramme, and there ought to be a huge audience. Apart from first-class selections by the band, a trombone solo, "The Sailor's Grave," will be tilayed by Mr. J. E. Hacking, and a cornet solo, The Fairies of the Water," by Mr. W. Ellison. Miss Martha Taylor will doubtless charm with !ler sacred songs, and the accompanist work will ue performed by Mr. Percy Ingham.</p>
        <p>Merriment at -the AVorkhouse. i.;4e/TY was the time the inmates of the 1 War- 41gtan Workhouse spent on Thursday evening, ,- Tllhen an excellent entertainment was submitted. thi"te,T'e were songs, mostly bright and humorous, : akt quartets, banjo solos, recitations, and. -. "Thatil,eai " Mixem's Matrimonial Mart" and "a John. from Yorkshire." , The artistes .: ti'ilet. their guests with delight, and amongst the : 11, ougled Performers were Mesdames Grounds,l 'tessiee,dge, Misses Fletcher and Brocklehurst, , Fleto:' A. and W. 11. GrOunds, P. Pemberton, F, and C ' Whitley (banjo). The Bill were-:";t:ell Party, responsible for th3erepslsarysletGs,. 1110,s, ,i; 'ls. Poughsedge; Miss r! e.Y._ , and W. '• Nt9ss, o,llolt, J. Roughgedga , op rounds. , The company ~ 4nked for the excellency of the ewnetreert'a ~ , 1 The Contiri6 Popular- Leeture.. ii.lneamrtlty. ' 1,-11,e Museum Committee have arranged fee ...ainer series of popular lectures to be delivered Itt the Hall, der the auspices of the Co-operatiye under e °gden Trustees. There will be no charge for adinission, and, as Saturday evening has been etie_lected to suit the convenience of the many, lin.. tn'e should be good attendances. Each lecture id .;.stiWnielill be seen from the following list and pa be in the hands of an expert in his subjesubject,T_r- ulars: Saturday H , February 7th, Mr. B. H. , M.A., Curator of the Public Museums, - If'ord, will lecture on "How to enjoy a good jj,l,tll,re"; Saturday, February 14th, Mr. F. It Curator of the Walker Art Gallery, i:tverPool, "Black and White Art"; Saturday, rnarY 2lst, Mr. B. F. Martin, of the Victoria Itnd Albert Museum, South Kensington, " Artis- 'fie Glasswork"; Saturday, February 28th, Pro- lessor B. Newstead, M.Sc., F.R.S., Professor of etninelogy in the School of Tropical Medicine, ,(siverPuol University-, and a member of the last irlinrerninent expedition to Central Africa to testigate the cause of sleeping sickness, "A taturalist's wanderings in Br