        <p>, The Religion of Christianily." •t'rofessor Currie Martin gave the foairth and taat of the series of lectures on "The Great Reli- gions of the World," on Monday evening last, to a erowded audience at the Stockton Heath Adult •8 be4ool. Re said the new thing which the Zenhls of Christianity had added to religion summed up in the word " Love?' In the •Orirnitive religions the predominant element 'whs fear, in the Chinese religions it was rever- ttlee, in Islam it was power, and in Christianity ar eTe was thattitude of the individual to- Wds • . hts neighbour and his e,nemy. Christian. One of the great historic religions. It had • efinite origin at a Certain period in liistoi3- It" did not simply happen like other religions., of tad its causes alld its sources. The source t;re hristianity was Judaism, There were _ ,Inorernents of thought at work in the old cuiti• lsu religion—Legalism, and the Apo-calyptic t the time of Jesus. In the krst God • 'it; in the second God was near. Jesus th4letl was a Jew, who knew the traditions ii literature of Judaism. The Old I'esta- i tleYer been the same book since Jesus. Tertain tO ,rtir speak, underlined and emphasised gro.4)assages. Men did not•know what were _, Passages till Jesus pointeol them out. Teveo of Jesus, with His disciples, was to lite hitherto hidden meaning of the Fssen- tuld 411t1 critical passages, and which, rightly erstood, showed the meaning of the whole.</p>
        <p>Th Lateliford Garden Sodd. now ktunel_ Latchford Garden Society 'has cued upon its second year, having B,llo2ess. 11 „ oyercome the crisis of early infancy. This ins,s,,vident from the report at the annual meet - ,wig Pi'ash-lane Schools on Wednesday night,, 71i3 1641e1 e it Success, and stated that the annual show was a and the other work undertake society had also received Fortune, 14hve°111.r. The statement of accounts showed that eceipts amounted to £2B Bs. 6d., and this hditure to „t 26 19s 7d. The Rev. u Ihretggs, who presided, said he considered that _ could congratulate themselves upon the . eess attendant upon the first year. A society this description had been needed for some tqfrso, and although some of them were members the Stockton Heath Society, they really felt, \vier% was a need for. one nearer home, for there t„4.:4 Much scope in Latchford. It was very good Ctr have 120 members for the inaugural year, but Y should try to get more this year, for in Rolit'3llford and Latchford Without they had c-lethething like a population of 4,000. He was Ortih the committee had worked "downright cil'irds" and it was to their labours they owed the vreeeess• (Applause) The following officers iocre elected President, Rev. G. C. Briggs; hon. retarY, Mr. J. S. Burgess; hon. treasurer, Mr. i‘lizoll; and the following were elected the l_lbliiittee 1 Messrs. Posebottom, Turton, Wake- tockiHooker, Shakeshafti T. Taylor, G. Taylor, tt, (-1 Forster' Lawless, Johnston. B. II ‘ra,.11P1„.112 Harrop, Flindells, Toole, Holt, Ger- 7,;(;er. Saunders, Howleston, Kemp, Sykes and</p>
