      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Welsh Minister Honoured.</head>
        <p>A Welsh Minister Honoured.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. Parry Jones, pastor of the Welsh Church, Crosfield-street, Warrington, has been elected chairman of the Manchester Presbytery.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Y.P.S. . The meeting of the above, held on. Thursday .:night, was devoted to various papers, and im- promptu debates upon literary subjects. Mr W. Sanders occupied the chair.</p>
        <p>Minimum for Young Servants. The Warrington Guardians on Wednesday fixed a minimum for young girls going from Padgate Schools into domestic service at 2s. 6d. per week in wages, with 3d. additional as pocket money.</p>
        <p>Training College Fund. Next Saturday the Liverpool Players' Dramatic Company will give a performance of the comedy. "Liberty Hall" iu Latchford Parish Hall, in aid of the Warrington Training College Million Pennies' Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Goloshes for Bowlers.</head>
        <p>Goloshes for Bowlers.</p>
        <p>_ At a meeting of the Parks Committee the head gardener reported Amon the provision of goloshes for use on the bowling greens. It was resolved that goloshes be provided, and that an item of £5O be included in next year's estimates for the purchase.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Illness of Mr. 6outherden.</head>
        <p>Illness of Mr. 6outherden.</p>
        <p>The illness of Mr. Southerden for several weeks past has caused much anxiety to his family and friends, and about ten days ago Dr. Joseph and Dr. Harold Joseph operated for an internal complaint, with every satisfaction. It is hoped , Mr. Southerden will soon be out again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Accident at Pearson.and Knowles'.</head>
        <p>Accident at Pearson.and Knowles'.</p>
        <p>by Messrs. Pearson and William Butterton, aged 29, of 140, Hume- - The street, and empluYed Knowles, was admitted to the Infirmary on neoda, haring a piece of steel in his eye. steel was removed, but the vondition of the eye is serious.</p>
        <p>Guardians' Costly Deputations. At the meeting of the Warrington Guardians on Wednesday it was decided that the Chair- man, Mr. Bent; the Vice-Chairman, Mr. Jolley; and the Clerk, Mr. A. Bottomley, gates to a Poor Law Conference in be the dele- London next spent over .tlOO a year on such month. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell objected, as the "trip" would cost between .ers and -X2O. they spent were of little value. etelpreuatdayt'iotlhise,</p>
        <p>. . The Fatal Bath. Mr. F. A. Jones, the deputy coroner, in inquir- ing into the death of Harry Horton, the nine- year-old son of William Frederick Horton, of 36, Winifred-street, elicited a touching story. The mother was about to bath the deceased, and placed a bath of hot water near the kitchen fire. As the water was too hot, she went for some. cold water. Whilst she-was away she heard a scream and found that the bath had been upset ! and the deceased had sealded his feet. It was found that there Were only slight injuries, al-1 though the shock was severe.- Deceased died i from shock and meningitis on Tuesday. A ver- dict of "Accidental death" was returned, and the Foreman of the Jury, in expressing sym- pathy with the parents, said that the little fellow was a favourite with his playmates.</p>
        <p>Died Before Her 'Wedding. About to be married in April, death has inter- vened and removed the prospective bride. She was Beatrice Jones (20); daughter of Mr. Francis Jones, warehouseman, 32, St. Peter's-place, War- rington. The girl, who was in domestic ser- vice in Winwick-road, was drowned in the Mer- sey at Howley on Thursday morning. A man named Elijah Ball, of 22, Manchester-road, saw the girl acting strangely on the river bank, and he asked her what was the matter. She answered : "I should have turned into my place last night, but I never went: When • she reached Paddington Locks he saw her jump into the water. He ran to the place, but could not find her. The girl's sweetheart, the police state, informed them that when he saw her earlier on Thursday she was in good spirits. They had arranged to be married in April.</p>
        <p>Unitarian High Standard. Mr. F. W. Monks attended the annual meeting of the Unitarian Home Missionary College, Manchester, in the Memorial Hall, on Wednes. day, and seconded the adoption of the annual report, moved ,by Mr. Richard Durning Holt; M.P., which recorded the fact that the commit- tee had decided, after this year, to extend tho theological course to three years for all students, save in very special cases. This extension fol- lowed from the gradual development which tlui College curriculum had been undergoing foi many years past, and had become necessary owing to the wide range of instruction now re- quired by students for the Unitarian ministry. A student's entry into the ministry would also a be postponed for another year. The Chairman explained that it was absolutely essential fol thein maintaini v‘s-terl3i i the v. •lead,ofalvitel:laey:sr ministry that they should possessed standardnete only piety m but for learning.</p>
        <p>AhlennEvansasolnV.M. • MondayLowdas Alderman John in- stalled Worshipful Master of the Lights, No. 148, Warrington, by his son. Wg.Broof Harry H. Evans, a Past Master of the Lodge of., Friendship, No. 2,963, one of the daughter lodges. There was a large attendance, includ-1 jug many brethren of grand and provincial rank. The first part of the proceodings were presided : over by W.Bro. 11. IsherwoOd, the retiring W.M. The new W.M. appointed and inveSted is officer a as follows: Bros. 11. Isherwood, .P3•IIS-; C. J. Smith, S.W.; T. Irlam Fletcher,-T.V.; the Rev. 11. V. Pigot, P.Pr.G.C., Chaplain; J. Hallwood; P.M., Treasurer; T. , D. Amos, P.Pr.G.O., Secretary; R. T. Harding, S.D.; P. Peacock, J.D.; F. Howard, Pr.G.Std.,,D.C.; R. Shacklady, P.Pr.G.D.D.C., A.D.C.; W. B. Cook, Organist; John Muskeg, 1.G.; J. Banks, S.S.; 11. Haviour, J.S.; John 11. Singleton, James T. Locker and Thomas Locker, A.S.; and G. S. Rice, Tyler. The addresses were given by W.Bros. Shacklady and Rev. T. T. Lee-Jones P,.Pr.G.C.</p>
        <p>"Motor Lurry's Excessive Speed. Edward Leech Stockford, 24, Marsh-street, Warrington, was charged at Runcorn on Mon- day with driving a jleavy motor car along the Chester-road, Sutton Weaver, on January 13 at a speed exceeding eight miles per hour. Supt. Owen said that a Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., was driving a motor car along the same road at the rear of the defendant's ear for a distance of threequarters of a mile, and during that time Mr. Linaker's speedometer registered twenty ' miles an hour. Defendant had admitted that he was travelling about twelve miles an hour. Mr. Linaker corroborated, and said the reason he took particular notice of the speed was that he was accustomed to travelling abourtwenty miles per hour to his office at Frodsham. The road was a steep gradient. Stockford was fur- ther charged with obstructing the free passage of the highway on the same date. Defendant said he was not provided with a -Mirror, and, owing to the noise, could not hear the hooter of any car at his rear. There were three previous convictions. A fine of 20s. and 19s. 6. costs was imposed in the first case, and the defendant was ordered to pay costs, 9s. 6d. on the second charge.</p>
        <p>OSRAM and TANTALUM ELECTRIC LAMPS at reduced prices.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street. Warrington.</p>
