      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 31, 1914. " THEM( THOM E RULE DEPUTATION"</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 31, 1914. " THEM( THOM E RULE DEPUTATION"</p>
        <p>Forthcoming Auction Sales. Monday, 11 a.m.—Sale of Silcock-street Lock Works, Golborne; auctioneers, Messrs, Lomax, Sons and Mills; solicitors, Messrs. Woodcock, Stodart and Co., Wigan. Wednesday, 2 p.m.—Horse and cattle medi- cine, &amp;c.; auctioneer, Mr. R. Lowe. Thursday, 2 p.in.—Sale household effects, Market-place; auctioneer, Mr. Franw Hickman.</p>
        <p>"Our Lady's" School Dance. A fancy dress ball was given in the Co-operA- tive Hall last night, when VO juveniles and 300 spectators attended. The May.n. and Xlayore.ss were amongst the guests, as, also was the Mayoress of Dewsbury. Mr. P. Delaney was the M.C.. A concert was given by the Misses 31. Boyle, 31. Rabbitt, Rourke (Blackpool),-and- M. Warren; Rev. J. Tallon, and Mr. J. Babbitt. Accompanist, Miss A. Richardson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Municipal Officers as Shots.</head>
        <p>Municipal Officers as Shots.</p>
        <p>The new rifle range in connection with the Municipal Officers' Guild was formally opened last night at the Town Hall by the Mayor. A large number witnessed the event. The organ- isation of the club has been in the capable hands of Mr. M. A. Piercy, deputy b irough surveyor.</p>
        <p>Motor Fire -Engine for Town and Country. . _</p>
        <p>The Fire Engine Committee have decided that on and after the Ist April the motor fire engine shall be allowed to attend fires within a five- mile radius of the borough boundary at the same charges as at present made for the use of the steam engines; and that the charges for the use of the motor fire engine within the borough be the same as for the steam engines.</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society. Everyone acknowledges Mr. T. Lee Syras, of Tyldesley, as a master 01 portraiture, and to secure him for an evening's talk is to be certain of a good attendance, 'despite unfavourable weather. Tuesday's lecture on "Pictures, Painters and Photographers" was crowded with points especially valuable to portraitists, but, dealing as it did with all the things that go to mak% up a really great picture—composition, tones, light and shade, and deeper meanings— every phase of the photographer's art was touched upon, portraits, landscapes, decorative subjects and picturemaking generally. His slides illustrating the work of the greatest masters were extremely well-chosen and executed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Salvation Army Prize Distribution.</head>
        <p>Salvation Army Prize Distribution.</p>
        <p>The annual, prize distribution of the Sunday School of the'Salvation Army took place in the Acadenty-street Hall on Monday. Councilloi S. Gough presided and referred to the commend- able work done by the Army in this and other towns. As a Sunday School teacher of nearly 30 years' experience, he gave some very .encouraging and helpful advice to teachers. He also commented upon the influence of Sunday Schools upon the future of our nation. A varied programme rendered by the children, comprising solos, recitations, and a dialogue, woe given. Several interesting selections of music were contributed by the recently formed Young People's Band, under the capable conductorship of Bandmaster Tames Lee, of the senior band. The prizes were presented by the Chairman and included 39 firsts and 11 seconds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>St. Ann's Memorial Reredos.</head>
        <p>St. Ann's Memorial Reredos.</p>
        <p>The interior of St. Ann's Church has been undergoing a transformation, and special ser- vices are to be held next week, when the memorial reredos .will be dedicated. The oak- work which is in the chancel will greatly add to the beauty of the edifice. It is to be erected in memory of Mr. Joseph Bunch, formerly- a warden and sidesman, and is broken into five panels, four of which are filled in with full-sized figures of the evangelists. The communion table is also of oak, and in style of treatment is in accord with the reredos. Mr. Ralph John- son has been entrusted with this important. work, which has been carried out in a creditable manner. .The re-painting and decorating, which had been in progress for six weeks, was completed last Saturday, and the result does credit to Messrs. S. and F. Acton, who worked from the designs of Mr. Ernest Neale, of Man- chester.</p>
        <p>Clever People's College Pupils. The school concert of the clever pupils of the People's College in December was such a success that Mr. Wallington and his staff were invited to repeat it in aid of the Infirmary and Nursing Association. This was done and the triumph of December proved as great a triumph in January. The Parr Hall was crowded on Thursday even- ing, and the concert proved not only most en- joyable, but was highly appreciated, as the encores -indicated. The programme was the same and in some items the youngsters seemed to do better than at the earlier performance. "Hush, here comes the dream man," led by Ada Knight, and "Fair Areadee" were remarkably well rendered. Dora Hope sang sweetly "When the swallows homeward fly" "Who is Sylvia? a Shakespearean lyric, was well done by Neville Davies. May Woodland was delightful in a " humorous skit, "When grandmamma was quite a little girl," and well did she deserve the encore. A clever boy, Dyson Whitham, made a hit with "The land of I dunno where," and he was encored. A smart recitation, "A voyage of disaster," was given so admirably by Donald Dunbavand that he had to be recalled, a welcome fate which also befell G. 11. Clubley chese Salniadkeehspeaorarzn mcaodoenusponagn e"xleleylleJnutliperto.': iDnant gramme, which reflected the greatest credit on teachers and pupils.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>"Spider and Silkworm Authors.- ..</head>
        <p>"Spider and Silkworm Authors.- ..</p>
        <p>A lecture on "Random Recoll-e-c-t-i;;;'". was given by,Mr. H. H. Field, the general manager of Rylands Bros., Ltd., to a large attendance at the Rylands' Workmen's Recreation Club on Wednesday night. Mr. Leslie, the secretary, who took the chair, apologised for Mr. W. Petei Rylands' absence, as he was at that time in London. Mr. Field described authors as being of two classes---ene the spider, and the other the silkworm. The former, he said, wrote becausc, he was empty, and the latter because lie was full, and he advised his audience to choose the second of the two. Light literature was read too much to-day. 'When he said light literature, he meant that type in which they were in a hurry to get to the bottom of the page in order to turn over and see what fresh adventures "John and Angelina" had on the other side. It was better that they should read literature of a type which showed the marvels of the Almighty in the shape of the meanest things, amongst them being the kitchen fire, with its coal, the lumps of which each had such a romance as was undreamt of lying wrapped around their stony bosoms. The lecture, which was much enjoyed, came as a surprise even to those intimate with andMrie. trel d a z; uosus theylif didlife such not a thinkthithing that in s b eah i us ty busyof diction ever appealed to his mind. Mr. Field has promised to give a further lecture on the same subject next session.</p>
        <p>Sixty Years in One Works. The unique event of a workman receiving a presentation after sixty years, faithful service with one firm has occurred in Warrington this week. The recipient was Mr. William Watson, who last Tuesday completed this long time with Messrs. Peter Stubs, Ltd., Scotland-road. He received a handsome timepiece, and on a silver tablet is inscribed the following: "Presented to William Watson as a mark of esteem, by the directors and fellow-workers of Peter Stubs, Ltd., Warrington, on the completion of 60 years' service. January 27th, 1914." The veteran was born in Warrington in 1841, and on the death of his father went with his mother to Sheffield. When he was eleven years of age he commenced working and twelve months later he returned to Warrington and commenced his connection with Messrs. Peter Stubs, Ltd. During the whole of the sixty years he has had only one month's ill, ness, and that occurred about three years ago, when he was slightly hurt by the slipping of a ladder. In April, 1873, he married Mary, the eldest daughter of the late Mr. Thomas Daintith, of Grappenhall. Mr. Watson has been a mem- ber of the "Constitution" Lodge of Oddfellows, Manchester Unity, since 1864, and since 1888 has been the treasurer of the lodge. He has also been a District Trustee for over twenty years, and a trustee of the Children's Burial Society in connection with the Oddfellows for many years. He was a sidesman at the Parish Church for 'three years. He is a great walker, and for.his agels remarkably robust.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Some Criticisms of the" Findings" of Our</head>
        <p>Some Criticisms of the" Findings" of Our</p>
        <p>Visitors to Ireland.</p>
        <p>AN ULSTER PROTESTANT RIDDLES THE "REPORT.'</p>
        <p>BY CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>I notice in an interview with the representa- tives of the " Standard's that our Member, Mr. Harold Smith, says " The deputation's report, in view of the un- prejudiced statements supported by the Liberal section, should open the eyes of the country not only to the unwisdom of Home Rule, but to the duty of honest citizens ti 4 support the Ulster cause." And again: "Referring to my plan for housing the women and children in Warrington, in my own constituency, the initiation of this h scheme was largely due to the fact that we there, I believe, the largest Women's Unionist Association in Great Britain."</p>
        <p>The association he refers to is, of course, the chameleon-like organisation called the War- rington Women's Tariff Reform Association, whiCh can change its name and character to suit occasions. We cannot accept, at their face value, the claims of the members of the deputa- tion, made on their behalf by Mr. Smith. They appeared in the Press as an " unpreju- whose political complexion was 'evenly • balanced,- *equal numbers being labelled "L. and C•. as "unpreju- diced" " deputatio n? though they were equal in character and political conviction as well as have been skilfully used by Mr. Smith in the lim papers. He says he thinks it ought t es of the country, because oAi nfun u.t t o open . smb, number. Rule T n "unprejudiced" htuhe dieti cee' yunprejudiced statements" d• character, "supported by the Liberal section." lam in doubt whether most to condemn the tactics, or to praise the astuteness of the Member for Warrington for the clever way he has used the report to create political capital in spheres where the facts are not known. In such tactics he is a past-master and merits our admiration.</p>
        <p>If the deputies had put their claims on a lower level, had explained how definitely con- vinced Unionist politicians, Anti-Home Rulers, had roped in six individuals, who, from con- viction, or from some other cause, were occupy- ing a middle position, who were obliged to com- mandeer their past to establish a Liberal con- nection, then there would be no cause for ridicule, and their report would have been accepted for what it was worth. There is in any party always a certain number who, for various reasons, incline to change their poli- tical colour. Where this shows independence of mind, the attitude should not be condemned. But for such to join a Unionist-financed deputa- tion as representative Liberals, be conducted by special Unionist arrangements from town to town, be shown the special things that would impress them, send telegrams in the name of Liberalism, and allow themselves to be ex- ploited all over the country as Liberals, is con- duct which weakens the claims made to be unprejudiced," and also the right to repre- sent Liberalism. I have no desire to disparage the 'deputation, or to contend that the opinions formed in the tour, and by the surroundings, were not honestly expressed in their report. But this report is subject to a heavy discount because of the fact that the trip was planned, financed, directed and controlled by Anti-Homo Rule agency solely for Anti-Home Rule pur- poses. •</p>
        <p>The Ulster " Nots."</p>
        <p>But leaving the report, we naturally ask the question. What went these gentlemen forth to Ireland to see and to do? Was it simply to emphasise the barren negations of the North- East section of Ulster?</p>
        <p>These negations take the following forms We will not have Home Rule. We will not be left out of Hume Rule, Hence the majority of Ireland must not have Home Rule.</p>
        <p>We will not have Howe Rule within Home Rule, as suggested by Sir Edward Grey. Even if the British electorate again declare for Home Rule we will not submit.</p>
        <p>s sece st pi expectsol ann toaof- We search in vain the speeches of of those who if . it were enacted. hat would can ontrdh etaeorn siyHip, jteouaamnsktgeifioobßorl tangible which Rule, len, i deN. her t h Ulster- E intelligiblea w Warrington s difficulty mentioned. The report ' .Ulstertcs Ilioflee constitute ut e it a "crime ," thgeiou report that justified the statement about Home There is certainly nothing in the literature we get R.liiilieehbeloinogmsa Zcrlaimrgee."inThe reli concerns itself with industrial from Ireland is not even conditions and these are stated in incomplete and misleading forms. Even were the industrial condition fairly stated, they would not -: luatlfY the use of the word " crime" or furnish any good reason why Ireland should not manage her local affairs. Are the deputies prepared .to endorse the action of this section of Ulster in attempting to h old up the State and plunge i t in disorder? The Liberal party claims that all substance for real grievance and danger has been taken i away; if there is any left it s pledged further to safeguard the position, and in any way the Orange section of Ulster suggests, short of aban- doning the grant of self-gtinernment to Ireland. If Ulster is still determined to precipitate re- bellion it will be for a phantom.</p>
        <p>we can nis side better manage the Unionists Hopeless—But What of the Liberal Section? To the Conservative members of the deputa- tion it is useless to appeal. With them is still than the people themselves, that we know what is good for them better than they do ltheocabred ; that the om policy, which has been a ghastly failure, of concernsconviction o f lreland tha tf r management from Westminster, through Dub- lin Castle, is still the hest. Such appear to consider that the Irish agitation for self-gov- ernment can be ignored; ostrich-like, they hide their heads in the sand and think it is non- existent because they will not look at it. I appeal, therefore, to those members of the deputation who still call themselves Liberal. If they have a spark of Liberalism left, does the right of self-government carry no weight? Is no response to be made to the Irish demand maintained unabated through stress and storm, prosperity and adversity, for nearly one. hun- dred years? Is the principle of self-govern- ment, which has been found so beneficent in every section of this vast Empire, inapplicable to Ireland? If these gentlemen are still deter- mined to call themselves Liberal let them tell US how they are going to deal with this long- continued, consistent demand for self-govern- ment. If this principle means nothing to them, is not their Liberalism dead? And if so, were it not better that they brought their political title into harmony with their changed prin- ciples? ' If, however, it is the details of the present measure they object to, if the "crime" they , speak of in their report has reference to details,, let them expose this crime, and let us know how they would right it. But let them beware lest they fall into the error which characterised so much of the British legislation for Ireland during the nineteenth century. I mean the framing of a measure which, in the opinion of the six Warrington Liberals, the Irish ought to want, and ignoring what the Irish themselves say they want. And let them remember that the legitimate desire for freedom of the meat majority of the Irish people cannot b rut.n- lessly set aside without grave national danger, and cannot. be blocked merely because a section of one province pleases to act the parr of dog In the manger,</p>
        <p>BY AN ULSTER PROTESTANT. The following notes on the report of the Anti- Home Rule deputation have been sent to us by a gentleman from Belfast. He is an Ulster man and a Protestant, and occupies an im- portant and responsible position in the city. As the notes indicate, he is a convinced Home Ruler, and he furnishes good reason for his faith. We claim that his long residence in Belfast entitles his opinion to be considered with at least as much respect as those of the local gentlemen who, probably for the first time, spent one whole day in this city.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>I.—Rates and Slums.</head>
        <p>I.—Rates and Slums.</p>
        <p>In the absence of details of the towns the "commission" visited, it is impossible to reply to the first paragraph of their report in regard to any comparison of rates, conditions and pur- poses to which the rates are applied.. To con- trast the existence of slums in Dublin, a city , hundreds of years old, with Belfast, a city that has grown within the last fifty years or so, is both foolish and absurd. One might as well contrast the gum portions of Edinburgh or London, the accretions of hundreds of years, with some town, say, in Yorkshire or Devon- shire, that has sprang up within the last half- century. •</p>
        <p>I I.—lntimidation.</p>
        <p>If the Warrington "deputation" had had time or inclination they might have had many instances of how Unionist organisations have "indulged in intimidation, boycott, and social ostracism"; but do they assert that they came upon any "intimidation, boycott, or social ostracism" in Nationalist districts on religious or political grounds, and c.an they deny that where any cases of boycott [strange that they give no names, places, or details] were found it was in those parts of the country Where .the agrarian question still remains unsettled? Did the "deputation" find that in all the most im- portant Catholic towns many of the largest business men were Protestants who were depending entirely upon Catholic customers for their support? If the Nationalists and Catholics of Ireland are intolerant, would such a state of things exist? Moreover, if the Catholics and Nationalists of Ireland wished to persecute and crush the Protestants, what necessity is there to wait until Home Rule comes, seeing that out- side a few counties in Ulster the Catholics have overwhelming power if they wished to exercise I it? 111.—Land and Housing Reform.</p>
        <p>It is admitted that the Land Acts have effected a peaceful revolution in Ireland, and that the Labourers' Cottages Acts have also revolutionised the housing conditions of the labourer, but did the Warrington " deputa- tion " inquire, or were they informed, that these great reforms were only brought about -after years of agitation and self-sacrifice on the part of those very people who at the present time are seeking Home Rule, and that the opponents of these measures, which are now admitted to be producing such beneficent results in Ire- land, are the very people who are now opposing Home Rule?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>IV.—Prosperity and Emigration.</head>
        <p>IV.—Prosperity and Emigration.</p>
        <p>- - It is not denied that in North-East Ulster there are large and prosperous industries, but might .the same thing not be said of England, where,. I suppose, if these People were investi- gating, as between North and South, they would point to the industries, say, of Lancashire and Yorkshire against the lack of industries in, say, Devon, Cornwall and Kent, which are largely, if not entirely, agricultural or fruit-producing parts? How do they account for the fact that if life in Ulster is as "comfortable and pros- perous" as they indicate, in 1913 there were 12,392 emigrants from Ulster, as compared with 3,944 from Leinster; 6,744 from Connaught; 7,807 from Munster; so that Ulster actually sent away 1,704 young people more than Leinster and Connaught together, and 641 more than Leinster and Munster together?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Ulster Wages.</head>
        <p>Ulster Wages.</p>
        <p>Did the "deputation" make any inquiry in the "comfortable and prosperous district" as .to the rate of wages that are paid in the linen industry, as compared with those paid in the cotton industry in Lancashire? Would they be surprised to learn that in the land, which, according to them, is flowing with milk and honey, the average wage paid in the linen trade to men, women and children, is ns. Id. per week, whereas the average wage in the cotton industry in Lancashire to men, women and children, is 20s. 2d. per week, and in the woollen trade in Yorkshire is 20s. per week?</p>
        <p>V.---The Rights of Self-government. To say that the vast majority of the people of North-East Ulster (and it is something to be thankful for that in this connection they did not use the word "Ulster," which, it is pre- sumed they are aware, returns a majority of representatives as Home Rulers to the British House of Commons) are convinced that Home Rule would have certain disastrous results and be of no benefit to Ireland, is simply to assert that a certain number of people hold that opinion, just as the Unionist party, led by Mr. Balfour, refused to take any part in what. they considered a "disastrous experiment ",--the giving of a self-governing Constitution to SOuth Africa. Yet, despite these doleful prophecies, Mr. Balfour, in his recently issued pamphlet on "Nationality," admits that the granting of Home Rule to South Africa has been followed with beneficial results.</p>
        <p>Sincerity and Grim Determination, With regard to the opportunities that the deputation had of witnessing evidences of the " sincerity and grim determination" that exists amongst Unionists, they could have. witnessed, had they liked, just as much sincerity and grim determination amongst thousands of others in their desire to secure the establish- ment of an Irish Parlianmnt. What better proof of real sincerity and real deter- mination could be adduced than the fact that year in and year out, election after election, and during brutal coercion days. the overwhelm- ing majority of the Irish people have, with a political consistency unparalleled in British politics voted for Home Rule? As to the unrestricted opportunities they had of -conversing with the people in several of the largest works in Belfast, the people of Belfast know what "unrestricted opportunity" means when probable converts to Unionism are being chaperoned through Belfast and district, accord- ing to a pre-arranged programme, by the mem- bers of the Ulster Unionist party.</p>
        <p>Vl.—" Interests of Humanity." How can it be in the "interests of humanity" that the legitimate national aspirations of mul- titudes of the people of Ireland should be frus- trated? How can it be in the " interests of good government" that a minority, led by lawyers. K.C.'s, landlords and Privy Councillors, are, when constitutional methods fail, to be success- ful in resisting the wish of the majority, simply because that minority resorts to threats of civil war and general anarchy? How can it be in the "interests of democracy " that a minority should be able to veto permanently the wish of the great majority? How can it be in the " in- terests of Imperial safety" to deny self-govern- ment to a people demanding it, when the his- tory of the Empire proves that only by the granting of self-government to the different units of the Empire has the Empire attained to the</p>
        <p>(Cant nu tin next iluntn,)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>IRISH "DEPUTATION."</head>
        <p>IRISH "DEPUTATION."</p>
        <p>Letters from Messrs. Bennett, BeMan, and England.</p>
        <p>"EXAMINER" CRITICISMS.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—As a Warrington man and a regular __reader of your paper, I have been very much interested in the articles appearing in your paper recently on the Home Rule question, espe- cially as it affects Ulster. I have been living in Belfast for some time, and I have been amazed at the amount of ignorance shown in the mis- leading and most inaccurate statements pub- lished in your columns from time to time about Ulster bluff and wooden guns, &amp;c. May I be allowed to tell you that the Liberal patty are making a very big mistake if they think that the working men here are giving up their foot- ball and other pleasures to drill three and four nights a week and Saturday afternoons for fun and show. No; they are in deadly earnest, and I can say from personal knowledge that it is not the leaders who are urging the men on, but the other way about. The workers are Pie real moving power in this matter, and if the leaders gave up their positions to-morrow, the rank and file would soon find others to take their. places. I I know men who have made sacrifices to provide ! themselves with rifles and ammunition, and who can be depended upon to make the best use of them; good steady going men who are very de- termined that they will never submit to the unjust ptiWisions of this Bill, for they believe it would ruin their prospects by bringing trade to a standstill. That they .are prepared to lay , down their lives in defence of their right to re- main under the -Union Jack is certain. I hope :that your ridicule of the actions of these men will cease, for as night follows day civil war will . follow the pegging of this A.R.</p>
        <p>Belfast.</p>
        <p>4-, To the Editor of "'The Examiner." Sir,—The criticisms of the Radical party upon Lady Greene A's action in sending a dozen men to Ireland are somewhat beside the mark, and :show considerable fear of anything tending to 'show light on the question. Why should the Liberal party ex"pect to, choose the six Liberals? Do they suggest that they would have ihdeav- ourect to get the must open-minded and -dispas- sionate members of their party? It was never suggested that the six, Liberals were officially representative of the Liberal party. That they were Liberals is undoubted, albeit, perhaps, of a somewhat more independent type of mind .than some members of the Liberal Association. .That they were sent under Unionist auspices :does not detract from the value of their reports. ,Surely the presence of the Unionist agent did ;not prevent them forming their own conclu- sions?</p>
        <p>. The most absurd criticism was that of "A :Walton Tory," who, shows his "Toryism" in every line of his letter. I suspect if the veil of 'anonymity behind which he modestly shelters 'himself were drawn aside we should find a very .strong Liberal' or Radical. It is a surprisingly childish device to write a strong party letter, and then pretend it comes from a member of the party you are denouncing: Surely in "vain is the net spread." The plain fact established— and no, one claims more, I understand—is that half a dozen men of Liberal' sympathies, but fairly open minds, came to a practically unani- mous conclusion adverse to Home Rule. . G. W. S. JARRETT, 1 , Provincial Secretary Primrose League. ' Hale, Cheshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</head>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>. _ Sir,—l have read with interest your own and other people's criticism on the report of the local deputation of Liberals and Tories to Ireland. Whilst it is not for me to doubt your veracity as to the town being convulsed with laughter, I ' fail to see why the spirit of levity -should be indulged in, even if the most humble citizen of this town had taken an opportunity of seeing for himself, if only fov a day, what is actually happening. in. Ireland. _ _ . .</p>
        <p>Your criticism of the Liberal section is, to put it mildly, extremely harsh, and you will pardon my saying so, not quite true. However, I fail to see what this has got to do with our joint report, and I am prepared to leave the question of these gentlemen's politics to a more unbiased section than the officials of the Liberal party, viz., the men amongst whom they live and work. With reference to our report, I feel that pro- bably the best method of proving our case up to the hilt would be to carry out the suggestion of Mr. 11. W. Griffin, and I shall be only too pleased to co-operate with him by collecting and subscribing to a fund for the purpose, and I should be extremely obliged if the Honorary Treasurer of the Liberal Association would also join in. Knowing by my short stay in Ireland the seriousness of this question, I feel no efforts should be spared to educate the people of this country as to the true position .4 lifia,its in Ire- land. Personally, I should be prepared to leave the judgment of our report to this deputation, and would even agree it should consist of eleven Liberals and one Tory, providing they were not "official" Liberals. There has, up to now, been no serious criti- cism of our report, but if any does arise I hope you will kindly extend to us the opportonity of ;proving our case. Your correspondent " Serious" is apparently :very much upset, and I believe the only possible :cure would be the sobering effect of a visit. It ,may be of interest to him, along with our other critics, Mr- P. M. Oliver and Mr. E. Wynne !,Jones, to know we have had the pleasure of ,speaking to Nationalist' M.P.'s, mayors, solici- tors, business men and prominent officials of the Nationalist organisations, and if further proof is required of the representative character of our inquiries, I need only say their official news- paper of Sligo comments on the unrestricted opportunity they had of-laying their case before us. I will even go further and say there was not a single member of-the deputation who did not wander about to make his own private inquiries in every place we called at, and with- out the least restriction. I merely mention these matters so that time can be saved if any- one wishes to criticise the opinions we hare expressed. W. H. I3ELLLkn The Spinney, Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner." Sin—The only letter appearing in your issue of last Saturday worthy of a reply is the one signed "H. W. Griffin." It is to the point, and not so " scurrilous "as the others were. The other writers appear to be suffering from poli- tical prejudice and excitement. Mr. Griffin suggests a further deputation. Why not? It can easily be arranged. If the Liberal Association are prepared to pay their share, I, along with Mr. Griffin and others, will give a donation towards this end. When they come back and report, as we did, and as six other Liberal Scotchmen also did, they must be prepared to be sent to everlasting perdition for truth is not always palatable to some political people, unless it happens to fall in with their particular doctrine and faith. Why didn't "Serious" sign his name? What is he afraid of? It doesn't say much for the man's spirit to shelter himself under a "nom de plume." His "nom de Plume," however, should have been "Dreamer," for our report evidently a se, upset his digestion that his sleep was dis- tulrbmedigiaandrelmieinhdadouthr eot";lenrigelritimticas thre"abtd personally abuse is no argument. Nothing will be kept</p>
        <p>5</p>
        <p>back, not even the photograph of .the National- ist M.P., and other prominent Nationalists, who were taken along with the deputation. As Josh Billings said: "Facts are stubborn things," and all the ink slinging by our critics won't get over the report. E. ENGLAND. 1, Mill-street.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner." Sir,—lt is very good of you to devote nearly a page of your last issue to the party of "pollU- cal nonentities" who have just returned from a most interesting and instructive week in Ire- land. It is not necessary for me to defend the members of the party who are opposed to me in politics from the somewhat ungenerous critic- isms to which their honesty and candour have exposed them. I spent a week with them, in constant intercourse, and they all struck me as undoubted Liberals of a peculiarly straightfor- ward and intelligent type, and, if the worst that you can allege against them is that one of them was naturally open-minded enough, some time ago, to express his doubts as to the infallibility of Free Trade—which, by the way, is an economic, and not a political, question—l do not think they need to worry much. As to the ill- natured innuendoes of your correspondents, most of which are as peevish as they are untrue, it may, perhaps, be better to treat them with contempt.</p>
        <p>I gladly acknowledge that you were fair- minded enough to print our report, which nearly all your Liberal contemporaries carefully sup- pressed; but neither you nor they have made any attempt to dispute our conclusions. These are the things that really matter. They were arrived at quite unanimously, after full discussion, and after many quite exceptional opportunities of seeing and hearing both sides of the question, and every one of them is fortified by evidence, the force of which I venture to believe that even an "official" Liberal would find it difficult "to resist. This is all available, and much of it will, no doubt, be made public in due course. We challenge you to dispute it.</p>
        <p>- . ARTHUR BENNETT. Warrington, 30th January, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>"THE ANTI-HONIE RULE DEPLTATION " continued.</head>
        <p>"THE ANTI-HONIE RULE DEPLTATION " continued.</p>
        <p>position of strength and solidarity which it has reached to-day? Has it not been proven, again , and again, that the denial of self-government to a people has been practically the only thing which has ever seriously threatened the safety and unity of the Empire? To force any measure into law may be "a blunder and a crime," but if Constitutional government is to be the recog- nised method of government in this country it might be a far greater blunder and a far more serious crime not to force a measure into law as an absolute necessity for the preservation of even the semblance of democratic and cenetitu- tionai government. ' '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>CANADA AND TARIFF.</head>
        <p>CANADA AND TARIFF.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edward Porritt Shows How the Farmers Are Attacking Duties.</p>
        <p>THE PEOPLE'S FOOD QUESTION.</p>
        <p>In an article contributed to a recent number of the "Westminster Gazette," Mr. E. Portia, formerly. of Warrington, now of Hartford, Conn., U.S.A., discusses the question of Tariff politics in Canada, pointing out the enormous influence which the recent Tariff legislation in the U.S.A. is bringing to bear on Canalian opinion. Says Mr. Porritt:— , The tariff is again the predominant issue in Canadian politics, not because the Conservative party has voluntarily changed its attitude; not on account of the speeches which Sir Wilfrid Laurier recently made in Hamilton and Mon- treal; but chiefly because the United States tariff has undergone a general reduction, and because the organised grain growers of the prairie provinces are agitating for the repeal of the duties on grain and flour, and for many other changes in the Dominion tariff in the in- terests of consumers. The whole political situ- ation in Canada to-day is largely due to the fact that the grain growers have been continually demanding reductions in the tariff since Sir Wilfrid Laurier made his epoch-making tour of the four Western provinces in 1910. To-day the organised grain growers an? farmers of four provinces—Ontario, Manitoba Saskatchewan and Alberta—are using the down ward revision at Washington as one of several arguments in favour of a downward revision at Ottawa; and when it comes to voting strength the grain growers and farmers of these four provinces easily outweigh the 3,000 members of the Canadian Manufacturers' Association.</p>
        <p>When the representatives of the grain growers. and farmers were in Ottawa on December 16th, in 4dditiop to the change necessary to free them from the retaliatory duties of the American i tariff, they urged (1) a reduction n the tariff on imports from Great Britain to one-half of the duties in the general list, and further gradual reductions until in five Years there - shall be Free Trade with Great Britain; (2) that all foodstuffs shall be transferred to the free list; (3) that agricultural implements and ' cement shall also go on the free list, and (4) that any tariff or trade concession granted to any other country shall be immediately ex- tended to Great Britain. . . . The provincial Legislatures of Saskat- chewan and Alberta have both . this winter passed resolutions supporting the demands of the grain growers as regards the free list and the United States tariff. In both these Legis- latures the Liberals are in a majoritt ; for these provinces are the most Radical in the Dominion. A similar resolution is to be proposed by a Con- servative Member of the Manitoba Legislature. At Winnipeg the Conservatives are in power; and the relations between Ottawa and Winni- peg are pretty. close. The Conservative Gov- ernment at Winnipeg will not embarrass the Borden Government; and if a resolution of the Manitoba Legislature supporting the grain growers were unacceptable to the Ottawa Gov- ernment, there is little likelihood that it would have been placed on the order-book by a Con- servative Member. It would be good politics for the Borden Government to accede to the de- mands of the grain growers--politics that might . stave off for a few years reductions in the tariff such as would be unwelcome to the manufac- turers; and the likelihood is that in the coming session of Parliament the necessary legislation will be passed to open the American market to Canadian grain growers and Canadian millers. An this is in direct contradiction to the atti- tude taken up by Mr. White, the Minister of Finance, who, according to a Renter's message, published in the "Pall Mall Gazette" of .Tann- ary 21st, declared that the present moment was not propitious for a downward revision of tariff.</p>
        <p>He did not believe that duty free food would reduce the cost of living, while it would affect industries employing 52,000 hands, and having an annual output of 25.000 dollars. All the evidence available seems td show that it is Sir Wilfrid Laurier and not Mr. White who has correctly ganged the mind of the Dominion on the question of the people's fcco.</p>
        <p>IF YOU WANT POSTERS (we print the largest one-sheet poster of any house in Warrington), SHOW CATALOGUES, AUCTIONEERS' CATALOGUES,</p>
        <p>PROGRAMMES, BOOKS OF WORDS, HANDBILLS, ARTICLES of ASSOCIATION LITHOGRAPHY, DIE-STAMPING, SHOW &amp; WINDOW CARDS, Any Kind of UP-TO-DATE PRINTING in fact,</p>
        <p>CONSULT</p>
      </div>
