        <p>11;',.</p>
        <p>i, 4.' ( - , t '</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>• .</p>
        <p>Ye owners of unkempt gardens, look out ! The Garden Society are to be on your track. They object to so many gardens existing In a neglected state, when for a few Pence tuey could be made things of beauty. The cost of looking after the trees, which Ornament the chief roads, during 1913 Amounted to £2 135., and there is still . About £9 18s. in hand—equal to four years' work. This is money extremely well spent. The "Looking for Miss Montgomery" case Will next be heard of at the Chester Assizes. The inhabitants of the parish feel grateful To the police for the Way they have handled the matter. The Chrysanthemum Show was in danger. However, it is to go on for another year. Wives and sweethearts, you must really take More interest in the welfare of the Garden Society. Do flowers—not to mention vegetables—appeal To the lady "Heathens" ? If they do then Why are they so disinterested in the. Garden L.iety? They should be the first to encourage the "Hubbies" and the " boys " to go in for gardening. Troubles which afflict the "Heathens" are: (1) Scores of senseless barking dogs. (2) Unnecessary hooting of motor horns. (3) Church or Boys' Brigades practising buglers. (4) The sirens of the ships upon the Canal. There is talk of forming a Noise Abatement ' League. Cheshire is now free of tramps, says the Chief Constable. • :* • These "road gent?' always give the Heath a missing baulk. What has come over the cottage building scheme?</p>
        <p>Have '‘he District Council quietly put it on the shelf?</p>
        <p>"Hie! Who are you looking for?" is the latest query?</p>
        <p>_ "I'm looking for Miss Montgomery I" is the answer. And this is proving the latest catch-phrase with the " Heathens."</p>
        <p>Will of Mr. W. Crampton.—The will of the late Mr. William Crampton, of "Penrhyn," Whitefield-road, Stockton Heath, who died a few weeks ago, shows that he left estate veined at -t9,541.</p>
        <p>The Ice-rammed Ship.—The "Manchester Commerce," which had a sensational encounter with an iceberg in the Gulf of St. Lawrence, passed through the Ship Canal during the week- end. The bow of the vessel, temporarily patched, bore signs of the force of the impact..</p>
        <p>Returned After Many Days.—A "Heathen," some two years ago, lost a ring when working in his garden. While transplanting a privet hedgo a few days ago he found the ring, little the wors for its burial, attached to one of the roots.</p>
        <p>The New Tram Stage.—The new penny stage from Victoria Square, Stockton Heath, to Flet- cher-street, has been in operation this week, but the public have used it very little. It ought, however, to be appreciated when the first senior football match takes place, as •a good many- " Heathens" attend at Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>A Novel Exhibition.—Thursday was a red-let- ter day in connection ,with the members of St. Thomas's Band of Hope. The girls brought their Christmas dolls, and the boys their toys or things they had made. These interesting articles were arranged on the platform by the lady workers, and made a very nice display. It was also Christmas card competition night. There was a large number of cards sent in. The work of the members, done on plain post cards, was excellent. The exhibits had been examined by Mrs. Milling and Mrs. Bedell and other ladies, who awarded six, prizes to the boys and four to the girls, and complimented all the members on their work. Mrs. Milling presented the prizes to the winners and chocolate to all the other members. Mrs. V. Davies, who regretted being unable to be present, sent a large number of oranges, which were given to those who had brought dolls or toys, and to the card competi- tors. The Vicar spoke very highly of the dis- play, and asked the members to try and please by their conduct the ladies and workers who were so kind to them. Mr. George Davies pro- vided gramophone selections; Miss Heath gave a suitable recitation. Mr. Hallows spoke to them on perseverance. At the close Mr. White- man proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Davies, to Mrs. Milling, Miss Heath and to Mr. George Davies. This was seconded by Mr. Stannard, and carried heartily.</p>
        <p>r LITT- .00D. ---1 1, 'DR. SANDS' LIVER SEEDS For INDIGESTIO7 Each Bottle Wrav • 01 all CI</p>
        <p>STOeKTON HEATH.</p>
