      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>"LOOKING FOR MISS AIONT-</head>
        <p>"LOOKING FOR MISS AIONT-</p>
        <p>GOMERY."</p>
        <p>A Youth's Curious Conduct Leads to Coruniittal to the Assizes.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR THANKS TIIE POLICE. An unusual incident in a Court of Law hap- pened at-a special sitting of the Stockton Heath Petty Sessions on Tuesday afternoon, when Councillor James Whitley, chairman of the Parish Council, spoke in glowing terms of the smartness of Acting-Sergeant White, who cap- tured an alleged housebreaker in Stockton Heath:</p>
        <p>The prisoner was a youth named Reginald Abey, who stated that he Was only 17 years of age, and that he lived at 35, Patterson-street, Earlestown: He was charged on two counts with breaking and entering dwelling-houses, and on another count with attempting to break and enter another dwelling-house.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>MONEY BOX VANISHED.</head>
        <p>MONEY BOX VANISHED.</p>
        <p>Mr..Arehibald Marshall, manager; 14, Cawdor- I street, Stockton Heath, said that on the morn- ing December Ist he left home. Then a money box (produced) was in the cupboard in the back.kitchen, and it contained about 4s. He returned in the evening, when the box was.mis- ,sing, but he did not report his loss to the police. Nothing else in the house had been disturbed. He again saw the box on January 12th, when Inspector Breese took it to the house. He valued the box at sixpence. He did naftnow the prisoner. John Antwiss, a gardener, of Old London-road, Appleton, said he was in the road leading to Brookfield Farm on January 9th, and on looking over the hedge he saw the money box lying at :the bottom of the hedge. He afterwards handed it to the police.</p>
        <p>"LOOKING FOR In the second case Mrs, Maud Smith, wife of Mr. Walter George Smith, 50, Grappenhall-road, Stockton Heath, said she went out in the after- noon of January 16th. When she left all the windows and doors were secured. She returned in about half an hour. She was accompanied by a small dog, and on arrival at the front door the dog began to bark and ran towards the backyard gate. Witness followed and saw a man coming down the garden. She asked him: "What do you want here?" and received the reply: "I am looking for Miss Montgomery." Witness then said: "She does not live here; what did you open the door for?" but prisoner walked away without giving any further explan- ation. After the prisoner had gone she SaW the step-ladders had been taken fran an outhouse and placed against the wall, just underneath the water tank, near the back bedroom window. She informed the police and gave a description of the man. She had since identified the.prisonei as" the man she saw in the yard. WHAT THE GIRL SAW. Elsk Cartwright, a domestic servant, em- ployed at Ashley House, Grappenhall-road, said that on the afternoon in question she was in her bedroom, which overlooked Mr. Smith's house As she was looking out of the window she saw a man go through the front fate of Mr. Smith'd 11 house. After trying the door he went round to the back gate and made an unsuccessful attempt to climb into the yard. He then went into a field at the end of the house and climbed over the garden fence into the yard. She saw him get the step-ladders and rear them against the wall near the water tank. She witnessed the arrival of Mrs. Smith, and after Mrs. Smith had spoken to the man he walked away. She had since identified him as the prisoner. She thought the man would be about 20 years of age. Prisoner (sobbing) : I am only 17. A COUNCILLOR'S STORY.</p>
        <p>With regard to the third case, Councilloi James Whitley, who resided at the Post Office, Walton-road, Stockton Heath, said that on January 16th he visited the house, Penrhyn, Whitefield-road, Walton Inferior, and left every- thing safe. He was in charge of the house during the absence from home of the occupier, Mrs. Crampton. Witness next visited the house on Sunday, January• 18th, and, to his surprise, he found the garden gate open. He went round the back of the house and found the back gate unfastened. The kitchen window was broken, and the broken glass and stones were lying on the window-sill. There were fresh foot prints underneath the window on the garden bed. _ .</p>
        <p>Everything in Great Disorder. On going into the kitchen he found everything was in great disorder. Cupboards and drawers were opened, a drawer from the sewing machine was on the table. A constable was passing at the time and witness called him in. The two stones produced were the stones found on the P.C. window-sill. eCjompton corroborated Mr. Whitley' ev s Told to the Policeman. 1 P.S. White said on the afternoon of January 17th lie was in Walton-road, Walton Inferior, wleen he met the prisoner, who was just cOming down the footpath belonging to the house called "Newlands," Walton-road. Witness stopped the man and said "Where do you belong top" and the man replied " Earlestown." In reply to a query as to his business at that particular house the man replied : "I was inquiring for Ass Montgomery." The man was taken to the rli lice Station, as he was not satisfied with the statement</p>
        <p>Mr. T. P. Bradsha-,v, the Clerk: Why did you stop the man? Sergeant White: Because he answered the des- cription of the wanted man. Sergeant White added that he cautioned the prisoner, who made a voluntary statement in which he admitted the offences.</p>
        <p>A COUNCILLOR'S THANKS. Councillor J. Whitley, after' accused had been committed to the Assizes, said he could not allow the opportunity to pass without recognis- ing, in some way, the 4bility displayed by Ser- geant. White in the case. The inhabitants. of Stockton Heath considered that the Sergeant had effected a very clever capture, and it was deserv- ing of some recognition. He wished to express the thanks of the public' to the police. , • Superintendent Owen said he Would bring Mr. Whitley's remarks render the notice of the Chief Constable, although, he might say, he had lready reported the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>STOCKTON HEA TH Continued.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEA TH Continued.</p>
        <p>THE HEATII'S NEGLECTED</p>
        <p>GARDENS.</p>
        <p>The Garden Society Secretary Suggests a Better Culture Scheme.</p>
        <p>LOSS ON CIIIiVSANTIIEMUM SHOW.</p>
        <p>- ------ - "The comnaittee.are sorry to have to again call attention to the -fact that there are still many garden plots very indifferently cultivated. They suggest that an effort should be made to influence either landlord or tenant to do their share to improve their surroundings. After 12 years' work it is felt that there should not be so many neglected gardens as are to be seen on some of the mail' roads of Stockton Heath, especially as in most cases a very small effort would make them exceedingly attractive." This important iecommendation appeared in the annual report submitted to the annual meeting of the .Stockton Heath Garden Society held in the Hall Cafe on Monday evening. Sir Gilbert Greenall, who presided, was pleasal that the society had a balance in land. If they kept on becoming wealthy probably somebody would tax them. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>SYSTEMATIC CAMPAIGN WANTED. Mr. E. G. White submitted the annual report, which stated that the meMbership had not been quite so large as in the former year, but to smile extent that was accidental and did not indicate any falling off in interest in the society's work. It was mainly due to members having left the district, and the committee would use every effort to have the ranks reinforced. They sug- gested that a systematic campaign should be instituted-to bring large additions to the mem- bership, so that the influence of the work might be felt in every home. (Hear, hear.) • The summer show was a success, and had been attended by upwards of 3,000 people. The Chrysanthemum Show in September in respect of the quality of the exhibits was very success- ful, hut it was felt that members ought to take more interest in it. If not, the committee would have to consider the desirability of discontinu- ing that show</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. Ellison, seconded by Mr. Garton: the report was adqpted.' unani- mously. 4</p>
        <p>PECULIARITY OF THE "HEATHENS." Mr. D. E. Watson, the hon. treasurer, in sub- mitting the balance sheet, which showed a fav- ourable balance of £7l Ss. 5d., compared with £3B 15s. id. last year, asked to be relieved of the duty of treasurer on account of business engage- ments occupying all his time. ' I am not leaving the society, he said, for any other reason than that I have not the time re- quired for the work. It is a peculiarity of Stockton Heath people that if anyone resigns an office they want to know what is at the back of it. I say here, I have no complaint against the society, and I am sorry circumstances re- quire me to resign. He would have liked to have seen a balance of £lOO to £2OO invested in Warrington Corpora- tion stock, but now they had a balance of £7l. If they were to continue having a financial suc- cess they must, at the Summe; Show, provide attractions other than flowers to get the people of Warrington to attend. (Hear, hear.) Mr. H. B. Houghton proposed, Mr. W. S.kelton seconded the adoption, and this was done unani- mously. _</p>
        <p>THE OFFICE-BEARERS. The office-bearers were elected as follow : President, Sir Gilbert Greenall ; vice-presidents, ;Rev. J. Collins,, Rev. H. V,. Piga, Messrs. R. G Armitage, T. Baxter, J. H.*Clarke, L. G. Davies, V. B. Davies, E. J. Hall, VV:' H. J. Jenkins, _R. C. Parr, A. J. Willett, R. I. Wynne-Jones, W. Bain- bridge, and H. G. Westbrook • treasurer, Mr. J. bit; secretary, .Mr. E. G. White; connnittee Messrs. W. H. Woodcock,, J. H. Fletcher, F. Baker, J. B. Baxter, J. Bell, D. S. Birrell, E. Bradshaw, F. Brook, G. A. Dunlop, J. Ellison, R. Garton, G. Houghton, H. B. Houghton, .F A Jenkins, J. McDermid, J. Morris, J. Owen, T. H. Plevin, F. A. Rathbone, W. Skelton, F. Tim per- ley, E. Vernon, E. Whitehouse, A. S. Wright, W. Yarwood, F. Broady J. D. Cannell, J. Ferguson, R. Mothrie, and J. Richardson. • WOMEN'S AID WANTED. , Mr. White, thanking the meeting for his re- , election as secretary, said they did not have the. interest taken in the society by the ladies they had hoped for. If those members with wives would get their wives to take an interest, and those who had not wives got their sweet- hearts interested, it might greatly help. (Laugh, ter and applause.) , They had had 3,000 persons at the Summer Show, and that indicated they were interesting the public, and it must be the duty of the com- mittee to still further find means of attracting large attendances. He was satisfied with the financial position, but he was not with the entries at the show., Five years ago they had 500 to 600 entries, last year they had only 370. He would be glad to learn the reason for such a decrease. Competitors should send in more exhibits and every member should be a com- petitor. (Applause.) THE CHRYSANTHEMUM SHOW. There was a long discussion as to whether the Chrysanthemum Show should be held in the, autumn, after the meeting decided to hold the Summer Show on Bank Holiday, 3rd August. It was stated that the Schedule Committee had decided to recomtnend that the show be not held on account of the loss.</p>
        <p>Mr. Watson said at. the last Chrysanthemum Show Gs. was taken at the door, and the cost of running it was £l2 ss. 41 There was a net loss each year of about 1;10.</p>
        <p>Mr. White said in 1012 they had 40 competitor 3 and in 1913 that number had decreased to 28. Mr. Clarke did not think it right to look upon the £lO as an actual loss. Against that had to be set the opportunity given to members to attend, and they were a source of income, lie moved that the committee reconsider the advis- ability of holding the show.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>GREAT MISTAKE._</head>
        <p>A GREAT MISTAKE._</p>
        <p>Mr. Timperley thought it would be a great mistake to stop that show. He moved that it be held as usual. Mr. J. 11cDermid said it was a show much ap- preciated by members. Mr. Clarke then withdrew his motion, and .Mr. Timperley's was carried unanimously, the committee being empowered to make the neces- sary.arrangements. Mr. White said they did not .get the- support they expected from gentlemen with gardens and greenhouses. some years ago they sent „out! 26 letters asking for exhibits. They got one iepty,l and it was in the negative. (Laughter.) So- thing more might he done in that direction. (Hear, hear 4</p>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL. MR. P. 111. OLIVER AND TIIE PILORINIS.</p>
        <p>AVhat the_ Warrington Trippers Vere</p>
        <p>Not Allowed to See.</p>
        <p>MR. JACKSON HOLMES AND TIIE NAM</p>
        <p>An important Liberal meeting, under the auspices of the Knutsford Division Liberal Association, was held on Tuesday night, in the Methodist New Assembly Room, Knutsford-road: Grappenhall. Mr. C. Jackson Holmes presided, and. he was supported by Mr. P. M. Oliver, Mr. J. Bathell-Jones, Mr. Prank Fox, Mr: Robert Nelson, and Mr. J. Wood.</p>
        <p>" VERY_ DEPLORABLE."</p>
        <p>The Chairman alluded to the Navy Estimate. He did think that in these enlightened times it was very deplorable that we had to spend. so much upon our military and naval organisa- tions. They were always told, of course, that there was ttie fear of an attack from Germany. He did not think that Germany was at all anxious to attack England. Ho had a very pleasant and very intimate acquaintahce with a number of Germans and they always pooh- Peohed the idea. He hoped the opposition to the great expenditure on naval matters would have an effect upon the Government, and bring about a reduction of the estimates. ;r</p>
        <p>THE TORY- NICKNAMES. Mr. Oliver said that everybody would agree that the Opposition papers who called the Liberal party the " Suicide Club" were very wide of the mark. He did not think there wari anyone, certainly any Liberal, who was desirous of endangering the security of the country. They were sometimes called "Little Eng- landers," now they Were called the "Suicide Club," but they had no desire, to .see their security in any way diminished, and they had certainly no intention, while they were in office, to_commit suicide.</p>
        <p>He thought figures showed that they had an overwhelming superiority and absolut• security. .According to the Official figures, Germany ex- pends £23,000,000 Sr on the Navy, and the Triple iAlliance £39,000,000. We alone spent £47,000,000, or, if they took the triple entente, £97,000,000. They should be safe with figures like that.</p>
        <p>The Irish Deputation. To come nearer home, he said there had been a deputation to Ireland—claughter)—composed of the twelve apostles from Warrington. These Pilgrims visited nine places and travelled 2191 miles, probably most of it in railway trains, and the six 'Liberals now considered they were competent to over-rule the judgment of Mr. Wil- ham Ewart Gladstone. This pilgrimage was not tragic, but rather farcical.. He wanted them to realise how one-sided the ." inquiry" was.</p>
        <p>In "The. Warrington Examiner" of last Sat- urday there appeared an article reprinted from "The Ulster Guardian," which showed how " impartial " the tour had been. The deputa- tion did not visit the Ulster Liberal Association, or the United Irish League in Belfast, neithex did they visit the Dublin Liberal Association, or the Dublin United Irish League.</p>
        <p>" Absolute One-sidedness." The absolute one-sidedness also was apparentin a telegram sent by a Liberal, who told them "that all trade nnionists here (in Ireland) unite in condemning Home Rule." ,As a matter of fact, the Chairman of the Belfast Trades Council and other trade union leaders he mentioned were all in favour of Homo Rule. Mr. Ravenscroft would have to have another shot. It was only natural that the trade union officials should be in favour of Home Rule, because the very fact of the question being unsettled interrupted the social reforms which Ireland needed. Still examining the perfectly impartial manner in which the inquiry was conducted, they found that the deputation did not visit such a large works as Messrs. Harland and Wolff's, but that of Messrs. Workman, Clark and Company, where all the recent bitterness amongst the Pro- testants and Roman Catholics commenced. A gospel, or whatever they liked to call it, had been issued, and apparently it contained all the reasons which had converted the six Liberals to the opposition of Home Rule. Mr. Oliver dealt with the various pare graphs of the " trippers' " report, showing that in the main it was an indictment against the Conservative idea. The Land and Housing Questions were also referred to by Mr. Oliver.</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY HAPPENINGS. Mr. Bethel-Jones remarked that it appeared there had been some extraordinary happenings in Warrington. (Laughter.) The report spoke about the poverty in the Nationalist quarters of Ireland. Ire thought that gave a very strong reason why themselves. _ __ the Irish people should govern</p>
        <p>Mr. Nelson proposed, and Mr. Fox seconded, a vote of thanks to the Chairman and speakers, and this was carried amid applause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>LINES FROM LYMM.</p>
        <p>A jury of women. wanted!. This was the statement of a Councillor. It was alleged that the disinfector Did damage to clothing sent there, And the Councillor thought only a jury af Women were capable of valuing the damage. The Sanitary Inspector has had his Lynx eyes upon the Canal boats and Generally he found these satisfactory. Two loans of .t 254 and £226 have been allowed. These sums will not break the bank.</p>
        <p>Two Loans.—The Local Government Board, it was announced at Lymm Council on Thursday, had sanctioned a loan of 06254 to purchase land for an ash tip, and a loan of .6228 for land for the purpose of allotments. Both are to extend repayment over 60 years.</p>
        <p>Assistant Clerkship.—For the Assistant Clerk- ship of the Lymm District Council, Mr. Walter Timperley, Heatley, *as appointed at the meet- ing of the Council on Thursday night, out of 16 candidates.</p>
        <p>... • Diphtheria Abating.—At Lymm District Coun- cil on Thursday, Dr. de Jong reported that there were no further cases of diphtheria, and the cases in the hospital Were doing very well indeed.</p>
        <p>Octogenarian Dead. Another well-known Ly-mm octogenarian, in the person of Mr. Mairs passed away on Monday at the residence of his daughter, Mrs. Moore, Higher-lane. The funeral takes place to-day.</p>
        <p>A Tour of I nspection.—The 'Sanitary Com- mittee recommended to the Lymm District Council on Thursday night that Councillors Thurston, Davies and Gould inspect certain sewerage farms before the laying out of the Lymm Farm.—Agreed.</p>
        <p>A Series of "At Homes."—We understand that no efforts are being spared to make the series of "At homes," which are to he held in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom next Wednesday, Thursday and Friday as successful as on previous occasions. Pull particulars will be found on our front page.</p>
        <p>Members at the Council.—The members and officials present at the Lymm District Council on Thursday were: Councillor S. Davies, chair- man; Rev. G. B. Thurston, Councillors Gotobed, (deputy-chairman), Lockyer, Gould,. Warburton, Holt, Welford and Groves; Mr. W. Mullard, clerk; Dr. de Jong, Mr. J. Mort, sanitary inspec- tor; and Mr. E. I. Barnes, water engineer.</p>
        <p>Objectionable Vegetation.—At Lymm District Council on Thursday, Councillor Gotobed asked if the large deposit of vegetable matter near the Mill could not be cleared away at once, now tho water had been run off the stream. The mattei was a serious menace to health.-:-Councilloi Gould: It is disgraceful and would not be ex- pensive to remove.--The meeting decided to havc the work done at once.</p>
        <p>Canal Boat Inspection.—Mr. Mort, the Sani- tary Inspector, reported to Lymm Council on Thursday that in 1913 he had inspected 68 canal boats with registered cabin space for 225 i adults This was occupied by 91 men, 23 women, and 35 children, a total of 169. Of the children, 19 were males and 16 females; 24 were over five years and 11 under that age. There had been no case of infectious disease, and it had not been neces- sary to resort to legal proceedings. The report was approved.</p>
        <p>Primrose League Tea.—The annual tea party and soiree of the Lymm (1,141) Habitation of the Primrose League was held in the Drill Hall on Wednesday night. After partaking of tea, for which Mrs. Allman was the caterer, Mrs. Legh, the Ruling Councillor, presided, and was supported by the Hon. J. E. and Mrs. Cross, Mr. and Mrs. Callender, Mr. and Mrs. Coxon, Messrs. W. M. Beckett, T. F. W. Dixon, 11. E. Gibbon, J. R. Newton, E. Oliver, W. Thomson, T. Ridgway, and Mr. and Mrs. G. C. Hamilton. After a short address by Mrs. Legh, the Mem- ber for the Altrincham Division gave a speech on the present political situation, especially as effected by Mr. Bonar Law's Edinburgh speech. The speaker also dealt with the Conservative land policy. Mr. Humphreys contributed several songs, to the accompaniment Of Miss Esme Callender.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>JURY OF WOMEN WANTED. The Rector and the Clothes that Come</head>
        <p>JURY OF WOMEN WANTED. The Rector and the Clothes that Come</p>
        <p>Back from Disinfecting.</p>
        <p>"JUST CHUCKED IN." At Lymm Council on Thursday tl/2. letter was read from a resident, stating that after a case of diphtheria clothing had been taken to be dis- infected and were completely damaged on re- turn. The writer thought some recompense foi the damage should be given. Councillor Davies, the chairman: Is it a fact they were damaged? Mr. Mort (sanitary inspector).:-Yes.</p>
        <p>The Rev. U. B.' Thurst-on said he was calling at the house when the things were returned. They were tied, he said, in a bundle, and were just chucked into a sheet. They were all crumpled. That put the lady's back up. (Laughter.) He thought they were slightly scorched and ginged. The blankets were dis- coloured. He thought the temperature applied was too high. _</p>
        <p>Dr. de Jong: We should have sheets to wrap things in and keep them from the iron. The Rev. G. B. Thurston: They were just as if chucked together. Councillor Gotobed : We should have blankets i to prevent the articles coming in contact wit the hot iron.</p>
        <p>Dr. de J-o—ng : That is what I mean. Rev. G. B. Thurston: They were done very carelessly. I suggest Mr. AI ort visit and exam- ine the things. lad not think the damage is serious, but the complaint is more over want of courteous attention. (Laughter.) A Member: We want a jury of ladies to esti- mate the damage. (Laughter.) Dr. de Jong thought they deserved a little credit for the methods they adopted for the pre- serving the clothing sent to the disinfector from damage. He thought a great deal too much was made of these querulous complaints. Councillor Gotobed : I agree with you entirely, Doctor. The matter was left with the Sanitary I nspec. tor to investigate and report.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>WIN WICK.</head>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>Druids' Annual Smoker.—Druids of the "King George V." Lodge thoroughly enjoyed themselves on Saturday evening at the Hermit Inn, when the annual supper and smoking con- cert was held. The 32 members and friends who attended fully appreciated the excellent musical programme which was contributed to by several of their favourite artistes. Bee. C. A. Peckitt, N.G.A., presided. Messrs. J. W. Taylor and Smethurst pleased with their fin- ished rendition of "My heart's delight" and "I fear no foe." Mr. E. Smethurst sang "Mary" and "Carnival," being encored vociferously. "Ragtime Cowboy Joe" was one of Mr. Jack Woods' most pleasing numbers, and he was equally successful in "Ragtime" and "Playing the game in the West." Bro. G. E. Richards was called upon four times and gave "Grey eyes," "Annie Laurie,' "Roses," and "My amn folk," in an appreciative manner. Humorous songs were supplied by the irresistible Mr. R. A. Chandler, who sang with success "Stick to me tight," "The old grey coat," "Boiled beef and carrots," "Oh, to-morrow !" and "I shan't mind your waistcoat any more." Best whes for a speedy recovery were sent to Lord Wolmer, an honorary member of the lodge, on the proposi- tion of Bro. D. Valentine, seconded by Bro. Hodnett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—On Wednesday Mr. C. Wil- cockson gave a most interesting lecture. "The Lil' Islan'," illustrated by splendid lantern slides, all of which Were Mr. Wilcockson's own production. These slides gave an insight into the most .pretty glen and coast scenery which the Isle of Man affords.</p>
        <p>"`""'"7"---77</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Presentation to School Teacher.—Miss Gorse; who has recently resigned the office of honorary secretary to the St: Mary's SundaY Schools, Was; on Saturday evening, the recipient of a token of the high esteem in which she is held by the scholars and teachers of the schools which com- prise Great Sankey, Penketh Mission, and Church Infants' School:, The occasion was the teachers' and scholars' annual social, held in the Sankey Schools, and dancing, as well as games, for which prizes were given, were in- dulged in. After supper, the Vicar made the presentation and spoke of the untiring efforts Miss Gorse had put forth during the seven year. she was secretary. Mrs. James Ray, jun., would now take up those duties, and they were glad to be able to have Miss Gorse still amongst them as a teacher.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>PENKETII.</head>
        <p>PENKETII.</p>
        <p>Famous Sweet Peas.—Mr. Peter Davies, Daisy Bank, Greystone-road, has earned fame as a cultivator of sweet peas. A picture of these, which grew ten feet high, appears in "The Gardener," Mr. Davies says he finds autumn sowing best or early shows and spring sowing for late ones. Last season he grew 1,200 plants.</p>
        <p>Councll Seim)! Entertainers.—The entertain- ment recently given in the Penketh Wesleyan Schoolroom by the Council School-scholars was fully enjoyed by those present. Under the able management of _Miss Florence Watson the pliysi- cal exercises with the musical accompaniments were gone through with alacrity and precision Miss May Watson was the accompanist, whilst Mr. James Woodward was a capable stage mana- ger. , On Saturday evening the children enjoyed an excellent tea at the Church Infants' School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>HIGH LEG IL</head>
        <p>HIGH LEG IL</p>
        <p>A Sturdy Nonconformist's Death.—Mr. W. Cross, of Kay-lane, High Legll, passed away on Saturday morning, at the ripe age of 85 years. Mr. Cross retired from farming some years ago, and has latterly been failing rapidly in health. He was a Liberal in politics, and a sturdy Non-1 conformist, and for many years was the prin- cipal supporter of the Primitive Methodist cause at Broomedge. The funeral took place on Wednesday at Rostherne Parish Church, the officiating clergyman being the Rev. Mr. Ed- wards, of High Legh. Chief amongst the I mourners were the two daughters of the de- ceased, ad Messrs. G. W. Cross, W. Sumner, J. Platt, T. Wright, and J. Wright, of Carden (nephews) G. L. Welford, solicitor, and J. Per- _ _</p>
        <p>. .. civil, the four first-named nephews acting as bearers. The funeral arrangements were suit- ably carried through by Mr. Atkinson, of ji i gli Legh. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>The annual visit of is an event in the the The eCv r. asz .e Ef o. r itPaltet--aesnubrien.. y Guild forward to with eager n people in the district. On e ley. e preached anticipation p at syllabusnn by looked gentl m Thursday afterno n tan ?h _ iji called Nathaniel" to from the wo an appreciative congregation. n the evening Mr. Rattenbury alecture delivered to a crowded his subject. being "Christ and the audience, man. In the course of his remarks modern the lecturer spoke of the suitability of Christ to every age of the world's history. Speaking of the last century as one of intellectual struggle between the claims of religion and science, he said that the person who had intellectual doubts and difficulties was now quite out of date. The great evils of our day were self-assertion and the love of pleasure. A great danger to the church was the fact that self-expression must be gained at any cost. There must be a limit to the craze for pleasure. In our church. life we needed more heroism. We must get nearer to the ideals of the Puritans, while of course avoid- ing some of their extravagances. Alderman Bennett, who presided, commented on the love of pleasure, and endorsed the views expressed by the lecturer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>LYONS EXHIBITION. One of the Finest Ever Held on the , Costing. ' GREAT FORTHCOMING FESTIVAL.</head>
        <p>LYONS EXHIBITION. One of the Finest Ever Held on the , Costing. ' GREAT FORTHCOMING FESTIVAL.</p>
        <p>On the Ist of May, 1914, will be opened in the historic city of Lyons an international and urban exhibition, one of the finest ever held on the Continent. The exhibition, which will re- main open till November 1, will be international and urban, and will bring together in the magic city, now nearing completion in the ,capital of sunny Provence, the latest word in science and art. It will be divided into 52 sections, com- prising the usual range of subjects in the ar- rangements and treatment of which the French are unparalleled. It will be the apotheosis of industry, science and invention. The exhibition will comprise 30 magnificent palaces and buildings, situated in a splendid park at the junction of the Saone and Rhone. All the- nations of the earth are competing for the best positions at this forthcoming festival of the inventor and the scientist, and thanks to the warm friendship uniting the hands and hearts of the French and British people, the British section has been assigned the place of honour at the main entrance of the exhibition, side by side with the French Government sec- tion, where will be seen the latest masterpieces of French genius. .</p>
        <p>Special Features. Special features will be made of aviation, motor cars, textile and all kinds of machinery, potteries and cerimaques, traction and motive power, silk and lace industries, pure fc,od, beers and spirits, textiles and new inventions of all , descriptions. The magnificent gardens will be one gigantic bouquet of flowers, trees and ornamental shrubs. Some 12,000 gardeners are engaged in the indus- try of preparing masterpieces for this exhibi- tion. The exhibition is situated inside the town of Lyons, in much the same war as the White City of -London, about ten minutes by tram from the two principal railway stations, Lyou Perrache</p>
        <p>and Lyon Bellecour. Cars run every minutt from both stations, and from the principal centres of the city. There will be a fine staditni and sports ground, also a part of the ground. some ten acres, reserved as a Fun City, wheri will be found the latest novelties in amusements</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Anglo-French Trade.</head>
        <p>Anglo-French Trade.</p>
        <p>The total trade between France and the United Kingdom in 1912 was ,t 83,036,747. Ger- many, the United States of America, Italy and .Russia, not to count the .host of less importani nations, are all taking important sections and showing of their beet at the exhibition. WC must not be left behind in the race of nation for commercial supremacy. Now is the time to strike, thanks to the present peculiarly 'favour able situation, to the entente cordiale, to the love and respect, which every Frenchman and French woman has for Great Britain, her people and products; we are in advance assured of specially favoured treatment at the hands of oar cousins across the ChanneL The more Grand Prix and gold medals the British exhibits merit, the better pleased will be the French Executive Committee. Special traffic arrangements have been made for excursions regularly from all parts at Europe. A powerful committee has just been formed jointly by the Mansion House, the British Chant ber of Commerce and the French Chamber Of Commerce, under the chairmanship of the Right Hon. the Lord Mayor of London. An official delegate of this committee has been appointed who is at the Same' time official representative of the Executive Committee of the Exhibition, and to whom all communications and applior tions for space should be addressed, at the cool'</p>
        <p>mittee offices, London Chamber of Comma Oxford-court, Cannon-street, London, E.C.</p>
        <p>The man who was found .drowned in the rice' Mersey at Chorlton-cum-Hardy has been identl. fled as Peter Dwyer (56), labourer, of Lupins. court, Edgeley, Stockport. At the inquest a rev diet of "Found drowned" was returned.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the closing of the infai schools of Burnley for six weeks at Christm to combat walk measles and scarlet fever, the has been such a recurrence that it is likely tj schools will have to be again closed for a peri°</p>
        <p>" Alcohol as a racial poison" is the topic, pith which Dr. C. W. Salechy h; to deal with in man- chester to-day at the fiftieth annual meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Elope and Temperance Union.</p>
        <p>WRITING to the agent en- trusted with the purchasing of the stores for the-forthcoming Imperial Trans-Antarctic Expedi- tion, Sir Ernest Shackleton uses these words :</p>
        <p>"I consider the ques- tion of the concen- trated beef supply is most important--</p>
      </div>
