      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>BY Way of Contrast. At the Warrington Borough Court last week a Sowerby Bridge man was convicted of "loiter- ing with intent." I was reminded that it was at Sowerby Bridge—a small Yorkshire town , Some half-dozen miles from the East Lancashire border—that I reported Stanley's lecture on " now I found Livingstone," shortly after his return from that successful expedition. The enterprising committee of a local Working Men's Club had ventured upon the speculation, the expenses in connection with which amounted to wellnigh .4;50, and the event at- tracted enthusiastic admirers from all the region round about. The promoters evidently expected the famous explorer to "sort o' chum NP," and I often smile as I recalr how disap- Pointed they were to find Stanley so "stiff aucl stand-offish."</p>
        <p>Dr. Mellor. The Rev. Dr. Stanley Mellor, who is already to leave Warrington under circumstances set forth in last week's "Examiner," will be at least the second minister of the same surname and title to adorn an important Liverpool pul- i Pit. I suppose it to be some 50 years ago since the late Rev. Dr. Enoch Mellor, the great Con- tiregationalist leader, took rank among the foremost preachers in that city. He was one a." hvo ministers of that denomination whom I Have personally known as returning to former Pastorates. That was Dr. Mellor's experience at Halifax, where for many Years before and after his sojourn in Liverpool lie was pastor of Square Church, and, together with other ineas citizens, included among his members ;fle Brothers Crossley, the great carpet manu- . lecturers and philanthropists. Possibly Dr. Mellor may in like manner come back to Werrington one of these days.</p>
        <p>EsPerantists in Council and at Play. Last Saturday I had the advantage'of attend- at the quarterly conference of the Federa- tion of Esperantists of Lancashire and Cheshire " at Accrington. The afternoon session was held Qt the Town Hall, and the visitors were cordi- allY welcomed by the Mayor (Captain J. Hai- :wood, J.P.), who, besides .spending some years in India, has travelled a good deal, and enter- tained us with appropriate tales of his adven- tures. Among the Liverpool delegates were a German and his Polish wife—a charming pair. afternoon was devoted to serious business, but in the evening there was a diverting mis- cellaneous programme, comprising songs, short addresses, and a laughable little comedy. My Point is that the proceedings throughout were exclusively in Esperanto, and that the, entire zet)alPanY, including several quite humble work- lag folk, intelligently followed and appreciated all that was done for their instruction and amusement.</p>
        <p>"Not If He Could Arrange It." Last week's tit-bit respecting the husband's retort to Mrs. Henpeck's remark about going to Heaven brought to my mind .an incident which I met with in the train some years ago. My row-passengers were a Lancashire cotton nufacturer and a Canadian traveller. The compared notes freely, and the time passed Y agreeably indeed. When, having reached destination, the millowner alighted, he ex- ssed his pleasure at haVing encountered so 1-informed and delightful a companion, dring "I. trust we may meet again." "Not ', 82e You first," promptly exclaimed thi trirY stranger, were in a joke in favour o; hi 3 countrymen. But the Lancashire f was not familiar with that style o Raspealb• Re took it very seriously. -First ho who (1118 astonishment, and hesitated as one e opened -neted theidce of his senses; then and stal ji• even Protest niouth iasf to express indignanth ; thn he apparently e changed his mind, ed off in high dudgeon. k</p>
        <p>;nlie Very nett-, pa.ragraph ,to the re which Ra)ired the foregoing recollection was of one, 'lad a habit of feigning deafness on occa- ol% 11l the old School Board days I knewa tii;tal.'3llaati who was supposed to be very deaf, a time came when he was elected chairman. e were to each • otl; wont to talk quite frankly . ed • e,r. and I told him privately I was surprised acceptance of the responsible position, en" was so hard of hearing. "But, my dear (rw,” said he, with a twinkle in his eye, ~rtlay not deafness be here rather an advantage an a handicap? As you know, there are diasSetY. things said now and again during our u' ")05, and you may be sure I shall hear 'of these." Nor did lie.. I heard most l'eensive and provoking epithets hurled at his r ?ted head, and lie invariably received them ;tn. .a smile that was childlike and bland, as qUB oblivious of what was going on; but in r,i°llB debate, amid normal conditions, he _`11:411 missed a material point. Thus there fe convenient kinds as well as various degrees deafness. There was that of the old lady ,naodev,ent bargain-hunting; and who always a- practice of pressing for reduction of the price of an article. "How much?" e once asked concerning certain small oPery goods. "Fi'pence," replied the shop- "sistant." "Fifteen pence," said the customer, 11 give you thirteen." "L., said fivepence, adatu," corrected the salesman. "Oh, five_ .1loo," went on the incorrigible one i "I'll give on threepence !"</p>
        <p>Tinel.. . Y and Doubly I -- welcome. opening the new Star Kinema House last t;eek.the Mayor of Warrington gaveautalioar fan itii- "e assurancfOthat the pictures would b Mucational character, and such as parents in the comic- eti.aild take their children to :41 that they would receive isieetruction so as ,11-aesltl aelosement. Most obviously, when. 11111!,enterprise gets into the field of morals, some: a.,iing is bound to happen one way or.the other; h.'al it has 1 —lllll I )een "s ' • instance, Er aith ways., observed that so Ifaareiatdisinhmapy- tdleitriandows and 4 ..B,,,Paper how one Charles Jtteiddsio)nictaairtenseiSisae,dc at ii":eide" scene in an anima tile ilrlrk• Then he went home and copied .aansdfaoulai found in 111°isil°144tilng a°lfortnbieng hispicture dead i'alr)aoidaye,l4 ~, Vas-filled room- tjt:erl he h own on the ad copied to What te was st di:km.B444/1g of the cracks of the detail, even to inf,-11; I presume that to be the sort Mayor, —llthillY deprecated by our worthy,. with his ' 4 a . sympathise v.l .. Ivor , .great many will As the w rt of thing b B°lP in this. Ido most strongly. ~,, ..n ,y cOef -_ eases ni 8° (a-lie!) beautifully and touch' gi e-, it °ving picture shows remind us, 1,148 they flash their scenes of crime, —lat we'd hate to leave behind us N Imprints on the films of time." cilaildsar: there are films offered that unfole, d agriculture, aeronautics, animal life, tiaaeteriology, biography, biology, botany,ie."° tin- jaP. ethnology, fisheries, geography, 118lory,. r4'lustrial, kindergarten studies, mining an ltallUrgy, microscopy, military, naval, eaht'ral history, ornithology, pathology, Pi.sli".. co"re (note this, ye angleis !), • railroad, tell . rejoll? scenic, travel, and zoology. I sincere tli,, lee.in the prospect of a more frequent use of ark 'n Warrington. I quite recognise that lilit Ina niust continue largely to hold the field; I 44A know that for myself two of the most ait7able films I ever witnessed were illus