      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <p>wr</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 31, 1914.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORTHIES.</p>
        <p>PETER RYLANDS.-11.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Some Local Agitations Recalled: A Famous Election.</head>
        <p>Some Local Agitations Recalled: A Famous Election.</p>
        <p>BY ARTHU</p>
        <p>The literary efforts of Mr. 313-lands, which we have briefly described, appear to have been his last. His opinions, so courageously formed and expressed, were luminous sparks from a brain that had many interests,, and though literary pursuits may have had some attraction for him, they were speedily to give place to interests that were more public and insistent. From early life he was a politician. At the age of 21 he was a familiar figure upon the plat- form, and an asset of the Reform Party in their attempt to oust Mr: Blackburn, the Protectionist member. As a member of the Anti-Corn Law League, he delivered many speeches between the years 1841 and 1848, and was present as a Warrington representative at the four days' con- ference of Free Traders held in the Free Trade Hall at Manchester just 71 years ago. In his native town he made himself felt, and his youth did not prevent him from sharing in the movement for the Incorporation of the Borough. When this object was achieved in 1848 he became at once -a 'member of the Town Council, and speedily rose to the rank of Alderman. Three years later he was appointed a Justice of the Peace for the Borough, an&amp; for the County of Lancaster. Two years more and he was elected Mayor. Five years of service had given him opportunity to display his powers of judgment and argu- ment, and the vote of his colleagues was .unani-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>MOUS.</head>
        <p>MOUS.</p>
        <p>THE WAY OF THE REFORMER IS HARD. Though a year of progress in municipal mat- ters, it can hardly have been a happy year for Mr. Rylands. Sohemes. of general improvement were pushed forward, and Mr. Bylands was able to introduce a Warrington Improvement and Market Act" Which was passed by Parlia-</p>
        <p>Photograph by Birtles. THE LATE PETEIt RYLANDS. [ln our last week's issue we gave, in erior, the Portrait of the late Mr. 'l'. Glazebrook ltylands Mr. Peter Rylands's brother.] _</p>
        <p>me, and which gave Kinction for the building of the present Market Hall; but upon ,other sUbjebts.controversy raked long and bitterly. lin3 worst difficulty arose out of a proposal to inc,rease the Police Force. In 1851 the police staff of the town numbered ten—a chief con- stable, an inspector, and eight constables. As a magistrate, Mr. liylands knew that they were inadequate to the needs ,of the town. Assaults and robberies were committed with impunity, and the magistrates had often made complaints to the Watch 'Committee. It was a state of things which created uneasiness, and many persons and firms felt obliged to employ private watchmen.</p>
        <p>WHEN WARRINGTON MANAGED WITH , SEVEN POLICEMEN. As Chairman of the .Watch Committee, Mr. Rylands put forward a proposal-to add two con- stables to the police staff. .It was , not an extravagant proposal, but the Mayor had Sue- . ceeded in calling into being a body of determined: and, perhaps, jealous opponents: These took popular line in pleading for economy, and managed to defeat his proposal by 12 votes. to 3. Confident of popular backing, they went to the further length of refusing to fill up a vacancy, and thus reduced the Force froni 10 to 9. The struggle owe begun was to last for twelve years. Mr. ylands, a year later, resigned his seat upon the Watch Committee, refusing to accept re- sPonsibility for the order of a town so inade- quately policed, Whereupon his opponents reduced the Force to seven. Long and animated discussions in the Council followed, and Mr. ll .3' ands, .in the end, succeeded in getting the al Gs emde rtnhnr tozgth glr'aunr tliatonZt•dsin natusmber back to ten. , The Police Bill, which t11815e5; saunpdpooiffte r col police, gave him another opportunity of opening the subject, but he was again defeated. . Not until 1864, after twelve y defeats, was . victory gaeli'nr!3.l 0f agitations Watch - Committee, and the Police Force .in- creased to 29 men.</p>
        <p>--, ,„' ;,,,,,',;., ' , '',,'.- • ‘,,,..,‘,--,-,,- -,-- 4: ":',4---,4,,,.%-iffil,,,,; :1,#%;9;1!,.04:-;,,;':,'!!-, ,": -,s"'•--; ' It/itt."',-"--...'."",--- ";-'1;:(4,/,44-•,-...,, " : ~, . kir e, ,Ae.:', ;4-:A.,;,::-''.:l°,- .. -,t4•'''',tt-.1,13* ,- ' ",- i „ , 4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>STORM IN A TEAPOT.</head>
        <p>A STORM IN A TEAPOT.</p>
        <p>Another controversy that disturbed the year of his Mayoralty was what was known as the Footpaths' Question. This Was of 'so simple a character that it is now difficult to believe that a town could be seriously agitated over such a matter. It arose out of the making of the Stock- Port. and Widnesjiailway, which cut footpaths along which the public had a right of way, par- ticularly a favourite one that led to Arpley Meadows. The company offered to make a. riverside road under the railway and to make a level crossing at the same spot. A body of agitators formed a Footpaths' Protection Society and presented a demand that a footbridge should be made at the spot where the 'path had been disturbed. An excited public meeting was held, at which Mr. Rylands, as Mayor, presided. Mr.. Wihiam Beamont and Mr. J. G. MaeMinnes were howled down, and by an almost unanimous vote the bridge was demanded. One cannot help the belief that personal feeling lay behind the agita- tion. The bridge was built. It proved ugly and was little used, and -in a few years was quietly removed, without a word of public pro- test. ' - - - - • • -</p>
        <p>Clearly Peter Rylands was sharing the lot of every eager and not over-cautious reformer. lie ref used to offer himself for re-election as Mayor, and when proposed in 1855 was defeated by Mr. Joseph Chrimes. Two bitterly hostile parties now filled the Council. Mr. Chrirues, as a Tory, had Tory support. Mr. Rylands, as a Radical and Pro- gressive, led another section. The columns of the local Press sparkled .with sarcastic and pointed letters, and unprecedented public inter- est was aroused in the doings of the 'Council. The most damaging of these letters appeared over the signature "Oliver West." Cleverly written and avoiding personalities, they never- theless levelled pitiless satire against the men who put party first and seemed to be prompted by personal ambition or pride. In their scorn of little-minded -men they were merciless. The victims vowed vengeance and threatened actions for libel. Two leading men were publicly charged with th? authorship, but the secret was</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>• • ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>MOUNFIELD.</head>
        <p>MOUNFIELD.</p>
        <p>kept. Suspicion, however, was well founded, and as all the persons concerned have passed from our midst, it may now be recorded that the "Oliver West" letters were the joint labour of Peter Rylands, Ralph Allen Mould, and John Gordon MacMinnies.</p>
        <p>THE MARKET HALL: AWAKENING WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The laying of the foUndation-stone of the Market Hall in October, 1855, must have been a day of gratification for Mr. Rylands. Most readers will know that until the building of the Hall Warrington had no covered market-place. Buyers and sellers met in the open Square upon which the Barley Mow looked down, and which was adorned by a central lamp and some raise‘i fish stones.. A proposal to build a Market Hall had been made in 1852, but had been withdrawn in consequence of the opposition it had received. It fell to Mr. Rylands in the year of his Mayor- alty to obtain an Act of Parliament giving sanc- tion to this-and other imiftovements, and it was under his chairmanship that a committee of the Council planned the Market Hall and Shed which we have with us to the present time. A yzar was spent in building operations, and on October 15th, 1856, the Market was opened. There were other evidences that Warrington was in the process of awakening. The Museum and Library was built, the first in England to be supported from the rates. A Public Hall was built in a street appropriately named after Peter Rylands, its author and originator; and a Proposal for a Cemetery was taking shape. To these projects Mr. Rylands gave his voice, and to the discussions he brought sound and pene- trating judgment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>FAMOUS ELECTION.</head>
        <p>A FAMOUS ELECTION.</p>
        <p>. _ The most stirring event in NT. Pylands' War- rington career, and the one which has lingered long?* in the public memory, vs the famous election of 1868.</p>
        <p>Politically, Warrington . was Tory. • Mr. Gil- bert Greenall had represented the borough from 1847, and his position was scarcely challenged. In 1859 Mr. Rylands was asked to oppose him, but business pressure made the suggestion im- practicable. When the General Election of 1868 cable into view the request was renewed and Peter stepped into the arena. - M. Greenall had held the scat. for 21 years, and the intrusion of Mr. Rylands was bitterly resented. Anger soon changed to fear, for Mr. Greenall could not come within 'measurable dis- tance of his opponent, either in argument or eloquence. The two questions before the country were the Ballot and Irish Disestablish- . ment. A debater to the finger tips and a most attractive speaker withal, Mr. Rylands drew crowded audiences and sent them away enthu- siastic. Unable to Answer his speeches, his enemies launched out upon a disgraceful cam- paign of calumny. The fact that he had ceased to be a Dissenter And become a Churchman was held up to discredit, contributions to local charities were quoted and compared with those of rich men, and a series of wretched letters in the "Guardian," signed "Benjamin Darby," narrated everything in his career that could be made to bear an odious interpretation. As the election day approached feeling in- creased in bitterness. Thoroughly alarmed at the progress made by their opponents, the Tories posted the town with placards, accusing him of anarchy, plotting and treason. Probably never before nor since haS feeling run so high in Warrington. •</p>
        <p>" BOTTLED " VOTERS. - On the nomination day order was maintained only with difficulty, and when the election day dawned the excitement was beyond description. The poll closed at four o'clock. About a quarter before this hour matters looked ugly indeed, and reached a crisis. A number of working men Liberals had not appeared at the poll, and a rumour was circulated that they had been " bottled," or, in more classic language, made drunk and locked up by the Tories. A body of Liberals went to the place indicated, but were checked at the gate by band of roughs armed with sticks and bludgeons. There was a scuffle, and three of the attackers were badly hurt, but Without gaining entrance to the yard. At the close of the poll nobody had any doubt that Mr. Greenall was elected. Under the sys• tern of open voting both parties could keep a record, and progress could be ascertained hour by hour. In this instance, though there was a Wide discrepancy in the figures, everybody agreed that it was a Tory victory. The Liberal figures showed a majority of seven for Mr. Greenall, and the Tory clerk gave the more cheerful report of a majority of 78. Nobody waited for an official announcement. The Tories spent the night in frantic joy, and the Whigs made the best attempt they could to find solace in their homes.</p>
        <p>Next morning the town found its ears ting- ling. The Mayor, Mr. Nield, announced that according to his official polling book Mr. llylands had received 1,984 votes and Mr. Greenall 1,957. Mr: Ryland* was declared the victor by a majority of 27 votes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>AN EXTRAORDINARY POSITION.</head>
        <p>AN EXTRAORDINARY POSITION.</p>
        <p>Following upon a contest marked by so much party feeling, the effect was extraordinary, and not for some time did the town or its business get back to its normal condition.</p>
        <p>The circumstances out of which this extra- ordinary situation arose were described not long Robert Pierpoint. Some of Mr. Greenall's sup- ago in the pages of "The Examiner" by 311.. porters, seventy or eighty in number, had crowded into the polling room and banded in their voting cards. But these were not handed to the official polling clerk. They were given to the check clerk of Mr. Greenall. In the con- fusion of a busy period he placed these in his pocket, and failed to hand them to the Mayor's -etiatvetimieeynthiVedvotteedi; representative. The voters left under the im- 11)eraessosinolui votess f Tirle tnothe officially entered at all. The Mayor held that he could only accept such votes as were found in his own official record, and though a fierce storm of anger was aroused he held to his view.</p>
        <p>Failing to impress the Mayor, Mr. Greenall's friends lodged a petition against Mr. Rylands, and Mr. Baron Martin was appointed to try the case. Again, after a few weeks, passion flamed afresh at the coming of the judge, and excite- ment •reigned..</p>
        <p>From t'lie point of view of the jtidge the case presented no difficulty. He ruled that the handing of a card to a party clerk was not the legal tender of a vote, that to become a vote it must be recorded in the official register, and that the votes claimed for Mr. Greenall were not votes at all.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rylands was taunted in the House of Commons and in the Tory Press as the .repre- sentative of the minority of Warrington. His retort was that about 200 of hie supporters had been tampered with and prevented from voting, that under fair conditions he would have won, and he considered himself, morally, as well as legally, the Member for Warrington.</p>
        <p>(To be cOntinued.)</p>
        <p>The body of a married woman named Ellen Vaughan, aged 65, of Doulion-street, St. Helens, was found in the lake in Taylor Park.</p>
        <p>Charged at Hull on Saturday with causing serious injury to another lad by throwing cor- rosive fluid over his face, Edward Renshaw, aged 14, was remanded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>TABLE TALK.</head>
        <p>TABLE TALK.</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute of Interesting Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSING.</p>
        <p>Children at Woodridings School, Hatch End, Pinner, said the principal at the annual prize- giving, were being taught to write with either hand, and in almost all cases the writing done by the left hand was as good as that done by the right.</p>
        <p>A Collecting Cat. A cat has been added to the list of Hospital Saturday Fund collectors, and has gathered in £1 us. 3d. " Fluffy " belongs to Mr. Kerry, of Penge, and ever since his kittenhood has been taught to collect pence from customers. At the call of his master " Fluffy" jumps upon the counter and puts his head through a hole in a card, purposely made and painted, requesting the public to spare* a penny for the fund.</p>
        <p>.11 Football Family. If there is a sporting family comparable with that of the Charlesworths, of Scunthorpe, in Lincolnshire, we have yet to hear of it. Eleven brothers, whose ages vary from forty-three to eighteen, are with one exception all experienced footballers, and they desire to challenge any other team of brothers to a series of two matches. The only condition is that their oppo- nents shall be " genuine teams of brothers." Such a match, if it comes* off, should be worth seeing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Motor Razor.</head>
        <p>A Motor Razor.</p>
        <p>A safety razoi, connected to a motor by means of a flexible shaft, is the latest invention to simplify shaving (says the "Electrical World"). The blades are actuated in such a way as to cut the beard by impact or Mows. The blades, therefore, do not need to be so sharp as in hand- shaving. No soap is necessary, wetting the skin being sufficient, mid a clean shave can be accom- plished, it is declared, in a very short time. The after-effects are described as being those following a mild massage. A plug is provided for attaching the razor to any lamp socket.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Channel Tunnel Cost.</head>
        <p>Channel Tunnel Cost.</p>
        <p>Trains could be run-n.i;;tl;;OUgli the Chan- nel Tunnel by about Christmas, 1920," said Mr. Arthur Fell m. a lecture at the Royal Society of Arts. ",Engmeers now consider that the tun- nels could be sompleted at a cost estimated at £16,000,000, to be raised over about six or eight years, one-half to be found by England, one-half by France. The £8,000,000 to be raised by Eng- land would probably be in £4,000,000 of deben- tures and £4,000,000 of shares. If the same fares which are now charged by the steamers were charged by the tunnel company," he added, "the shares shauld pay very large dividends."</p>
        <p>Barber's Shop in a Church.</p>
        <p>. _ The Rev. Carl Reiland, rector of the fashion- able St. George's Episcopal Church, New York, established a barber's shop for the choir. In explanation of the new feature Mr. Reiland said, "For the sake of uniformity in the ap- pearance of the choir boys on Sundays, and as a slight evidence of parochial appreciation, we have assigned ft special barber to the boys on Saturday mornings and Sundays before the ser- vices. Haircuts and shaves are given free on those days in the vestry, by an ex-barber, who is a member of the congregation, and I have found that the results have justified my experi_ ment."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Unique Arrest.</head>
        <p>A Unique Arrest.</p>
        <p>_ . Some years ago there departed this life a very popular retired police officer, who told me, a writer in the," Chronicle" says, low, under de- cidedly excusable circumstances, 'he had drunk too deeply of the flowing bowl, and in that con- dition had to take charge of a couple of pris- oners—man and woman—and convoy them to Holloway from a country district. His pris- oners knew him well—he was a very popular man—and gave him their support in the literal sense when he appealed to them. But for their taking charge of him he would have been unable to reach the prison with them ! By such pleas- ant touches of humanity are the annals of crime beautified.</p>
        <p>" Inverted Skyscrapers p,</p>
        <p>would fix a limit - The 800-feet high building which is to be erected in New York for the Pan-American aoru Union will surely be the limit in skyscrapers. IN' lei levr l York e y arvke sbteaeti ei rLuemg iostlira tu f e late years that the to the height of these monster structures, and that architects and engineers are already con- sidering the possibilities of the "inverted sky- scraper," which is to be built downwards in- stead of upwards. The boundaries of New York are so fixed as to prevent the naturar growth of the city, and it can onlynow. expand by climb- ing skywards or burrowing into the bowels of the earth, and any day we may hear of the first "inverted skyscraper" being carried fifty or sixty storeys underground.</p>
        <p>telhice:litalri for Derby- issued by sphio. Sunless Cinemas. The danger to health arising from the fact that consumptives are allowed to attend cinema )el NI cs iasle (01 officer rao wise. " There is," be says, "a .mil'saramr,eisee,mmi much more serious aspect than any moral damage in the harm done to physical health by Sitting for hours at a time in a close, dark, Stuffy atmos- phere, in a room which never receives the puri- fying rays of the sun, and never gets the fresh air sweeping through it. Properly regulated, the picture palace might be made to serve a use- ful purpose, but considering that the theatre must exclude all rays of the sun, special rules for their design and management should be made. Whatever is done the cinema theatre cannot be made into a hygienic :building."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Black Cap.</head>
        <p>The Black Cap.</p>
        <p>Discussing the action of Mr. Justice Ridley ei c!s recently in passing death sentence at the Old Bailey without ssuming . the black cap, the l)ray assigned to veil when it was re- " Law Journal" says 0! Judge Was well justi- fieda on this occasion in omitting the grim formality. The black cap owes its origin to the Order of the Coif, and according to one his- ti ohiei acioi , i fis o, t the the o v onlyerig occasionquired to be hidden. By the ancient privilege of the sergeants the coif was not to be taken oft ci ii even in the Royal presence. It was to be dis- played when sitting on the Bench or pleading at the Bar, but this rule seems always to have been departed from in passing sentence of death. The head of the administrator of justice was then covered or veiled as a token of sorrow by the black 'sentence cap.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Story of the Boer War.</head>
        <p>A Story of the Boer War.</p>
        <p>Brigadier-Genera•l J. E. Gough, in a lecture at the London University, on Counter Attacks," emphasised thee value of suds d example suddenness eaxiaidmfiee in British in a counter-attack, andLliceoduante danger of alemks, ah- Binrgitiistitl historyfar. o life aqtio successfulte d, a charge at Waggon Hill in the Boer War. For about ten hours the opposing sides were en- trenched within a distance of from fifteen to fifty yards of each other, and fierce firing took place. No successful charge could be made, for only a small party could hear the officer give the order, and in every ease they were shot down as soon as they got to their feet. It was facing certain death. But 230 men of the Devon Regi- ment came up unobserved by the enemy, and were deployed quietly. Everything was ex- plained to them, and when the order was given they suddenly topped the rise, raced across the few score yards, and the Boers vanished.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>WORLD OF LETTERS.</head>
        <p>THE WORLD OF LETTERS.</p>
        <p>found judgment ,and are governed by a high moral principle. A spirit "finely touched" to " fine issues."</p>
        <p>ILLUMINATING ANALYSES.</p>
        <p>Concluding his cominents on the state of society depicted in the play, and the thoughts on death and the fear of it, as imaged for us severally by the Duke, Isabella and Claudio, Mr. Brooke says: "These are grave subjects, and though they awaken our thought, they do not stir us to delight. Nor does the picture of a base society give us 'pleasure. Both have no charm, nor have either the stern loveliness they might have had if the play had been a tragedy. There is but one thing in the whole play which has romantic beauty. It is the image of the moated Grange, where Mariana mourns, and the song which her attendant sings." The great tragedy, "Othello," is analysed, and the principal characters in it interpreted for us, in a masterly manner, with conspicuous originality and most illuminating effect. Dis- cussing the state of mind in which Shakespeare wrote the play, Mr. Brooke says: "Everywhere in this play we seem to feel, in the writer's soul, his dark consciousness of the inexplicable aspect of the world, of the answerless problem of evil and sorrow, of his doubt, with the iismay that companied it, whether there wits any intelligent Good-will behind the life of man, or whether all that happened was by soulless Chance or by ruthless Pate. Fate dominates Macbeth, but here in Othello, Chance or Unreason, blind and deaf, is at the centre of human life. We may, in the end, bend before Fate, because it moves by Law; but nothing will ever induce us to be otherwise than in angry rebellion against un- reasoning Chance. Something of such question- ing, and the temper which arises from it, seems to have been in Shakespeare's mind when he wrote 'Othello.' The conception ,of the play, the movement of it, the events in it, the bring- ing about of the catastrophe, are all apparently in the realm of Chance. There is a shocking un- reasonableness about them, which is all the more curicus when we consider that the con- struction of the play, the linking and the sequence of the scenes, is so eminently clear, RO closely ordered by the imaginative reason and the logic of passion. This is a wondefful com- bination."</p>
        <p>Mr. Brooke's lecture on "King Lear" is, perhaps, the weightiest in the book, but we can 410 no more than convey an assurance to our readers that a study of it will enable them the better to understand this most complex play, and will also put in their possession ideas on morals and the philosophy off life which spring from a mind imbued with deep religious senti- ment.</p>
        <p>The remaining studies have Rs their subjects the historical plays " King John," " Henry IV." part 1 and part 2, and "Henry V.," and contain some very fine writing on real patriotism. A.P.</p>
        <p>___• ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>New Books.</head>
        <p>New Books.</p>
        <p>INTERPRETING SHAKESPEARE.</p>
        <p>Ten More Plays of Shakespeare. Br Stopford A. Brooke. London: Constable and Co., Ltd. 7s. 6d. net.</p>
        <p>A rich treasury of inspiring thought has been opened to the Shakespearean student in this latest addition to the literature which grows round the works of our greatest poet; and young, ardent spirits, who are ambitious to , achieve fame by impersonating his characters on the stage, especially, should be grateful for the opportunity afforded them, by the publica- tion of these lectures, to acquire clear ideas on the interpretation of most of the characters of importance in the ten plays dealt with. Mr. Stopford Brooke brings to his task not only a love of his subject and an ample knowledge of literature in general, but an intimate acquaint- once with the language • Shakespeare, Shake- spearean modes of expression, and all that makes up the style of a poet who was always forcible and often sublime, but who indulged now and then in Italian " conceits," was occasionally extravagant and sometimes trivial. Mr. Brooke is bold in his criticism and does not shrink from condemning the faultiness of conception in some of Shakespeare's characters, which, is he points out, almost invariably causes the com- position Of whole scenes to be wrongly or inade- quately done. His observations on the church scene in " Much Ado About Nothing," in this connection, are 'most pertinent and instructive. The conduct of Claudio and Don Pedro, he tells us, in publicly denouncing Hero (whatever grounds they may have had for belief in the accusation) was indefensible; the Prince being • her father's guest, he having been received with honour and rich entertainment by Leonato, who is governor of the city, worthy of character and rank. Yet Don Pedro allows his young favour- ite, Claudio, to stain with nameless shame, before the whole town, the daughter of his host: Mr. Brooke thinks this ugliness of conduct of the two men, and their unnatural -discourtesy, must have got .on Shakespeare's literary nerves and caused him in this scene not to write so well as tisual. The immediate acceptance by Leonato of the truth of the accusation made against his daughter, is also, Mr. Brooke thinks, altogether apart from nature, and all Leonato says about it foolish or violent. Leonato seems indeed to pass in a moment from a sensible man into senile weakness. But this, as Mr. Brooke shrewdly remarks, is more Shakespeare's fault than Leonato's, the conception of the entire scene being ill-shaped and the execution of it ill-done.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brooke, in discussing the characters of Dogberry and Verges, refers to the custom of representing the latter. character, on the stage, as a very old man, and Dogberry as middle-aged, and declares that if he were staging the play he would make them of the same age. The can. trast would then be not of age and middle-age, hut of character and character—the difference between the two men, both of the same type. We are quite in agreement with Mr. Brooke in this, presuming he means that they should both be represented as advanced in years though net very old men. It would thus accord with the innate vanity of Dogberry to pose as the superior in every we."' of " neighbour Verges," of whom he speaks, condescendingly (tl) Leonato) as "a good old man, sir; he will be talking, as they saw, ' When the age is in, the wit is out.' " Verges himself tacitly admits to being an old mall, when he says, "I am as honest as any man living that is an old man and no honester than I."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>. CHARMING CREATIONS.</head>
        <p>. CHARMING CREATIONS.</p>
        <p>Of Benedick and Beatrice, Mr. Brooke dis- courses in the most delightfully engaging terms. Nothing that we have met in a wide reading of Shakespearean criticism excels in beaut 7 of lan- guage or in intellectual aPpreeiation of the de- sign of the poet,•=the masterly delineations he gives of these charming creations. Interest in this particular lecture is heightened in War- rington owing to the selection by the Amateur Shakespearean Players of "Much Ado About Nothing" for the commemoration performance this year. We most heartily commend Mr. Brooke's book to all who are taking part in this production. . •</p>
        <p>• The other plays dealt with in this volume are " Twelfth Night," "Julius CEesar," "Hamlet," " Measure for Measure," "Othello," "King Lear," "King John," "Henry IV." Part i. "Henry IV." part 2, and "Henry V." It would have been a pleasure to us to have given otir readers examples of Mr. Brooke's thought on each of the plays, but our limited space will not permit of this. We therefore confine our- selves to noticing one or two prominent features.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>HAMLET'S "ANTIC DISPOSITION."</head>
        <p>HAMLET'S "ANTIC DISPOSITION."</p>
        <p>In his analysis of Hamlet's character, Mr. Brooke pours scorn on those critics who have been in ,no doubt as to the madness or half. madness of the young Prince—they never having asked themselves whether Shakespeare meant him to be mad, or why Horatio never thought him mad or even on the verge of madness; or whether a madman can be so sequent as he is in all he says, or so continuously intelligent along with a weakened brain.</p>
        <p>"The fact is," says Mr. Brooke, "that Shake- speare never intended to represent Hamlet as mad or half-mad or verging on madness. He expressly made him a feigner of madness, and when he wished to represent real madness and to contrast it with feigned madness, he created the real madness of Ophelia, and (lid it with wonderful truth and skill." After all, the main question with regard to the matter is, not whether Hamlet was mad or half-mad or not mad at all, but whether Shakespeare meant him to be mad, and to that there is but one answer possible." . • •</p>
        <p>"For my own part those passages of thought which are used to prove that he was mad, con- tain the wisest and deepest things be says; and those actions of his which are used to prove the same theory obey a higher law of reason than any mere logician has ever conceived." And, again, Mr. Brooke, in referring to the staging of Shakespeare's plays, remarks:</p>
        <p>"When we read Hamlet's advice to the Players, we wish that all the critics of the Drama, and all the acting-managers were (if he was mad, or half-mad) quite as mad as he. We also, in reading it, understand what Shakespeare was as an acting-manager. We hear his per- sonal experience." There is a passage in Mr. Brooke's lecture on Hamlet" with which we cannot agree. Re- ferring to his treatment of his mother in the closet scene, Mr. Brooke says: "his mind is grave, not furious; be does not suspect her of any knowledge of his father's murder. He is only indignant with her marriage with the mur- derer." •</p>
        <p>We cannot reconcile this interpretation with Hamlet's retort to his mother (upon her ex- claiming at the killing of Polonius—" 0! what a rash and bloody deed is this"):</p>
        <p>'A bloody deed! Almost as bad, good mother,</p>
        <p>As kill av, king, and marry with his brother." These words, to our understanding, imply suspicion of complicity in the murder. The Queen repeats Hamlet's words, "As kill a king?" and he replies "Ay, lady, 'twos my word."</p>
        <p>Mr. Brooke's reflections upon Shakespeare's choice of a subject for his play "Measure for Measure," and his criticisms on the treatment of it, are worthy of close study. -They show pro.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Literary Oleanings.</head>
        <p>Literary Oleanings.</p>
        <p>PROBATIONERS WANTED.</p>
        <p>But what has become of the type that used to apply? Where are the bright, melt 4xia,,ated, refined, and thoroughly domesticated giz Is, daughters of sensible, middle-class, British mothers and fathers, the girls who tan turn their hand to anything, and are not afraid of hard work, who are capable, unspoiled, and who have been brought up to help themselves and others as a matter of course? These come to hospital knowing how to make a bed and sweep a room, how to cook a dinner or make a frock, help the boys with their home-work, or bath the baby. They need not be exasperatingly clever. Such knowledge as is represented by passing the examinations of the College of Pre- ceptors is quite -sufficient, especially if aided and abetted by the atmosphere of a cultured home and a love of general reading. The conditions of life in a modern hospital ought to be just what would appeal to girls like these. There they find ample scope for their energies, clean and wholesome surroundings, in- teresting work, with the human element strong in it. They are among congenial companions, they are enjoying an independent useful life,with plenty of pleasant interludes of recreation, , study, holidays, as well as the interest of the work itself. It is a life that enlarges their mental powers wonderfully, makes their sym- pathies deeper and more catholic, gives them that wherewith they can always earn their liv- ing, and, moreover, brings them into contact with the deep, solemn, and absorbing things of life in a way that no other occupation can do. And it is this type of girl who will get the most out of hospital work. There is a wider field than ever for the young, bright, educated, sympathetic woman of to-day in our hospitals, and never was she more badly needed. With- out such, there are signs that decadence is near, that the profession is being cheapened, that the spirit of the market-place, of the house of busi- ness, is creeping into our midst to cloud our minds and paralyse Our energies. This would be a great pity.—From the " Girl's Own Paper."</p>
        <p>• THE WRESTLING MATCH. "Hell fell him, will Kit! He's mair lish nor Boom'la!"</p>
        <p>"Ay, but Bootela'slitten t'weight. Ole him time au' he'll be master."</p>
        <p>" Naw, he'll not. Kit'll git in just now. He's a good tak'er-hod an' all is Kit."</p>
        <p>What's ta bet on't, then?" "Nay, I'll nit bet. But Kit's t'better man, for a that." Two or three times the combatants parted and renewed their preliminary grip before getting hold, and the excitement was intense when at length came the sudden tightening movement, followed by a mighty heave and strain on Boomla's part as he strove to lift his slighter opponent from the ground. " Noo, noo, Kit!"</p>
        <p>" Noo, Boonfla !"</p>
        <p>"Noo, he's got him!" Terrific cheers succeeded by a moment of breathless suspense as Isaack's superior weight seemed to tell. But Kit's lithe sinews also told, and for the time he held his own, legs firmly closed round isaack's, the stretched muscles quivering in high tension, and every nerve strained. Again and again they abandoned the struggle without any definite result, and close once more in renewed determination. The con- test was a long one, and in the final fall it was next to impossible to tell who had really won, so closely were they interlocked as they rolled over and over.</p>
        <p>"He's downed him. What did I tell ilia' 1)••• said Kit's most strenuous backer to his neigh- bour, an equally determined supporter of Isaack.</p>
        <p>"He's not then. Boomla was atop when they fell. Thou's leein', George Henry Wilson." " I think Boonfla lies it," was the ultimate decision of the umpire, George Selkirk, the Blacksmith, himself a brawny heavyweight wrestler of no mean reputation. "It was a gran' fight, anyway, an' there's nowt to choose betwixt them," he added, as the boys, hot and tired, and still trembling from the strain, 61100 k hands again and moved off to re-clothe them- selves.—From " The Sorrow Stones," by Maud Williams.</p>
        <p>urem,ovo,2,ovo,ivoeo4cr-owwiewn CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE JACK. kv,A24,203,W1:05' eMOJEN4.2,Ow4s,ov&lt;V4'</p>
        <p>STILL ONE WEEK MORE.</p>
        <p>My dear _Children,—</p>
        <p>Still one week before the closing date of our competition arrives. I hope to get many entries, and that we shall have a most inter- esting competition.</p>
        <p>The subject is very simple. .just write down what you would like to be when you are grown up, and give the reason why you would like to follow that calling. Then send your letter in not later than Saturday next, 7th February. I am very pleased to say new members are rolling in steadily, and this week I have re- ceived nice letters from Beatrice Marshall, 75, Folly-lane, and Sarah Stephen, 15, Hoyle-street, who ask to be enrolled as new cousins. I have the greatest pleasure in welcoming them as . members, and hope to hear from them fre- quently.</p>
        <p>_ Now do not forget to enter for our competi- tion.</p>
