      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>If/sICLE JACK.</head>
        <p>If/sICLE JACK.</p>
        <p>A MEMORY GAME. This game is played as follows: After the subject has been decided upon, the first person makes a remark, and each successful player adds a word or two to the sentence, until one of them fails to remember. all that has been pre- viously said. say, for instance, the subject is an imaginary dinner, the players might begin thus : • •No I—l had for dinner yesterday tomato soup. No. 2—l had for dinner yesterday tomato soup and filleted plaice.</p>
        <p>No. 3—l had for dinner yesterday tomato soup, filleted plaice, bceuf a la mode. . _</p>
        <p>And so on. If the company. is composed of adults, the longest and hardest words should be used.</p>
        <p>ALL SEPT. 1. A story is told of a quaint old negro who stepped tqt to the window of a ticket office and hurriedly demanded a ticket.</p>
        <p>- " Pleasure trip, uncle?" asked the agent, pleasantly.</p>
        <p>"No, suit; my nephew's re'y low, suh. Hope de train won' be long coming." "About ten minutes, uncle," the clerk re- assured him.</p>
        <p>The old man went to the platforin, and studied the bulletin-board seriously. Then he returned to the window. "Did you say my train would be 'long in ten minutes, suh?" he asked, anxiously. " Yes, uncle." "I jest axed you, sub, 'cause dat dere board says, "All trains on'time 'eept one,' and I was jest figurin' dat dat one would be mine." "All the trains are on time. Someone's tam- pered with the bulletin-board!" declared the clerk, excitedly, and rushed to the platform. He stared for a moment at the board, then at the old negro. Slowly his face relaxed into a broad grin. The bulletin read : " All trains on time—Sept. I."</p>
        <p>JOAN OF ARC. I would like to tell the young folks what I know about Joan of Arc, that poor shepherd girl who, like the Hebrew shepherd boy, rose suddenly out of the quietness of country life into the noise and din in the van of armies. I have been reading much about her. When the English were at war with France, and the condition of that country was very bad, Joan had visions that she should lead the Dauphin to Rheims to be crowned, after she had defeated the English in battle many times. The education of this poor girl was very mean. To speak correctly, she was self-educated. She could not read, but she listened as others read parts of Roman martyrology. But there is one thing, she for ever heard the voice that was calling her to her doom.</p>
        <p>When Joan reached the Dauphin he was on the point of giving up the struggle with the English, but -after persevering for some time Joan at last obtained permission to lead the armies. She marched to Orleans and liberated it. Going on she defeated the English at Patay. She took Troyes, and so on, gaining victery after victory, and putting fresh courage into the French soldiers. On the 15th July she lead the Dauphin to Rheims, where, two days after, he was crowned.</p>
        <p>Her work was now done, she would gain no more victories, she had now to suffer. The Bishop of Beauvis, though French, took the part of the English, and condemned her to be burned as a witch. Cruel though he was and guilt-burdened, this was one of his worst crimes, which would haunt him to his death. As her last moments drew near, her languish- ing heart was dreaming of her home at Dom- remy—of the fountain and the wood in all its hen beauty—w the golden chariot carried her from " France delivered" to the Eternai King- dom.</p>
