        <p>• SCOTS' NATIONAL FERVOUR—continued.</p>
        <p>- the town still more prosperous. (Applause.) lux. W. MASON, in a racy, anecdotal speech, proposed " The w arringtou Caledonian Asso- wation." This was suitably replied to by Mr. J. L. DAVENPORT. "THE IMMORTAL MEMORY." Mr. J. UPSON had the honour to propose the toast of the occasion-- The Immortal Memory of Robert Burns.- As he came from the extreme 6outh, it was appropriate that he should pro- pose a toast relating to a man who came troui the extreme North. Lord Rosebery had characterised Burns as the poet of democracy. There was a great deal of truth in that. Burns was of the people, and for the people, with sympathies of the broadest and deepest. The fact that he was the poet of democracy accounted for his great popularity Burns was popular not only with Scotsmen; be was the poet of. Scotsmen, of Englishmen, of Irishmen, of all men—a man whose dream of brotherhood impressed men of every land and of every colour. (Applause.) The real secret of Burns' popularity lay in the fact he as not only the poet of democracy, but of humanity (Loud applause.) OTHER TOASTS. "The Guess" were proposed by Mr. P. II BROWN, and Mr. COPPOCK replied. Dr. Lim, in a humorous speech, proposed The Lassies," and Dr. R. WILSON SUTHERLAND wittily replied on behalf of the fair sex. "The Artistes" were proposed by -Mr. J. McDEamm, and "The Officers" by Mr. B SHEARER. , A splendid programme, Composed largely of (Scottish items, was contributed to by Messrs. A. Aitken, J. Robertson, R. B. Burns, T. Goos- trey, G. Marshall, J. Hunter, and Dr. Law.</p>
