      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>IT IS REALLY NECESSARY</head>
        <p>IT IS REALLY NECESSARY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>TO TAKE</head>
        <p>TO TAKE</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, J.</p>
        <p>SCOTS' NATIONAL FERVOUR.</p>
        <p>The Sons of Caledonian Eat Haggis and</p>
        <p>Toast the ROMP of Burns.</p>
        <p>MAYOR'S " BEITHERLY" WELCOME.</p>
        <p>" The pipes they bla'ed, the puddin' came in fair steandn' het, an' the wee drappie o' it— weel, it wis great, legs it wis." • The quoted sentence was the way a worthy Warrington Scot summed up the chief event in the menu at the " Burns' nicht " supper of the Warrington Caledonian Association to " a chid" sent there to " tak' notes" with the sole idea of " printin' them." This great event in tile annual diary of Scotsmen was held in the Lion _hotel on 'Saturday night. The brethren ot Burns were there in full force, but, whisrer ' it not in Scotch, there was also present a con- siderable body of Sassenachs. Still their pre- sence demonstrated that the Warrington Scots are not so out-and-out clannish as elsewhere, and the bouthrons, with speech, song and story, helped to make the Burns' festival the succes;) it proved. T "he menu, as cust ary on a Burns' nicht " banquet, was simple and plain—fish, beef, mutton, pudding. The item was the Haggis, a purely Scottish dish, so rich as to have been ascribed as the death-knell of the Sassenachs. The English guests, however, ate, enjoyed and lived, and thereby proved another story told about the Scottish fare .quite untrue. "The king o' the Duddin'" race was played into the room by Pipe-Sergeant Stoddart, of the Liverpool Scottish, and this expert with the bagpipes also played the lament after the immortal memory of Rabble Burns had been honoured as a toast. Mr. J. Fulton-Smith presided, and the chief guest was the Mayor. At many meetings the Mayor has attended of late lie has been made a " brother "—Oddfellows, Buffs, &amp;c. 'At this gathering the usual custom could not be ful- tilled, for the Scots could not make him " vera brither," but they welcomed him as one; and there was a touch of the "real Mackay" about the cordiality of the welcome.</p>
        <p>A TOAST INNOVATION. . Dr. J. S. ALissoN started the somewhat lengthy toast list. His proposition was that of " The Army, Navy and Reserve Forces." A daring innovation did the Doctor propose, when he sug- gested that the toast should be extended so as to embrace the Police Force. The audience were dumb for a second at the proposal, but as Dr. Manson seemed too serious to be perpetrat- ing a joke, the point dawned upon us, and we gave the suggestion a hearty cheer. The idea, said the Doctor, is not so strange as some of you seem to think. When the King and Queen came to Warrington, whom did you have to look after them? Did you have the Life Guards from Whitehall.' No ! You had the stalwart, bright-buttoned Warrington Guards in blue from Arpley-street. (Laughter and applause.) You see the police are taking over duties which hitherto have been the right of the military, therefore I think they should be included in the toast. (Applause.) THE POLICE HONOURED. They were, and so soldiers, sailors, "terriers," and policemen were drunk. Then after this enthusiastically pledged toast, Major F. M. APPLETON replied. The Major was pleased to see the police thus honoured. In Warrington Force they had , among the members old soldiers, and very smart men they were. (Applause.) He made a sympathetic reference to the men who had lost their lives in the submarine disaster. Recently he met and chatted with a Warrington lad home on furlough from the Navy. They talked about the disaster; and he asked the seaman if lie would dread going on one. "No, sir," came Jack Tar's reply, "as soon as I get back I expect to be drafted into one." That showed the old spirit of Nelson still prevailed in the Navy. (Loud applause.) Mr. W. SKINNER proposed the "Town of Warrington." He had come to like .Warring- ton, although before he came a man had told him Warrington was a most desirable town to get out of. (Laughter.) For business, industrial and social life the good old town of Warrington had much to recommend it. (Applause.) The toast he coupled with the name of the Mayor.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR GIVES STATISTICS. How the Town Has Grown. The MAYOR had a real, rousing Scottish wel- come. He felt grateful, he said, to Mr. Skinner, because he did not mention the old joke about the Bridge. (Laughter.) As Mr. Skinner had not mentioned it, he would say nothing about it. (Renewed laughter) When he worked in Liverpool he lived in Warrington, so there must have been something in the old town highly attractive for him. He thought it would he interesting to give a brief history of the town's development since he came to it as a boy in 1873. From the records he found that the population in 1873 was 34,000, to-day it was between 74,000 and 75,000, so the town had grown greatly in the interval. Financially Sound.</p>
        <p>The turnover in 1873 was £39,000; now it was £410,000. The assets in 1873 were £84,500, and the assets to-day were £2,000,000. The debt was £1,300,000, so Warrington was financially sound and equal to any bank. (Laughter and applause.) He mentioned that, by the way, in case any- one wanted to lend money—the Corpora:Eon were prepared to take it. The present finan- cial position had not been brought about With- out sacrifice on the part of the ratepayers. They were suffering to-day, and had been suffering for years from the faults of their forefathers, and he had no doubt those in time to come would blame them in the present time, for the mistakes they were making, (Laughter.) The rates in 1873 were ss. 9d., and to-day there had been a substantial increase to Bs. in the 1. Still that did not show an actual in- crease, because of 2s. 3d. additional they had is. 9d. for education, and the improvements in Bridge-street, Buttermarket-street and Sankey- street had added another is. Id. Actually Lower Than in 1873. If they eliminated the education rate they would find that the rates were lower actually than in 1873. He did not take any credit for doing that. (Laughter.). He was one, how- ever, who tried to keep the rates down in order to encourage manufacturers to come to War- rington. The town was now better served than ever it was. The health was better looked after. They kid other services which did not exist when he came to Warrington, and lie did not claim any credit for that. (Laughter.) They had now got trams, electricity, gasworks, waterworks, and all were making profits aad heaping up reserves. (Hear, hear.) An Education Bridge. The Education Authorities were now endea- vouring to bridge a serious educational difficulty and soon it would be possible for their youths to go to the University with the aid of a Cor- Poration grant. (Applause.) The Education Authorities were hopeful of being able to pro- vide six University scholarships of about £7O each. He did not think the ratepayers would grudge the £4OO required. (Applause.) I am going to insist, added the Mayor, that these scholarships should be open for boys who have the brains, but not the opportunity, of getting to the University without such aid. (Loud applause.) During the time he had been in Warrington the town had received handsome gifts—Xl,ooo from Mr. George Crosfield, £3,000 from Lord Winwarleigh, and £l,OOO from Mr. Peter Walker, whose family also gave a splendid donation to the Park. An Organ Wanted.</p>
        <p>They had wanted a new organ for the Pail Hall, but the Local Government Board would not allow, the Council to borrow the money. To some of the generous people be suggested that if they gifted an organ to the Parr Hall Warrington would be very pleased to receive it.-- (Laughter and applause.) If the Town Council appointed him a deputation to wait upon two or three of these people he would tell them that they might to give it. (Laughter and applause.) • He hoped the present trade wolild continue, and that Warrington, would go on prospering, and he hoped with the normal rates, the river accommodation, the Ship Canal and the rail' facilities they,had to offer would induce •other manufacturer s to come to Warrington and make</p>
        <p>Continued in preceding column</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>FORESTERS' SECRETARY.</head>
        <p>FORESTERS' SECRETARY.</p>
        <p>Bro. Ll. Davies Honoured After 27 Years' Faithful Service.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S PRAISES.</p>
        <p>There was a large attendance of members of the " Court Mercy" Lodge, No. 240, of the Ancient Order of Foresters, at the Roebuck tiOtel, on Saturday night, to witness the pre- sentation of a gold watch, suitably inscribed, to r.D.C.R. Bro. Ll. Davies, upon his retiremeht from the office of secretary, which he had held with much distinction for 27 years. His Wor- ship the Mayor (Bro. Peter Peacock) made the presentation, while the Mayoress kindly ,re- seated a diamond-centred gold brooch to Mrs. Davies. There was an especially strong mus- ter of ladies, including Mrs. Corradine (secre- tary of the Female Court " Winmarleigh"), Mrs. O. W. R. Baines, Mrs. E. Hough, Mrs. F. Davies; Mrs. H. C. Cooper, Miss James, Sister Morris, Sister Baines, Mrs. Henry Babies, Mrs. J. Davies and Mrs. F. Bate.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Pierpoint and his old Friends. District Chief Ranger Bro. S. B. Healey, who occupied the chair, said they had gathered to recognise the faithful services, extending over 27 years, of one of their veterans, who, however. was still young in spirit and his desire for the welfare of the Order. (Applause.) P.D.C.R. Bro. G. W. R. Baines, the new secre- tary of the.: court, read letters of apology for non-attendance from Mr. Robert Pierpoint, Dr. Reginald Smith, Captain Appleton, and Er. J. Fulton-Smith. Mr. Pierpoint said he would</p>
        <p>BRO. LL</p>
        <p>have been delighted to attend as an honorary member to take part in honouring Bro.-Davies had he been in his old home; but, though away, the receipt of the invitation had been a pleas- ing reminder that lie was not forgotten by his old friends. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>The, Insurance Act: Adverse Opinion Withdrawn. Bro. Aldernian Tinnion proposed "Our Great Order, and in particular ' Court Mercy, No. 240.' " lie had, he said, known Bro. Li. Davies for something like 35 years, and it was a per- sonal pleasure to take part in honouring him. Everyone should belong to this great Order, which was one of those institutions which brought their members together in the great brotherhood of mankind, broadening their sym- pathies and extending their outlook. No. 240 was the second, if not the most ancient, court in the district; a court which had a history dat- ing from 1834 was one in which members might be justly proud. It was for the present Mem- bers to see that its high traditions were main- tained and handed forward to their successors as pure and unsullied as they received them. (Applause.) P.D.C.R. G. W. R. Barnes, responding, said the honour of succeeding Bro. Davies as secre- tary had fallen upon him, and he recognised how difficult would be the task of following an official who had done his work in such an excel- lent and painstaking manner. They had had a very rough time during the last twelve months. The National Insurance Act had had a rebound- ing effect upon them, but he was pleased to say they had got on the right side of that now— (hear, hear, and applause)—and if things con- tinned to progress as now, their would be in the same position as before the Act came into force. It had been said that the Insurance Act would kill Friendly Societies. "I believe," said the speaker, "I expressed that opinion myself, but (Applause.) We are still making new members, and I don't think Tweamarbeoulnosd tingo wanityhdraw it." greater proportion of old members than we used to do. If the members will give the officers the help that should be given there is no reason why this court should not be as prosperous as any court in the War- rington district. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>The Mayor Makes the Presentation. ' His Worship the Mayor then presented Bro. Davies with the watch. He said he was initiated in that court when sixteen years of age. He did not like to attend because he was afraid of going into a public-house. Afterwards he did attend, and found Bro. navies there giving advice and saying things which had made a lasting impression upon his memory. He in- sisted on everything being straight and above been board, and protested against that, "false deli- cacybeforethe greatest had " which sometimes prevented people from seeing that everything was above suspicion. Ile (the Mayor) was thus induced to take such influence l hadd an interest in the court that he was a brother officer, with Councillor Chrimes as auditor, le Mayor)pleame-ourrkee. d }list hrelei iimg. the district—lie District Secretary for 21 years and for that court punlaraotei.eo Bnawi-no satisfactorilyeuslctl illeyai al oadsnsad wpclroloimcmlptilsetiltilieearyc;cffioneyetl I:11.011;g not el. ia-iern-, hear.) He had worked with Bro. Davies dux.-- ing the whole of the quarter century he (the being speaker) had been connected with the Foresters. and he knew many members could not help contact with such a personality. Bro. Davies was a shining example to be followed by all. g made into better citizens for their close They all hoped that he would live long to look upon his watch and reflect upon their apprecia- tion of his work, which it represented. When Bro. Davies resigned his district office they court would likewise find him another job. Promptly made him a trustee, and he hoped the (Hear, hear.) They simply could not afford to lose him. . .. i:tillHot etseshiibs knew</p>
        <p>---..., ...—. Toe presentation was then made amid ap- plause. On the inner case of .the handsome watch was the following inscription:— A.0.F., " Court mercy," No. 240. Presented to Bro. L. Davies in recognition of his services as secretary for the past 27 years. 24 Jan., 1914. The Mayoress, at the invitation of the Chair- man, presented the brooch to Mrs. Davies.</p>
        <p>The Retiring Secretary and Sr.l.rs. Davies. P.D.C.R. Bro. Councillor Chrimes proposed ie health of Mr. and Mrs. Davies. In Bro. Davies they had had an excellent servant, who had always been willing to do all he possibly could, and had been looked upon by himself and others as a kind of father and one of the best of friends. (Hear, hear.) Bro. Davies must feel proud that evening at having received the watch at the bands of the Mayor, a Forester who had risen to the highest position the town could possibly offer him. (Applause.) He thought it would be a good thing if courts had a kind of freeman's list whereon they could record, in a conspicuous place, the names of those who per- formed such services as Bro. Davies had per- formed for them. (Applause.) P.D.C.R. Bro, Jos. Owen supported, saying he had worked with Bro. Davies, who was two years ahead of him, for 44 years. They had been in opposition for office, but they were quite satisfied when either of them had been beaten</p>
        <p>Continued in re xt eclumn</p>
        <p>, TA* &lt; • A•tailv•v4,- • • gv&lt;vg.',lo.o, • I • , .vgSt DAVIES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>NUARY – 31, 1914.</head>
        <p>NUARY - 31, 1914.</p>
        <p>A THRIVING MISSION.</p>
        <p>The Bank Quay Workingmen's Cause Makes</p>
        <p>Steady Progress.</p>
        <p>TOUCHING REMINISCENCES.</p>
        <p>Social functions of an ordinary character pale into insignificance at the Working Men's Mission, Bank Quay, when the annual congregational gathering is held. Such proved the case on Monday evening, when a large company of members and friends sat down for tea at well- set tables, and almost exhausted the patience of the ladies who had kindly undertaken to attend to their wants, The concert which followed was presided over by Mrs. John Crosfield, of Colwyn Bay, and she was supported by Messrs. T. Dale, N. Hough and G. Dale. ,The Lyric Quartet party (Madame Sanders; Miss C. Hastings, Messrs. J. Willett and E. Walker) were in good form, and in their quartets, "Moonlight and music" and "Good Evening," displayed a combination of voices which blended in a most harmonious manner. The duet "Barcarole" was splendidly sung by Madame Sanders and Miss Hastings; while in\ her songs "The rosary" and "Idle words," Madame Sanders displayed the rich- ness of her contralto voice. Miss C. llastings charmed the audience with her selections, "April song" and "Annie Laurie," and as a soloist Mr. E. Walker was heard to advantage in "Oh ! Oh !" and Glorious Devon." "My dreams" and "Roses" served to make Mr. J. Willett still popular with a Bank Quay Mission audience. M.r. Percy Smith accompanied with considerable skill. Mr. A: Dutton proved a capable elocutionist, and an action song by scholars of the Sunday school was rendered in a splendid manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>ITEMS OF INTEREST.</head>
        <p>ITEMS OF INTEREST.</p>
        <p>The year's progress in Sunday school work was given in detail by Mr. A. Edwards, a report which showed that much good work had been done. In presenting the chapel report Mr. A. Tilling, jun., made reference to the associations of the late Mr. John Crosfield with the Mission. lie said that in the Minutes of the meetings of the committee in the early days of the Mission frequent reference was made to "Mr. John," the inference being that it could not possibly be mistaken as to whom it referred. At a com- mittee meeting held on December 2nd, 1883, there was a record that "Bro. Long be appointed to wait upon Mr. John to see when it would be convenient for him to preside at the annual concert; also to invite Master Arthur to give pianoforte selections"; and at the next meet- ing " Bro. Long reported that Mr. John had kindly consented to preside, also promising to send his piano for members of his family to play on at the concert." Extracts of this description, continued Mr. Tilling's report, showed how very close were the ties whfch bound the workers to the founder of the Mis- sion. The reports had always borne expressions of appreciation to the Crosfield family, and they wished to assure Mrs. John Crosfield and Other members of the family that they still thanked them sincerely for their continued help and interest. Considerable progrpss had been made during the past year. The membership of the Mission had increased, and their finances showed a balance in 'hand of A:l7 Bs. The various organisations were in a flourishing con- dition, and the prospects for the coming year as bright as any of previous years. They were looking forward to an extension of the Mission premises, towards which a sum of £355 had been raised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>MRS. I. CROSFIELD'S DELIGHT.</head>
        <p>MRS. I. CROSFIELD'S DELIGHT.</p>
        <p>Mrs. J. Crosfield, who was deeply moved by the reminiscent note of the report, said she was delighted to hear of their splendid progress. It was a pleasure to her to be amongst them to witness such activity, and she would always endeavour to do what she could towards the carrying on of such a noble work. She thanked them all for their good wishes which they sent her from time to time, and she was sure when she considered all that had been done and was being done, that the future generations of the Mission, wherever their lot would be cast, would cherish the teachings and memories gained as a result of their association with that church. A comprehensive vote of thanks, proposed by Mr. O. Dale, was briefly responded to by Mrs. Crosfield, aad the singing of the National An- them brought a pleasant evening to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WIDNES PONY ROASTED TO DEATH.</head>
        <p>WIDNES PONY ROASTED TO DEATH.</p>
        <p>The Widnes Fire Brigade on Monday night were called te. some sheds behind Widnes-road, owned by William Palm The sheds were used as pig-styes and stables, but no pigs were on the premises when the fire broke- out. The only occupant of the stables was a pony. A youth named Keogh made a brave attempt to get it out, but without success, the pony being roasted to death. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>FORESTERS' SECRETARY—continued.</head>
        <p>FORESTERS' SECRETARY—continued.</p>
        <p>by the other. Bro. Davies had done a noble work, and the assistance he had given by words that came from the heart, and deeds, had, as the Mayor said, done a great amount of good. To Mrs. Davies their best thanks were due, for the wife of a secretary did a great deal of work in an indirect way for the court her husband was serving. (Hear, hear.) The toast having been drunk with enthusiasm. Bro. Davies responded. Funds, he said, were very low when he took over the secretarys but many improvements had been effected. As to his 'false delicacy" statement mentioned by the Mayor, he had told his_auditores_ to go The o into every figure without studying his 1 essential thing was that everyt,fe ling should be proved to be right. (Hear, hear.) He had tried to do his duty in three offices, the last of which he was now resigning. They had a new secre- tary who would do thework—there were plenty who would like the office, but would not do the work—(hear, hear, and laughter)—and, as they know, the success of the society depended upon the secretary. In thanking the Mayor and Mayoress and the court for their kindness to him and his wife, he was glad to say he was in as good health as when he was 20. (Applause.) But he was plagued with rheumatism, which made him look forty years older than he was— (iaughter)—but as long as he could limp as far as the Roebuck, if Court 240 was held there, he would try to come and do what he could • for them. (Applause.) C.R. Bro. 0. B. Jackson presented a ribbon to P.C.R. Bro. A. R. Baines. P.D.C.R. Bro. R. Thornley (district secre- tary), proposing the health of the ladies coupled with the name of the Mayoress, said it had given them all the greatest possible pleasure to see how the female court—Court " Winmarleigh had prospered under the diligent secretars73lliP of Sister Corradine. (Applause.) Mrs. E. Hough (daughter of Mr. Ll. Davies) presented a lovely bouquet to the Mayoress. Bro. .Councillor Georg n Archer, proposing "The Visitors," referred to the good work the present Mayor was doing in so many directions in Warrington upon thoreughly democratic lines. D.S.C.R. Bro. Boffins (Widnes) replied. Bro. George Allen and Bro. j . Davies pro- posed a vote of thanks to the Press and the Chairman respectively.</p>
        <p>TRUE EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>The Work and Objects of the Adult School Movement in Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>DR. S. MELLOR'S TRIBUTE.</head>
        <p>DR. S. MELLOR'S TRIBUTE.</p>
        <p>It was evident from the enthusiasm of the large gathering in the Co-operative Hall on Sunday afternoon that we have in our midst a living organisation under the name of the Adult Schools. The Mayor presided over the large meeting, and he was supported by, amongst others, the Rev. Dr. S. Mellor and Mr. D. P. Boyd, of Birmingham. The MAYOR said in the Adult School move- ment in. Warrington he had a large number of friends, who had kept themselves fixed to lofty ideals. They in Warrington were not satisfied with the progress they had already made, and he hoped .that power would be given to them to open out in every district. They were pleased to have Dr. Mellor with them, but it was with regret that they heard of his pending departure from Warrington for Liverpool. They could do with more men of his standard, and they trusted that he would be successful in his now sphere of work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>AN EDUCATIONAL INSTITUTION.</head>
        <p>AN EDUCATIONAL INSTITUTION.</p>
        <p>Dr. iTIEL—LO—R: who .1;-e1:17:d.c:N7e.rY cordial greet- ing, said that if he understood the Adult School movement aright, its main purpose and ultimate significance must be set down as educational. The Adult Schools were not social clubs, poli- tical institutions, or trade organisations, nor were they in essence any substitute for the church, for although worship formed part of their work, they were not in essence worship- ping assemblies. Amongst the high motives and fine ideals which inspired the founders and originators of the movement, there must be reckoned a genuine love of knowledge and of the objects of knowledge, things true, beautiful and good, and the desire to seize opportunities and chances of spreading this education. They sought the kindling touch of mind with mind to strike the flame of knowledge into a bright and warmful glow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>"Something Almost Tragic."</head>
        <p>"Something Almost Tragic."</p>
        <p>He had to admit That he found in the Adult School movement, in one respect at any rate, something rather pitiable, something almost tragic. He could not help seeing still another startling indictment of the system of the social life, and still another glaring proof of the in- herent futility and absurdity of that system. They had members of the schools meeting in the early hours of Sunday morning, stealing a few moments one day out of seven for the pur- suit of the best things in life. During the other six days of the week there was no time for these ' things. All the magnificent treasures of know- ledge and education were unnoticed and un- touched because men are bound unmercifully to the wheel of toil, and the pursuit to which they were enslaved was not the following after spiri- tual delights, but the leaping up t material riches, and even then, in the vas but majority of cases, not for themselves, for an absurdly privileged few,. of vain,</p>
        <p>Product of a Social true the beauti- Education, the pursuit of the 1 , . ful and the good, which silo% cthhaenecoestododf d be the main individual ual life, was System. tl:Leneawt business of social and and givenallitdis pushed aside, moments at odd times. , sacrifice which. ought never to be demanded. He did not think • they ought ever to forget that side of the truth. -. The ,Adult Schoo' , were the product and the result of a certain social situation, and their ' existence meant that the situation was imper- fect and faulty. Those who were interested in the schools should come to regard their move- ment as temporary, a brave endeavour 'and a courageous attempt to fill up what was lacking and make good the defects and deficiencies of the whole—as something which, were society as it ought to be, and social life what it should be, w uld not be necessary. He said all this not by , way of discouragement at all, but simply to urge the, truth, for truth must be. spoken in season and out of season. He had sincere praise , for the reputed object of Adult Schools—educa- tion. Many people hopelessly misunderstood education. Some had the audacity to despise it, but it was a sorrowful state for any man to be in, to either despise or misunderstand educa- tion. Such a man had indeed the lie in his soul. He was self-deluded and deluded others, and no words of condemnation would be too strong for such a creature.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>Greatest Thing in Life.</head>
        <p>Greatest Thing in Life.</p>
        <p>Education was the greatest thing in life save one, namely, religion, for which education was the way of preparing. By education he did not mean something that would help them to get a better income, or a mere imparting of know- ledge and giving of information, however use- ful and necessary, but genuine education. The only kind that mattered was a spiritual thing, i and everything concerned absolutely n spiri- tual things. It had to do with the awakening of personality, and with the bringing of a man to the realisation of his true, rightful and gel nine self, the cultivation of personality and the preparation . of a free, full, rich and abund- ant personal If they studied history, or ' examined their own experience, they would find i that everywhere t was ignorance that was the detestable foe of all progress and advancement. It went about under the guise of preillice, bigotry and narrowness of view, which hugged to its breast false conventionalities and out- worn beliefs, and which lurked about robbing men of their right to know.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Building Utopias.</head>
        <p>Building Utopias.</p>
        <p>- • There were many in these days whir looked forward to a better and nobler order of social life, many who built Utopias, and set about working for their establishment, but in their path stood ignorance, which must be driven into , the abyss before they could move forward. Education alone could conquer this. The great hour of freedom and redemption- would strike when education had done its proper work, when art and science and literature and music came to the mind of the people as things they must have at any cost and dare not be without. Therefore, wherever and whenever true educa- tion was imparted to men, he did not care what the particular agency may be, the effort was to be commended, and should command support. That was whY he commended the Adult School movement.</p>
        <p>Good and Valuable Work.</p>
        <p>In so far as their work was educational, it was a good and valuable work. If they opened the highway of education to any- man they (lid well. Make men think for themselves, and they</p>
        <p>(Continued in next column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>TOBACCO OR NO TOBACCO</head>
        <p>TOBACCO OR NO TOBACCO</p>
        <p>That Was the Question Left to th Runcorn Guardians to Settle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>WHAT AN,INMATE WANTED.</head>
        <p>WHAT AN,INMATE WANTED.</p>
        <p>Tobacco or no tobacco? That was the qui tion put by an inmate of the Dutton Wel house to the members of the Runcorn Board Guardians. He complained that his "bagel supply had been stopped by the Master, and asked the Board for the reason. He was oi sixty and was not able-bodied. It was explained that the Board had been the habit of giving tobacco to inmates oi sixty years of age. The Master considered ti this particular man was able-bodied, and even doubted that he was sixty. _</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., ihe chairman, . • -</p>
        <p>formed him that the distribution was left to discretion of the Master.</p>
        <p>The Inmate: I think I should have my sbi A Member: Take advice from me and go get work. (Hear, hear.) The Inmate: I would rather do it. I (14 want to stop here if I can get any work. stated he wanted to get work. A Member: Why don't you get your disclic and buy your own tobacco? (Hear, bear.) After the man had retired the Board um mously agreed that the Master had acted ail in the matter.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. MAWDSLEY. Mr. Robert Mawdsley, of Rainhill, w' thanking the Board for the resolution of s pathy passed. The relatives deeply apprech the kindness extended during Mr. Mawdsl illness and the sympathy shown in their reztvement.</p>
        <p>ASYLUMS CHARGES.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the County Asylum, Ches wrote stating that the committee had fixed rate of maintenance of pauper lunatics for year ending 31st March, 1914, at 98. Old., head per week, the same rate as previot charged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>PAUPERISM SLIGHTLY DECREAS</head>
        <p>PAUPERISM SLIGHTLY DECREAS</p>
        <p>The Clerk presented a Local GoverseP',-, Board return of pauperism in England Wales for for the half-year ending July, 191 • 3 statistics for the Runcorn Union were: POP; lation, 45,569; indoor paupers, 189, including ", able-bodied; 72 not able-bodied; 67 childre under 16; 6 insane; lunatics in county and oug,h asylums, registered hospitals, aOutdoorccou la of , g 154 relieved °I uiseclu s;dg women relieve' with-their husbands; 220 children under 16; insane; 9 casual paupers. Total paupers 11 lieved 749, a decrease of 11 for the year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>OVERSEER'S APPOINTMENT.</head>
        <p>OVERSEER'S APPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>- The Clerk Clerk to the Hatton Parish Council wr° stating that at the annual meeting on Friclo William Fowles, of Till Moss Farm, Mato was appointed overseer of the parish. THE MASTER'S APPLICATION. The Master made an application to the BO for a testimonial, as be was applying for t post of master under the Warrington Board _</p>
        <p>Guardians. Mr. W. Morris moved that the Board give best possible testimonial, notwithstanding fact that he, for one, would he indeed sorry their Master and his wife were successful. SI it was for their betterment, and he had I greatest pleasure in proposing it. Mr. J. A. Jones, in seconding; said that would be full of disappointment if the appli tion was Successful, for they had great adrai tion for the Master and Matron. It was mil tion that was making them apply for the Pc and that was what they all liked. _</p>
        <p>it lA as unanimously decided to give the monial.</p>
        <p>Alderman J. H. Maden, of Bacup, preside of the famous Irwell Springs Band, entertain the bandsmen and the committee, with Or concert and dance in the Town Hall on Satl day. The restoration of the Parish Church at Virl, dington, near Clitheroe, which has extent' over more than six years and kas cost X 2.5 was practically completed on Saturday, WI new vestries were dedicated by the Rev. J. Garnett, rural dean of Craven.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
