      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>TRUE EDUCATlON—continued.</head>
        <p>TRUE EDUCATlON—continued.</p>
        <p>would think for others and for the world; tl/, would break away front the narrowness of I merely private life, and learn to cultivate broader outlook and nobler aspirations. Ab° all they would begin to build and labour for t future, preparing for the time when the trel ures of education would be the free and oP possession of all, and when, in equality and 11, tice, men and women would learn and tittri/</p>
