      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>SCRAPPY AND MUDDY MATCH.</head>
        <p>A SCRAPPY AND MUDDY MATCH.</p>
        <p>Peaco had her victory. With the strike settled, the Warrington Club were able to turn out the full senior side at Wilderspool, and the result was a substantial 20 points victory over 11114°01'n, the final scores proving 6 goals 5 tries (27 poims) for Warrington, compared with ,trhe 2 goals 1 try (7 points) scored by Runcorn. hese g ..-gurea give a fair indication of the general trend of the game. w The match was of a scrappy character. This as mainly due to a sudden thaw occurring about three hours before the kic.c-off, which in.adn tire pitch extremely soft and greasy. The Li°ll/al was so slippery through mud that the "arlington forwards found it next to impos- sible to control the ball, with the result that their characteristic footwork was at a discount, "4 we saw little of it. The lthreequarters also found the ball so til'ea!sY that accurate passing was almost out of quefstion, and only. on rare occasions did ~,!`"r Side indulge in combined work:", In a it was a day for individual work, and Is was the game chiefly adopted.</p>
        <p>D. WARRINGTON SCORE EARLY. 4-'B4 filo ground been dry Runcern would un- ti?nbtedly have got a more severe drubbing than th eueitiitofl i 3 2.0 points for the ex-strikers were out for 'a big score, and they played e r hardest i , even f they were somewhat soft after their voluntary rest for a fortnight. tabu° game was not many minutes old before ti "'ildersPool men had/taken the measure of opponents, and the first points came within five minutes of the kick-off. Warrington we swarming within the Runcorn "25" line, l,lien the visitors were penalised for not playing ao ball. Jolley came up, took the kick and anded a fine penalty goal. !After this Runcorn made a desperate at.;.empt ° get level, and Young Jolley, brother of the home back, flashed into the picture several Lilllos, but Jolley, major was invariably too much for Jolley minor, and the young Runcorn "quit failed to get the points he wanted. WARRINGTON'S SECOND PENALTY GOAL. , A smart breakaway by the home forwards looked certain of a score being forthcoming, but St the critical moment the referee's whistle went. He had discovered two Runcorn men cgiside and awarded a free-kick to Warrington. , Jolley again landed a nice penalty goal. After this the Warrin tonians started to run ll g a . Over the visitors, whose backs had a hot time In the way of defensive work. A fine bit of he,Slng on the home right resulted in the ball leg snipped on to Ruddick, and he sped away hiThe a," fox, the Runcornite hounds speeding after 'n full cry. It cleverly swerved past • the,w and planted perhaps the smartest try of clay Jolley once more made good use of 40,wgiilal-kicking boots, and Warrington were tisve„a°lllllg 9-0. Soon. after Clare made a Peitirl'ia4terupt, but was pulled down. Latex laalixie3- got possession and scored easily. Jolley noint.., 1104hillila.t half-time Warrington led by 14 Iteh,„ ARE GETS A FINE TRY. . 11,0rtinn''t,,a became more aggressive in the second ainfy through the Warrington men , ° 1° their earlier bustling tactics -13c0re, °t long, however, before ViTarrin It gton "nother t. frorn ° a • young, getting the ball ..gainfo'ell°las. sent it out to Waywell, who . ground...zgith Clare assiduously backing three- , qu t 1,1• At an opportune moment the a4ted to dare, who ran over with .a 6o a— ~ l' try. Jolley added the goal points, and R witrrington were 19 points to the pod. tinco *Tolle rn next were in evidence and YoUn The 3, beat his brother and scored a good try. fatni!aunger member of the Jolley football ltsweY well deserved the try. His kick at goal, bnt j"?. failed. Soon after Vilaywell scored, frota 02.1_eY. failed to convert with the long kick for woeal the touch-line. Tranter got a try De 41,11.11gt0n. and other two points came from tr_ 8 /laity goal. Runcorn scored, through vit_se,Y, two penalties, the game ending in orrington's favour by 27 points to 7.</p>
        <p>ga%efythint PASSABLE DISPLAY. taken into account, Warrington liaSsable display. The ground was dead trinst the rt forwards, and they therefore failed Fe 4, into their usual stride. Clara and 1;11, 1e37 were, the more active of the pack, but til,ne men were triers. Harmer, who depu- f4;t4 for Cliesters, who was ill, made a most x°.nrable impression. "ich 141111 n Q'-as and Young have played better th 'S. but they were generally reliable, even-if Th' ground was dead against their style of play. binet.threequarters were very active. George itzkr,naon and Ruddick did much good work, naywell, with Tranter, made a good left Jolley was in his happiest mood and a series of goals in his cleverest manner. lia;;Lne Jolley, Watton, Massey and Shaw were iII evidence on the Runcorn side.</p>
        <p>By ii Scrum.”</p>
        <p>date has been fixed for 20th April, the week after Easter Monday, on which day the Rovers are at Wilderspool:</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON `.`A' BEAT RUNCORN. A Soft Proposition Ends in Victory. The greasy ground militated against the finer points of the game being developed. Taken all over, the Warrington "A" men were the smarter, and they well deserved their 4 goals 4 tries (20 points) to 1 goal 1 try (five points) victory. First points came to WarringtOn with a penalty goal, which Critchlq neatly kicked. Dainteth was not long in coming into the pic- ture with a clever try. This Critchley con- verted. Then Runcorn had a look:in, and Deakin- was almost over the line with a nice dribble, when ,he was obstructed. A penalty goal was -kicked by Shaw. This brought War- rington up to tune again. Brookes scored an easy try, and Dainteth converted. Douglas Was responsible for another fine try, which was not converted, and at half-time Warrington led by 15 points to 2. There was more life in the second half, and both goals were frequently endangered. Doug- las snipped over with a nice' try for Warring- ton. Critchley goalcd. Jackson scored a goalless try for Runcorn, and Warrington thus won by 20 points to 5. The state of the ground and ball considered, the whole of the• Warrington side played very well.</p>
        <p>• Team for To-day. Warrington "A" meet Wigan "A" at Wilders- pool to-day. The game should be an excellent crne, with victory to the home side. The War- rington team will he:— Jordan; Baker, Dainteth, Steel and Brown; Douglas and Brookes; Bevan, Green, Critchley, Boardman, Arnold and Bradshaw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>STIFF TASK AT II MAN.</head>
