      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>PICKS OUT OF THE SCRUM.</head>
        <p>PICKS OUT OF THE SCRUM.</p>
        <p>Pithy Pars About N.U. Games.</p>
        <p>Peace, perfect peace! The strike ended happily. • The players restarted happily, And a line victory marked all. 4,50 all's well that ends well. Although the League position has been Somewhat impaired through the trouble, It is-still possible to get into The hist four and the cup is open All the way to the final stage. The players are evidently out to DO something great. Good luck to them!</p>
        <p>cc e‘e The match at Wilderspool was Quite a "Jolley" game, wasn't it? TA-0 members of the Jolley family Of footballers ,played a prominent part., Our Jolley distinguished himself with His fine goal-kicking. By the way, Had he polished the goal-kicking boots up During the time he was out on strike? Runcorn's Jolley is another little hero. He played the best game for his side, And well deserved the try he scored. He had hard luck in not getting the goal.</p>
        <p>e. e ccc Tilley, the old Warrington. full-back, was another Popular personage in the visiting team. He is not, however, the player we Once knew him to be—age, of course. Still, Tilley did fairly good work. The Warrington dribblers do not like mud Upon the pitch—it spoils their style of play. An enthusiastic Huddersfield scribe has , Compared Gleeson to Steve Bloomer, the Well-known Association dribbler. Who would he Compare Clare, Fearnley, or Thomas with? Either would dribble circles round Gleeson,</p>
        <p>eeeeee Ruddick scored perhaps the best try seen. Clare scored another neat three points. Fearnley ran away from his opponents to Tranter was another try-maker for Warring- ton. Waywell was the other good "tryer." Three of the threequarters and two of the fel ards Thus were responsible for the five tries. It was a treat to see tries got by The quartet men, after what has been said That none of them could possibly score. The ground considered, they also put in A fair amount of combination work.</p>
        <p>coecee The match was inclined to be a bit rough. Referee ArCutcheon kept it well in hand. A man on each side was cautioned, Otherwise there was nothing very striking.' Skelhorne was carried off injured at one stage. It looked as if he had sustained a broken leg. Councillor Osborne and other committee men showed much Concern in the case of the injured player, and They summoned the services of a doctor. No bones were broken, and after a time Skelhorne was able to resume his game. Two Runcorn men were also temporarily hurt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>0000 Ce</head>
        <p>0000 Ce</p>
        <p>The three Warrington teams brought home wins.</p>
        <p>Warrington Seniors had a clear lead of 20 points. The Warrington "A" team won at Runcorn By a nine majority of 15 points. Rylands' succeeded in beating Cadishead After a close and exciting game by 5 points. When three sides bring wins home Ta roost it does not look as if The earring code is on the down grade In Warrington, as has been alleged, does it? What will happen at Wigan to-day? One would like to see Warrington pull off that match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>HYLANDS' DEFEAT CADISHEAD. Five Points Win After a Stirring Game.</head>
        <p>HYLANDS' DEFEAT CADISHEAD. Five Points Win After a Stirring Game.</p>
        <p>Rylands' secured a creditable victory over Cadishead in the return League match at Firs- lane. Rylands' were short of Warren, Laws tak- ing his place. Cadishead had a full team. Stretford kicked off and at once Itylands' com- menced to press. Lambert relieved well under difficulties. Rushton fed Povey, but lie was not allowed to go far. After midfteld play, Laws gave to Carter, who at once made tracks for the line, but being tackled close in, passed inside, and Whittaker fielding the ball scored. Morris failed at goal. From a misunderstanding be- tween two of Rylands' backs, Clare ricked up</p>
        <p>and made a grand run, being brought dem n on the line by Horrocks. Rylands' cleared their line from the scrummage formed. Half-time arrived with the score: itylands' one try (three points); Cadishead, nil. Forster commenced the second half, the ball being returned to centre. Rushton and Connor opened the game out for Cadishead, and, feed- ing Vinton, that player seemed to have a clear field, but was well tackled by Laws. Rylands' commenced to press, and A. Horrocks grounded, but was brought back, the ball having hit the. corner-flag. From a penalty for obstruction Stretford kicked a goal. Lambert, in defending his line, was hurt, and was carried off. Time arrived with the score: Rylands', one goal one try (five points); Cadishead, nil. It was a good, clean, keen game. For Rylands' F. Horrocks shone at full-back, Laws, Cartel and Morris also playing well. Rushton, Forster and Lambert were the pick of Cadishead.</p>
      </div>
