      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 31, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>N.U. Cup-tie To-day.</head>
        <p>The N.U. Cup-tie To-day.</p>
        <p>Rylands' Recs. meet Firs-lane at Leigh to-day in the fourth qualifying round of the N.U. Cup. They will turn out. the following strong side, and should win: F. Horrocks; T. Laws, S. Car- ter, A. Horrocks, P. Morris; G. Warren, J. Gallop; A. Stretford (caPtain), J. Whittaker, J. Brandwood, D. Slater, P. Mason, J. 'Broadhurst.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECS. "A " v. DITTON RECS. The above match, in the St. Helens and Dis- trict-League, took place at Gorsey-lane. In the early stages of the game Potter, the home full- back, in saving a rush by the visiting forwards, was kicked by Hankin, and the last-named got marching orders. Ditton now pressed and Forster scored at the corner, Whelan failing at goal. This reverse roused the homesters, and good play: by the forwards resulted in Peers touching down near the posts, Potter convert- ing. Half-time arrived with the home team two points ahead.</p>
        <p>_ On resuming, Ditton were early aggressive, and from a free-kick Whelan equalised the scores. Play to the close was of a very even nature, and a good game ended in a draw of five points each.</p>
        <p>Potter played his usual sterling game foi Rylands', and Myles, Cooper and Cummings also played well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>HOGKt.Y.</head>
        <p>HOGKt.Y.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD SENIORS OUTPLAYED.</p>
        <p>Latchford Ist entertained South Manchester and the match resulted in a 4-1 victory for the visitors Latchford were completely overplayed in the second half of the game, although they only gave away one goal after half-time. The outstanding feature of the game was the won- derful intercepting of the visitors; time 'after time their half-backs and full-backs toes- up passes intended for the home men.</p>
        <p>South Manchester were tue first t.) sore, their clever outside-left, Collar, taking a first-.time shot from a good centre from the left wing, ithich gave Bruhl no earthly chance of saving. From a corner, Pitt scored a second goal when the goalkeeper was unsighted. This was a fine piece of work on the part of the visitors' inside-left, for he had to manwnvre for position after gathering the ball. Latchford went away on the right, Isherwood putting in an excellent centre, which Pearson crashed into the net with a dangerous rising shot. The visitors' inside- right hooked in a third before half-time.</p>
        <p>In the second half South Manchester monopon used the play, butonly scored once, while Latch- ford, with much less play, should have scored at least twice. The feature of this half was the fine display of Edwards, the visitors' centre-half, who gave the finest exhibition which Latchford have been privileged to see for some years. He was wherever the ball was, and had the Latch- ford inside forwards completely outwitted. Defending and attacking came alike to him. The South Manchester contingent are a fine side and will give any team a warm seventy minutes. Bruhl was the only man on the home side to do himself justice, some of his saves being really fine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>Reserves Also Defeated.</head>
        <p>Reserves Also Defeated.</p>
        <p>The second eleven also had a tough proposition at Whalley-Range, 'whel'e they met Snuth Man- chester "A," being beaten by the same score as their first team, 4-1. Owing to the recent raino the going was rather heavy, but this did not prevent the teams from having a very enjoyablo game. The opening exchanges were fairly even, both sides pressing for some time without any tangible result: Latchford were the first to score, with a shot from Marsden, and this lead they retained till shortly before half-time, al- though Manchester made several attempts to score, but were kept out chiefly by the good goal- keeping of Mynekyme. Their first goal was the outcome of a, corner, followed by a scramble on the goal-line. Their left wing pressed continu- ally and had hard lines in not increasing the score, -Which remained at 1-1 at half-time. Both goalkeepers' had given a good display and had been the means of keeping the scoring down to its low level. _</p>
        <p>_ In the second half Manchester were "top dogs," anti only good full-back and half-back work by Latchford prevented them from run- ning up a- big score. Manchester's half-backs were playing a strong game, Croft and Roscoe especially showing up well. Pitt, at centre-for- ward, scored twice for Manchester, their inside- right also registering a goal, and time came with a score of 4-4 in Manchester's favour. New- bold, at centre-half, played well for Latchford, while the full-backs gave a good display, espe- cially in the second half.</p>
        <p>Teams for To-day. The first eleven journey to Southport to-day, with the following team: G. Mynekyme ; W. Fletcher and T. B. Wilkinson; C. Billingtbn, J. Albinson and T. Banks; P. W. Isherwood, R. A. Dale, J. E. Pearson, A. Knight and H. R. Colton. Umpire, Mr. F. Grounds. Train leaves Central Station 1.27.</p>
        <p>The second eleven are at home to Bowdon "B," with: W. Starkey; W. T. Eccles and P. Allan- son; D. B. Travis, F. C. Newbold and A. B. Starkey; J. J. McCauley, W. S. Furness, W. H. Minards, A. G. Marsden, and C. Newbold. Um- pire, Mr. H. Eccles. Bully-off 2.15.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>LADIES AT HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>LADIES AT HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's Ladies met " Divex" Ladies at Wilderspool, and won by 3-2. Crosfield's played well in the first half and deserved their 3-0 lead at the interval, Miss Jackson (2) and Miss shown h aoz good form. having scored. Miss Leigh had also In the second half, however, " Divex " Ladies improved wonderfully, and Miss Orme and Miss Whitlow.found the net. The visitors' defence was hard pressed in the concluding stages, and but for some resolute clearances by Miss D. Starkey, in, the goal, "Divex " would have equalised. Miss Marsh worked untiringly at centre-half for Crosfield's Ladies, and Miss Jackson played well in the forwards. For " Divex Ladies the Misses Orme, Blundell, Whitlcri and Moffatt were prominent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>SWIMMING CLUB'S GOOD SEASON.</head>
        <p>SWIMMING CLUB'S GOOD SEASON.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Swim- ming Club was held at the Masonic Hall, Bold- street, on Thursday night, Mr. T. Critchley pre- siding. Mr. S. Ince, the secretary, stated that the year had been very successful, especially the galas. The first team had played 14 polo matches, had won and lost seven, and had an aggregate of 69 goals for and 53 against. Pour of these matches .had been friendlies, three of which were won and the remaining one lost. The second team appeared in two games and had won one and lost one. They had scored four goals, against their, opponents three. Mr. J. Wilkinson, the treasurer, submitted a satisfactory financial statement. They com- menced the year with a balance in hand of £ll Bs. 10d. and finished with £.27 6s. 3d. in the bank. The profit on the year's working amounted to £l5 17s. sd.</p>
        <p>_ The following officers were appointed: Presi- dent, the Mayor; secretary, Mr. C. Turner; assistant secretary, Mr. .1, Peers; auditors, Messrs. A. Ashton and C. Woods; committee, Messrs. J. Newton, .T. A. Beddard, W. Furnival, H. Sisson, A. Barlow, A. Ashton, T. Higham, S. Ince, C. Woods, W. Sutton and D. Ward. Much regret was expressed that Mr. Ince could not see his way to again fulfil the position of secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>Stockton Heath Win a Stirring Game Against Daresbury by Two Goals.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath Win a Stirring Game Against Daresbury by Two Goals.</p>
        <p>BIG SCORES IN THE LEAGUE GAMES.</p>
        <p>The Latchford Club received a visit from the Altrincham Lads, and on the Thelwall-lane en- closure the home team demonstrated that their win of the previous .week was no flash in the pan. They inflicted defeat on their visitor's by. six goals, to one. '3' team received a visit from Heath, and are to be complimented a r e sb ui on their display, as at half-time neither team had scored, and it was only during the last fifteen minutes of the game that the Heathens opened their account and finally ran out win- ners by two goals to nil. The Weston Club entertained the Divex team Stockton The D in their return fixture, and after a hard and strenuous game they obtained the verdict in their favour by two goals to one. The home team wore black armlets in sympathy for the family of Mr. Cotterill, who was a very able and willing worker on the committee of their club. The E,xcelsior strengthened their position at the head of the Second Division by a meri- torious victory over St. Barnabas by three goals to one. owhig to the bad state of the Lovely-lane ground the Bank Quay Recs. transferred the venue of the match to the Fairfield United groUnd, where the game was keenly fought, the result being a draw. The Wilder spool Club playpd their first fix- ture with the Fairfield Club, when, after a very fast and exciting game, they failed to lift them- selves out of their run of ill-luck, and were de- feated by one goal to ail.</p>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Latchford v. Altrincham St. Vincent's. On the Thelwall-lane ground. The home team were at full strength, with the- exception of Siddall in place of Carter. The opening stages were evenly contested, and play was confined to midfield, after which the home players warmed to their work, and set Up a strong attack. Goals bt3heP. bb (2), Fogg and Lees. At score four goals to nil in lhe home team. goal of the day was obtained by SftwalmieTverioletluile:srlic.beowolerlysetto the eluded the opposing Indf-backs and back, and shot in to the top corner of the goal, the custodian having no chance to save, while Fogg scored a grand goal with an overhead kick, the net quite out of the reach ger. The visitors managed to goal after some fine combina- tsoteihfooeilt;e the tillylgte eohillraeltikeorrenifelloypirwards.' Result: Latchford, six goals; Altrincham St. Vincent's, one goal.</p>
        <p>By "Linesman."</p>
        <p>attacking strongly, when, Deakin increased the lead. Short reduced the lead with a shot that gave Daintith no chance to save. Half-time ar- rived with the score in favour of the visitors. The Fairfield forwards were better in front of goal, and Daintith brought off some good saves. The ball going to Woodhouse he equal- ised with a beauty. Result: A draw of two goals each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. First Division.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. First Division.</p>
        <p>Daresbury v. Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>At Moore. Players—Daresbury: Ravenscroft; H. Forster and H. De Prez; F. Ryder, A. Cash and W. Fleet; p. Hall, A. Houghton, H. De Prez, F. Forster, and A. Forster. Stockton Heath: Simcock; H. Sankey and P. Gosling; i. LMoackrshili; J. Denny and R. Gifford; Antrobus, Lockhart, Jones, Smith and Dunne. Referee, J. Hadkiss. The Heathens were the first to ad- vance, but were Sent back by the good play of Forster at full-back, and Ravenscroft saved several shots in splendid fashion. The de- fenders on both sides kicked well; and neither cnstodian was troubled very seriously. Half- time was called without any score. Antrobus was tripped up inside the penalty area, and Dunne being entrusted with the kick sent the ball wide. From the place kick the Heathens had the best of the game, and G. ling, being well up, scored from twenty Yards' range. Even play followed until a few minutes from the finish, when De Prez bungled the ball in front of his own posts, and Dunne scored a very easy goal. Result: Stockton Heath, two goals; Daresbury, nil.</p>
        <p>Bond, , Ditchfield, Orford Barracks v. Great Sankey. On the Barracks ground. Teams—Orford Bar- racks : Harford; Mullane and Hughes; Short, San -favour Staples and Callow; Campbell, Howard, Acton, tkimeye: arrived Emmett and Farsby. Great Glover and Stephens; H. Ditchfield, Newton. of the aand d pPaanyinteer; aDarlington,salal Iried gwe i, t. shot, Poulter and Houghton. The .visitors attacked, gave Harford no chance. Shortly after Hough- ton met a long pass from the opposite wing and sN,c_iotrhe dt the itslcioiae rattling goals s htoot.n ill I ianl hall _time kick against the custodian for carrying. j. theT hheo borne visitors' defenders. D ict co hmfiPeliedt completely i st outplayedd th e third goal, while Stephens scored from and after a spell a even Play Poulter and Ditchfield was again f. iccessful with a good Illidge scored in (—lick succession. Illidge scored again just before the whistle blew. Re- sult: Great Sankey, eight goals; Orford Bar- racks, nil. .</p>
        <p>a Weston v. DiVEX. l effort. .WTeshtiosn.reviMeorslye-naeuctxedscaorseda tonicwitll a oi capital both sets of players, and each end was visited, Leach and Barnes having thein work cut out to keep the visitors at bay. From partial Clearance Smith dashed in and scored It se"nd goal. Heif_ time was called with Weston leading by two goals to none. Roberts obtained the ball, and passing to Fielder in a good position, the latter scored a grand goal for the visitors, Naneollis being quite helpless with the shot. The final result Was: Weston, two goals; Dives, one goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>_ Second Division. Excelsior V. St. Barnabas.. On the Recreation Ground, Bewsey-road. Teams—Excelsior : J. Rigby; P. Boyd and j. Morris;!vira lkTer.,os7Leliol,ydß: x mrSii.eaLceearh,anGd. Co and Weisby 11. A. Hunt. St. Barnabas':. J. Shaw; E. Spruce and T. , patoidrstEer. ;LTongm; aßd.doeGklo,vleir., Wood, P. G. Appleton Challinor D. Vernon and J. Brown. Brown almost from a the kick off steered the ball safely Past Rigby, nd drew the first blood for the Saints. Spruce, miskicking the ball, handled well inside the area, and O'Neil beat Shaw with a low shot, and thus equalised the scores. When the interval was called the scores were one goal each. Leah took advantage of loose Play by the visitors' backs, and put his side ahead. Nettled al by this reverse the Barnabas' made rong at- tempts to equalise, but owing to the heavy state of the ground their short passing movements were easily checked. Spencer scored with an eighteen yards shot, the ball passing just under the bar, Shaw being helpless with the shot. Result: Excelsior, three goals; St. Barnabas', one goal.</p>
        <p>Bank 'Quay Recs. v. Fairfield United. At Fairfield. Players—Bank Quay Rees.: Daintith; Berry and Taylor; Worrall. Lager and dd Wroaevbisetser ; Eic,acilrefisteoidn, DeakiUnited: n: Bouiseahivlel, Green ; Row- lands and Jackson; Clayton, Bebbington and Skelmardine; Short, Woodhouse, Cole, Short and Woodhouse. Green opened the score. En- couraged by their success the visitors were soon</p>
        <p>A STRIKING EXAMPLE of genuine value is afforded by the fact that Steedman's Powders have had a steadily increasing sale for nearly 100 years, and still stand unrivalled as a safe medicine for children. These powders contain no poison. Observe the EE in Steedman.</p>
        <p>./</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>Wilderspool v. Fairfield.</head>
        <p>Wilderspool v. Fairfield.</p>
        <p>In the Brewery Park. Barnes put the home team on the aggressive, Astall scoring after the whistle had gone for off-side. The home for- wards made dccasional .breaks away into the visitors' half, but were easily repulsed, and by good combination by the visitors' inside for- wards they beat the home goalie and registered the only score of the match. The game ended: Fairfield, one goal; Wilderspool Brewery, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES. Emmanuel Old Boys v. Padgate United. _ _ _ .</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES. Emmanuel Old Boys v. Padgate United. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>At l'adgate. Teams: Emmanuel—Knight; Eccles and Roberts; Brooks, Hatton and Booth; Wainwright, Millington, Isherwood, Caldwell and Gregory. Padgate--Nork; Warburton and Sankey; Hodgkinson, Wadsworth and Williams; Houghton, J. Houghton, Critchley, Whitlow and Hardman. Even play ensued, until Millington tricked the opposition and opened the scoring, while Wainwright left Nork helpless with a fast shot immediately afterwards. Despite this double reverse, the .United tried hard to reduce the score,', and Critchley, after good work by Hardman, gave Knight a warm handful, but Eccles cleared, and the Old Boys again returned to the attack. Gregory, being prominent on the wing, rounded Warburton and centred, Milling- ton converting. The Old Boys were in excellent form and they completely baffled the homesters Gregory was very dangerous on the wing, and Wainwright headed through from his centre, while Caldwell netted after Gregory had beaten four opponents and made for him an easy open- ing. Interval: Emmanuel, five goals: Padgate, nil. _</p>
        <p>Wainwright forced a corner, which, being well placed, left Millington no difficulty in scoring. The United again attacked, and Hardman headed in, Knight having great difficulty in clearing. The homesters maintained the pres- sure, and from a shot by Hardman Critchley received and opened the United's accqiint with a good shot. The Old Boys now took charge of the game, Millington adding another goal. Final Emmanuel Old Boys, 7 goals; Padgate. 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Altrinchram. Amateur- Cup.—First Round. Oughtrington "United v. Latchford, at Heatley Warrington and District League. Great Sankey v. Adult School; 31. J. Kite. Stockton Heath v. Weston; W. Dennett.</p>
        <p>Wilderspool Brewery v. St. Barnabas'; 11. G Brandreth.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Old Boys r. Excelsior; T. Pittam.</p>
        <p>Widnes and District Alliance. Ditton. St. Mary's v. Stockton Heath Reserve.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR ,TO.DAY. The following will represent Latchford in their cup-tie at Heatley : Kite; Haspell and Robinson; Downs, Webb and Carter; Smith, ,P. Webb, Lee, Fogg and Siddall. , The Stockton Heath side against Weston, at West-avenue, is: Simcock ; H. Sank ?y E.nd Gos- ling; Glover, Denny and Gifford; Antrobus, Lockhart, Jones, Dunne and Stubb. Stockton Heath Reserve against Ditton St. Mary's play: Harrison: Jefferson and Hough- ton; Magill, Woodward and Isherwood; Wood. head, J. Sankey, Welsby, Rigby and Evans. St. George's Mission play Emmanuel Institute, with: H. Roberts; Livesay and Brown; Goulden, Thomson and Hands; R. Marsden, MaNden. Tulley, S. Hands and T. Hands. Emmanuel Old Boys play Warrington Excel- sior, at Victoria-Park, with: Knight; Eccles and •Harrison; Roberts, Hatton and Booth; Wain- xright, Millington, Isherwood, Caldwell and Gregory. Great Sankey team against the Adult School: Bond; Glover and Stephens; H. ,Ditchfield. Gardner and Payne; hllidg, Darlington, T. Poulter and Houghton. Grappenhall St. Wilfred's meet Higher Wal- ton, at Grappenball, with: Shakeshaft; Evans. and. Hankey ; B. Evans, Starkey and Parr; Wat- son, Ford, Richards, Dunn and Burns. Fairfield United play Newton St. Peter's with: Howard; Broadhurst and Webb; Hamblett, Jackson and Hollows; Monks, Shelmardine, Appleton, Bates and Osborne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>ASSOCIATION ITEMS.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION ITEMS.</p>
        <p>Latchford maintained their cup-tie. form by defeating Altrincham St. Vincent's. Fogg scored a capital goal with an overhead kick. S. Smith got in one of his characteristic runs along the touch-line and finished it off with scoring a splendid goal. Great Sankey improved their position at be head of the League by defeating the Barracks by 8-0. " Tommy " Illidge has strengthened their front line. Houghton and Poulter are the best wing for- wards in the League—they both know where tho goal lies. The display of the Barracks team was much better than the score against them represented it to be. The Daresbury lads put up &amp;good fight against the "Heathens." . .</p>
        <p>It only wanted fifteen minutes to time when Ravenscroft yielded his goal to a shot from Gosling. Lockhart made a poor show by missing four easy chances of scoring. Forster, De Prez, and Ravenscroft played a great game for the Daresbury lads. The Divex team made a fruitless journey for points at Weston. Jones and Nancollis, in their respective goals, gave the best exhibition of goalkeeping seen at Weston. _ _</p>
        <p>Alolyneux, Hulse and Aspey played well fol. Weston.</p>
        <p>• Fielder was the successful marksman for the Divex.</p>
        <p>The Excelsior Boys strengthened their position at the expense of their neighbours St. Barnabas'. Spencer scored the best goal seen for many a day. " Denny" Vernon is not playing up to his reputation. Shuttleworth was a spectator at the match. - Bank Quay Recs. could only make a draw against Fairfield United. The, result was a set-back to their chances of gaining the championship. To-day the Great Sankey team meet the Adult School on the Penketh Recreation. Ground.</p>
        <p>Neither have yet been defeated in the League campaign. " Billy" Hunter and his "merry lads" will give the Great Sankeyites a keen game for the points. The proposed Minor Medal and the Starkey , Cup Competition, which the committee of the Warrington and District League live decided ' to hold, is at present held in abeyance, waiting the official consent of the Liverpool County Com- mittee. It is expected, to have a favourable report for their League meeting at the Lion Hotel next week. Owing to the Latchford ground being unavail- able on February 7th, the semi-final tie between Bank Quay Rees, and St. Barnabas', in the Widnes Minor Medal Competition, will be played on the Orford St. John's Ground.</p>
        <p>"k</p>
