      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>7E' IF&amp;I!LIOT</head>
        <p>7E' IF&amp;I!LIOT</p>
        <p>BANK REPORTS.</p>
        <p>LONDON AND MIDLAND BANK.</p>
        <p>Sir Edward Holden's Review of the Financial Situation.</p>
        <p>THE GOLD QUESTION.</p>
        <p>At the annual general meeting of the London City and Midland Bank, Ltd., held in London last week-end, Sir EDWARD HOLDEN, Bart. (chairman), who presided, moved the adoption of the report. in doing so he passed in review the conditions which had prevailed in the leading monetary centres during the past year. Referring to the methods which the Germans had adopted foi meeting increasing demands for the Navy and Army, he said shipments of gold to Germany during the year had not been in accordance with economic laws. She took gold from Eng- land in March to an amount of about £1,426,000, and at the latter' end of May and in June-she took about £3,200,000. During both of these periods the exchange did not touch parity, except in one instance, when it fell half a point below parity. She took about J.:550,000 in the first week of September, when the exchange was again 'above parity, and in the second week of September she took about £500,000, when the exchange was a little below parity, but not suffi- cient to produce a profit. In the aggregate she took £7,000,000 of gold practically at a loss. The President of the Reichsbank made up his mind to increase the bank's stock of gold, and he had done it even though it had been at a loss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>Canada.</head>
        <p>Canada.</p>
        <p>In a review of the currency system of Canada, the Chairman detailed her borrowings, and said. judging from the facts, it would appear to 11(, the obvious duty of Canada to go slowly, to ex- pand less, and to borrow less, but it would be a mistaken policy for investors in this country to button up their pockets against further Canadian loans, so long as the securities were of a first-class character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>Our Trade Requirements:</head>
        <p>Our Trade Requirements:</p>
        <p>From 1844 to the present time the Bank of England had never been able to accumulate at one time more than about 42 millions sterling of gold, and during the last 13 years the gold had fluctuated between 42 and 26 millions sterling. The aggregate of our foreign trade in 1854, ten years after the establishment of cm present system and the earliest year for which complete returns were available, was about 268 millions sterling, while for the v?.ar 1913 OW total trade had amounted to about 1,490 sterling. Imports and exports were financed by bills of exchange. Consequnitty, the total bills in 1913 in respect of these transactions must have been greater by about 1,132 than they were in 1854, or about 422 per cent. larger. In addition to this, there had also Leen a large increase in the bills in respect to othex international financial transactions. If ever a Royal Commission were needA. it was needed to-day, for the purpose of thoroughly investigating the gold question in this country.</p>
        <p>Success of the Bank. '</p>
        <p>As to their own affairs, taking a retrospective , view of the whole of the amalgamations carried through, they had not had a single instance of failure or disappointment. The results had been in every way satisfactory. They had writ- ten off from profits during the past year in respect to the depreciations in securities no less a sum than £326,000, and inasmuch as the capi- tal value of those particular assets had been written down by that amount since the last meeting, while they held the same securitief, and received the same income in respect to them, the bank was really stronger to that extent Moreover, when money became cheaper, the prices of these securities would rise. The balance sheet for 1913 was thoroughly sound in etery respect. They had a cash balance on the night of December 31st, of close upon 17i mil- lions sterling, or 18.4 per cent. of their liabilities. They had nearly 12 millions sterling at call and short notice, three-quarters of which was prac- tically repayable to them on demand. Theft, profits for the year amounted to .81,235,183, and the amount brought forward from last year wiSl £132,992. Their dividend .for the year amounted to a little over £750,000. If they had declared 19A per cent. less income tax, they would still only be receiving 18_per cent., which they ob- . _ _</p>
        <p>tamed at preseni. They had placed £50,000 to their Bank Premises Redemption Account. They had given to their staff as a bonus £30,000, and also £20,000 to their Pension Fund, carrying forward to the next account about £148,000 They had been considering for the past two years the question of the division of their shares and they recommended that their £6O shares each with £l2 10s. paid up, be divided into .212 shares, each with £2 10s. paid up. The amount uncalled would then be £9 10s. per share, of which £7 would be reserve liability. By this division they would extend their market, thus, giving to those who desire a less liability,, greater facilities to distribute their shares. The report was carried unanimously. The retirine. directors, Messrs. 11. Simpson Gee and Arthur T. Keen, and the auditors, Messrs. Whinney, Smith and Whinney, were re-appointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>INCREASING ARMAMENTS.</head>
        <p>INCREASING ARMAMENTS.</p>
        <p>kPresent_Situation a Disgrace to Christian Civilisation.</p>
        <p>SOCIETY OF FRIENDS APPROACH PREMIER</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner." Sir,—Having sent the enclosed Memorial to the Prime Minister, we should be glad if you could see your way to insert same in your next issue.—Yours faithfully, for the Society of Friends,</p>
        <p>J. P. JACKSON, Clerk. Society of Friends, Warrington, 29th January, 1914. [con.]</p>
        <p>PENKETH PREPARATIVE MEETING. The Rt. Hon. H. H. Asquith, Prime.biinister, Down i ng-street, S.W. Dear Sir,—On behalf of the members of the Society of Friends resident in Warrington and District, we ask leave to approach you on the grave international position which our increas, ing armaments are creating, and would assure you of the serious apprehension we feel in the matter. Your recent utterances have given us grounds of encouragement, as we share the sentiments you recently expressed that "the beai hope—indeed, the only practical hope--of real theiconw eeorrlidedu pressurei)on tthose who arethe theis by civilsed responsible for their government." We cordially welcome the exercise of your great powers to help in relieving the powerful tension, and we hereby assure His Majesty's Government of our heartiest support, in this direction, and would ppeotOgpil'eesosf urge that steps be immediately taken to open negotiations with the European and A trieri.;an Powers, with a view to providing a tvearis for the joint limitations of expendituze ou arma- ments.</p>
        <p>The present situation is a 4'isgrveft Cliiist ;an civilisation increasing suspicion and dislike, and,- in consequence, hindering the spread of peace and goodwill among the nations. We would remind His Majesty's Government that "Righteousness alone exalteth a nation.' Signed on behalf of the Society of Friends, IL GRAY. , J. P. JACRSON.</p>
        <p>The Executive -Committee of the Manchester Liberal Federation have passed a resolution ex- pressing deep regret "at the daeth of Mr. Wil- liam Simpson, the founder and first treasurer of the federation.</p>
        <p>PETERIL FIRE GRATES never fail to please.— Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND. Ironmonger, 51 to 55. Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Punishing Problem -Solved-</p>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>NY arrington Workingmen's Associa- tion. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>PARR'S BANK.</head>
        <p>PARR'S BANK.</p>
        <p>A Record of Highly Satisfactory Progress,</p>
        <p>TRADE CONDITIONS ABROAD.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. F. Pima, presiding at the meeting of Parr's Bank (Limited) on Thursday, said that he found himself in the pleasant position of' meeting the shareholders with a record of highly satisfactory progress; in fact, in several respects, including the profits, they had enjoyed a record year. That was the more gratifying as the trade conditions in many parts of the world had been distinctly unfavourable. At the last meeting he had drawn attention to the grave events in the Near East. The war between Turkey and the Balkan States was now over, but its full financial and economic effects had still to declare themselves, and British trade with the Balkan States, including an important section of Lanca- shire's export trade, had been adversely affected. In the Far East the unsettled state of affairs in China had militated against trade with that country, and the troubles of Brazil in matters financial had also had an adverse influence. In several of the directions indicated and in South Africa, which had suffered like this country from labour troubles, conditions had consider- ably improved. If that improvement continued and political conditions in Mexico became leso unstable, they might hope for a steady revival of trade in all those instances. Reduced American Tariffs. In other directions political conditions abroad had been more favourable to British commercial interests. The effect of the new tariff law in America had not yet had time to manifest itself, but the result of the reductions in duty should tend to encourage British exports to the States As regarded home affairs it was satisfactory te know that during the last twelve months trad. and industry, although in some quarters showing symptoms of falling off, had been active through- out the period, and that activity was reflected in the good profits which this bank was able to sho*. Although trade had been brisk, and they had witnessed no labour disputes on so large a scale as those which had marred the peace of 1912, there had been many minor outbreaks. which had caused unrest. Turning to finance, they found that since the turn of the year money rates, which had been exceedingly high during the greater part of the year, had become decidedly easier. The Bank rate had been lowered in quick succession until it had reached 3 per cent, that day. Most Successful Year. Mr. Parr went on to refer to the amalgamation with the Crompton and Evans Union Bank (Limited), and then dealt with the leading features of the balance sheet. The year, he said, had been a record one for the bank. Their de- posits, amounting to £43,199,031, apart from the five and a half millions which they got from Crompton and Evans and which did not appeal in the balance sheet, showed the gratifying in- crease of over one and a half millions. The item "cash on hand and in the Bank of England" stood at £7,605,752, and money at call and short notice at A:8,476,058. The total of these two items bore a ratio of 36.82 to the bank's liability to its depositors, which should satisfy the most critical banker. The amount of theii investments was £6,822,235, so that their liquid assets amounted to £22,904,046, against deposits totalling £43,199,031. When he addressed them three years ago he had almost apologised- foi having written their Consols down to 77. As the shareholders knew, they had had to write these securities down to a considerably lower level since, and they now stood at a lower figure than they touched in the course of last year. The chief remaining item on the assets side of the balance sheet was A:24,355,332, representing bills discounted and loans and advances to customers. After paying a dividend of 20 per cent, and a bonus of 1 per cent., devoting £30,000 to the reduction of the bank's premises account, and £28,000 to the officers' pension and provident fund, they had been able to add no less than £lOO,OOO to the reserve fund, bringing the total up to £2,loo,ooo—another record-,figure. As a bank they had every reason to be satisfied with the results achieved, and could look forward to the future with every confidence. • The report was adopted,. and it was agreed to increase the capital to £15,000,000 by the creation of 50,000 shares of £5O each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>LANCASHIRE AND YORKSHIRE BANK.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AND YORKSHIRE BANK.</p>
        <p>, The Chairman's Advice to Business Men</p>
        <p>Making Wills.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the share- holders of the Lancashire and Yorkshire Bank was held at the Memorial Hall, Albert-square, Manchester, on Friday week, when there was a good attendance of shareholders. The CHAIRMAN (Mr. Thomas B. Moxon), mov- ing the adoption of the report, said lie thought they would agree that the balance sheet and report were so good as to leave little room foi comment. When he told them that there was a very large increase in the number of cus- tomers, that had debts 'had been very small, that full provision had been made for the fall in the price of investments, and that the inner reserve remained at a figure which they con- sidered quite satisfactory, he ,had said every- thing they could want to know. The directors had followed their usual habit of making every provision which they considered advisable and advantageous for the future before declaring the profits, and it was owing to this prudent course that they had been able to deal so satis, factorily with the depreciation in the invest- ments, and to maintain such an enviable reputa- tion. The shareholders would remember that the reserve fund had never once been drawn upon, but, on the contrary, had been added to every year without exception, the dividend had been steadily increased, and the capital value of their shares had been fairly maintained all through these years of depreciation in invest-. ments, and stood higher now than in 1898. Such a record was very rare. There was only one other bank in England that could say as much, and he hoped they might al l+ ays be able to main- tain this position. classesre Tp oh de reported hr investors.eact aot on o n3B of of ctThut nerleßipearsne skheead nrt ttAoi fvuaec ghsaci lei unarcl,i; ties of the Stock Exchange there was last year alone a loss of no less than ~.€186,000,000, which was equal to 51 per cent, on their nominal capital. That was after a drop of £83,000,000 in the previous year-1912. Such figures showed how much the general community had been affected by the fall in prices. Too much, how- ever, should not be made of this fall. Personal investors had a kind of consolation in remem- bering that their incomes remained the same, and, unless they wanted to realise their invest- ments, the fall in prices would only tend to reduce the exactions of the tax collector foi duties on capital.</p>
        <p>Disastrous and Cruel Wills. There was one feature that these violent fluc- tuations in the value of investments had im- pressed upon him, and that was the great importance of an alteration in the form of will- making. He found that usually the testat-ir, in dividing his estate, left certain definite sums of money to various relatives perhaps, ;he good- will of the business to others, and any saridaa to residuary legatees. He had seen, this method work out very dis- astrously and even cruelly—when there had been serious alterations in value of the various portions of a man's property between the date of his making his will and the date when it came into force. He had known sons, whom the father undoubtedly intended to benefit by giving them his business, having to work for years and years to make up the fixed amounts of money left to the daughters. Of course, such fluctuations in the values of securities as we had , experienced of late years would be pretty sure to cause some distress of this kind in the case of wills made years ago. _</p>
        <p>What he suggested was that a will should pro. vide that after the property had beetvalued it should be divided in certain proportions or per- centages of the total value, so that whether the various portions of the assets of the estate rose or fell in value, the division would remain equit- able according to the wishes of the testator. Of course, small legacies would still he made for fixed sums, but the grand division should be made pro porti onately.</p>
        <p>_ The report was adopted unanimously. Mr. John Healey. Sir J. B. lonsdale, Bart., and Mr. Jos. Wheatley were re-elected directors. Mr. David Smith and Mr. J. P. Garnett were elected auditors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-31_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>N.A. CENTRE.</head>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>ROACH ON THE MOVE.</p>
        <p>Several members visited the Gowy last week end, and although no great catches were regis• tered, all anglers who paid the stream a visit took fish. This goes to prove that the roach are on the move here, and larger numbers will visit</p>
        <p>it each week as the season advances. Roach</p>
        <p>to 14oz. were taken _last week by. several mel</p>
        <p>- hers, but then roach of this weight from Gowy are not _worth while weighing, as</p>
        <p>general run. of roach from here, that takes, prizes, is about 20oz. or more. No one seems to try the canal, but we hell': there will be great competition this year f° members fishing the Norton length. At the Runcorn anglers' general meeting, It was decided to give the Warrington NorthAl) anglers two more lengths of the Norton Cana'. The length now will be from the Horse Bridge' Preston Brook, to Green Bridge, Runcorn waY, except on a Sunday, when the length terminate' at the Turnover Bridge.</p>
