        <p>MOTOR OFFENCE AT ST. HELENS. Albert E. Halliday, driver of a steam maw waggon, was charged, at St. Helens, with leav- ing a motor under full steam unattended on Om highway at Raiuford, and also with showing no red rear light. Mr. Dean, Liverpool, for Om defendant, said that the engine was put out of gear, and could not have moved, so that the cast. ought to_ be dismissed.—Alderman Forster said it was not the right thing to leave a motor un- attended like that, but the Bench did not think it could have been started. That charge would be dismissed, but for the absence of the red light he would be fined 2s. 6d. and costs. Mr. Hamlet Roberts, who contested Flint Boroughs in the Conservative interest at the last election, was on Saturday again adopted as , prospective candidate. William Davies (50), a Post Office linesman, of Britannia-yard, Buyy. died suddenly while haying tea on Saturday. it is supposed that he choked-with a piece of meat.</p>
